          {
            "heading": "Part A: Bohr's Postulates (1913)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Bohr ki soch (1913)",
            "body": "To fix the stability problem in Rutherford's model, **Niels Bohr (1913)** proposed a new model of the atom based on three key postulates:\n\n1. Electrons revolve around the nucleus only in certain **fixed, allowed circular paths** called **discrete orbits**, **shells**, or **energy levels**.\n2. **While an electron remains in one of these allowed shells, it does not radiate (lose) energy.** Because it loses no energy, it never spirals into the nucleus—explaining why atoms are stable!\n3. Each shell has a fixed amount of energy. Shells closer to the nucleus have lower energy, and shells farther out have higher energy. An electron can jump from one shell to another only by absorbing or emitting the exact difference in energy between the two shells—never stopping in between, just like our elevator.\n\nBecause Bohr's model neatly resolves Rutherford's stability puzzle and explains the chemical behaviour of the elements clearly, it is the primary model we use in Class 9 Chemistry. (In higher classes, you will see how quantum mechanics refines this model further for larger, more complex atoms.)\n\nStarting from the innermost shell closest to the nucleus and moving outward, the shells are named **K, L, M, N** (or numbered **$n = 1, 2, 3, 4$**).\n\n```\n   .-------------------------------------------.\n   :  N shell (n = 4)                          :\n   :   .-----------------------------------.   :\n   :   :  M shell (n = 3)                  :   :\n   :   :   .---------------------------.   :   :\n   :   :   :  L shell (n = 2)          :   :   :\n   :   :   :   .-------------------.   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :  K shell (n = 1)  :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :         (+)       :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :       nucleus     :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   '-------------------'   :   :   :\n   :   :   '---------------------------'   :   :\n   :   '-----------------------------------'   :\n   '-------------------------------------------'\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.2** – Bohr's shells nested outward from the nucleus (+): innermost is the K shell ($n = 1$), followed by L ($n = 2$), M ($n = 3$), and N ($n = 4$). This diagram is schematic (drawn as nested boxes for clarity, whereas real shells are concentric rings/spheres and are not equally spaced). Electrons can reside only on these shells, never in the gaps between them.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Rutherford ke model mein ek kami thi: ghoomta electron energy kho dega. **Niels Bohr** ne 1913 mein ek naya, simple vichar rakha. Isse **Bohr ka model** kehte hain. Uski buniyadi baatein ye hain:\n\n1. Electron nucleus ke chaaron taraf **kuch tay, gol raaston** mein ghoomte hain. In raaston ko **shells** ya **energy levels** kehte hain.\n2. Jab tak electron ek shell mein hai, wo **energy nahi khota**. Isliye wo spiral banakar nucleus mein nahi girta. (Yahi Rutherford ki samasya ka hal tha.)\n3. Har shell ki ek tay energy hoti hai. Nucleus ke paas wali shell ki energy kam, door wali ki zyada. Electron ek shell se doosri shell mein tabhi jaata hai jab use utni hi energy mile ya de. Beech mein rukna mana hai, bilkul lift ki tarah.\n\nKyunki ye model Rutherford ke model ka dard door karta hai aur chemistry ke bahut saare nateejon ko saral tareeke se samjha deta hai, isliye hum Class 9 mein isi ko apne kaam ka model banate hain. Dhyan do: bade aur jatil atoms ko poori tarah samjhane ke liye aage ki class mein isme aur sudhaar kiye jaate hain.\n\nShells ke naam **K, L, M, N** hain. K sabse andar, nucleus ke sabse paas. Baaki isi kram mein bahar ki taraf. Inhe **n = 1, 2, 3, 4** bhi kehte hain.\n\n```\n   .-------------------------------------------.\n   :  N shell (n = 4)                          :\n   :   .-----------------------------------.   :\n   :   :  M shell (n = 3)                  :   :\n   :   :   .---------------------------.   :   :\n   :   :   :  L shell (n = 2)          :   :   :\n   :   :   :   .-------------------.   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :  K shell (n = 1)  :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :         (+)       :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   :       nucleus     :   :   :   :\n   :   :   :   '-------------------'   :   :   :\n   :   :   '---------------------------'   :   :\n   :   '-----------------------------------'   :\n   '-------------------------------------------'\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.2** – Bohr ke shells ek ke andar ek: sabse andar nucleus (+) aur K shell (n = 1), phir L (n = 2), M (n = 3) aur N (n = 4). Ye tasveer sirf ek yojana hai: asli shells gol hoti hain, yahan dabbon jaisi banayi hain, aur shells ke beech ke fasle bhi asli nahi hain. Electron inhi raaston mein hi rehte hain, beech mein nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: How Many Electrons in a Shell? The Formula 2n²",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Ek shell mein kitne electron? Formula 2n²",
            "body": "Think of each shell as a concert-hall tier with a fixed number of seats. Niels Bohr and Charles Bury showed that the maximum number of electrons that can fit into the $n$-th shell is given by a simple formula:\n\n> **Maximum number of electrons in a shell $= 2n^2$**\n\nHere **$n$** is the shell number ($1, 2, 3, 4, \\dots$), and $n^2$ means $n \\times n$. Let us calculate the capacity of the first four shells:\n\n- **$n = 1$ (K shell):** $n^2 = 1 \\times 1 = 1 \\implies 2n^2 = 2 \\times 1 =$ **2 electrons**.\n- **$n = 2$ (L shell):** $n^2 = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\implies 2n^2 = 2 \\times 4 =$ **8 electrons**.\n- **$n = 3$ (M shell):** $n^2 = 3 \\times 3 = 9 \\implies 2n^2 = 2 \\times 9 =$ **18 electrons**.\n- **$n = 4$ (N shell):** $n^2 = 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\implies 2n^2 = 2 \\times 16 =$ **32 electrons**.\n\n| Shell | Shell Number ($n$) | $n^2$ | Maximum Capacity ($= 2n^2$) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| K | 1 | 1 | 2 |\n| L | 2 | 4 | 8 |\n| M | 3 | 9 | 18 |\n| N | 4 | 16 | 32 |\n\nCheck: Total capacity across all four shells $= 2 + 8 + 18 + 32 = 60$ electrons ✅\n\nRemember: **capacity** means the *maximum* number of electrons a shell can hold—a shell does not have to be completely full in every atom. Next, let us see the rules for how shells fill up one by one.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Har shell ek hall jaisi hai jismein seats ki ek tay sankhya hai. Bohr aur Bury ke kaam se ek formula mila:\n\n> **Shell mein zyada se zyada electrons = 2n²**\n\nYahan **n** shell ka number hai. Ab hum ye formula n = 1 se n = 4 tak lagate hain. (n² ka matlab n × n.)\n\n**n = 1 (K shell).** n² = 1 × 1 = 1. To 2n² = 2 × 1 = **2** electrons.\n\n**n = 2 (L shell).** n² = 2 × 2 = 4. To 2n² = 2 × 4 = **8** electrons.\n\n**n = 3 (M shell).** n² = 3 × 3 = 9. To 2n² = 2 × 9 = **18** electrons.\n\n**n = 4 (N shell).** n² = 4 × 4 = 16. To 2n² = 2 × 16 = **32** electrons.\n\n| Shell | n | n² | Capacity = 2n² |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| K | 1 | 1 | 2 |\n| L | 2 | 4 | 8 |\n| M | 3 | 9 | 18 |\n| N | 4 | 16 | 32 |\n\nJaanch: chaaron shells ka kul = 2 + 8 + 18 + 32 = 60 electrons ✅\n\nDhyan do: ye **capacity** (zyada se zyada) hai, zaroori nahi ki shell hamesha poori bhari ho. Aage Part C mein dekhenge ki kab kaun si shell bharti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: The Bohr-Bury Rules (For the First 20 Elements)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Bohr-Bury ke niyam (pehle 20 tatvon ke liye)",
            "body": "How do electrons distribute themselves across the shells of an atom? **Bohr and Bury (1921)** formulated three straightforward rules:\n\n1. **Maximum capacity is $2n^2$:** No shell can hold more than $2n^2$ electrons (K holds at most 2, L holds at most 8, M holds at most 18, N holds at most 32).\n2. **The outermost shell can hold at most 8 electrons** (and if the K shell is the outermost shell, it can hold at most 2).\n3. **Inner shells fill first (stepwise filling):** Electrons occupy the lowest-energy (innermost) available shell first. A new outer shell does not start filling until the inner shells have reached their required count.\n\nThese three rules work cleanly for the **first 20 elements** (from hydrogen, with 1 electron, up to calcium, with 20 electrons), which is our complete scope in Class 9.\n\n**Pay special attention to Rule 2 after Argon (18 electrons):** Even though the M shell ($n = 3$) has a theoretical maximum capacity of 18 electrons, **it cannot hold more than 8 electrons while it is the outermost shell** (Rule 2). Therefore, once argon reaches **2, 8, 8**, the M shell pauses at 8, and the next electron goes into the **N shell**:\n- **Potassium (19 electrons):** **2, 8, 8, 1** (NOT 2, 8, 9!)\n- **Calcium (20 electrons):** **2, 8, 8, 2** (NOT 2, 8, 10!)\n\n**An honest note on scope:** Up to calcium (20 electrons), this simple scheme works without exception. Beyond calcium (starting from element 21, scandium), additional electrons begin filling the remaining spots in the inner M shell (up toward 18), following subshell rules that you will study in higher classes.\n\n```\n   Order of filling electrons (for the first 20 elements):\n\n   K (up to 2) -> L (up to 8) -> M (up to 8 first) -> N (1, then 2)\n   fill first     fill next      pauses at 8          starts after Ar\n\n   Ar : 2, 8, 8       K : 2, 8, 8, 1       Ca : 2, 8, 8, 2\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.3** – Order of filling for the first 20 elements: fill K (2), then L (8), then M up to 8, and then place the 19th and 20th electrons into the N shell (rather than putting a 9th electron into M).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Electrons ko shells mein kaise bithayein? Bohr aur **Bury** ne milkar ye niyam diye:\n\n1. **Capacity 2n².** Koi bhi shell iske aage nahi bhar sakti. K mein 2, L mein 8, M mein 18, N mein 32.\n2. **Sabse bahari shell mein zyada se zyada 8 electrons** hote hain. (Agar sabse bahari shell K hai, to wahan 2.)\n3. **Pehle andar ki shells bharo.** Electron pehle sabse andar wali (kam energy wali) khaali jagah mein jaata hai. K poori bhare, tab L; L poori bhare, tab M, aur aage isi tarah.\n\nYe niyam pehle **20 tatvon** (hydrogen se calcium tak) par bilkul theek baithte hain. Hum Class 9 mein bas yahi tak jaate hain.\n\n**Ek zaroori baat:** niyam 2 ki wajah se M shell, jiski capacity 18 hai, **8 electrons par rukkar** agli shell shuru karwa deti hai. Isliye argon (2, 8, 8) ke baad agla electron M mein 9wan nahi baithta, balki **N shell** mein jaata hai: potassium 2, 8, 8, 1 aur calcium 2, 8, 8, 2.\n\n**Class 9 ki seema (imaandari ki baat).** Calcium tak ye niyam seedhe kaam karte hain. Uske baad (jaise scandium se aage ke tatvon mein) pattern **zyada ulajha** ho jaata hai: M shell mein 8 se zyada electron bhi aate hain aur sirf \"8 ka niyam\" kaafi nahi rehta. Wo sab **aage ki class mein** seekhenge. Yahan hum sirf calcium tak ki tasveer ko poori tarah pakka karte hain.\n\n```\n   Electron bharne ka kram (pehle 20 tatvon ke liye):\n\n   K (2)  ->  L (8)  ->  M (pehle 8)  ->  N (1, 2)\n   pehle      phir         phir          Ar ke baad\n   bharo      bharo        8 tak         agla electron\n                                         N mein\n\n   Ar : 2, 8, 8       K : 2, 8, 8, 1       Ca : 2, 8, 8, 2\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.3** – Bharne ka kram: K, phir L, phir M (8 tak), phir N. Argon ke baad ke do tatvon mein naya electron N shell mein jaata hai, M mein 9wan nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Drawing Atomic Diagrams (Step by Step)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Atom ka chitra banana (step by step)",
            "body": "In this topic, we will take the **total number of electrons** in a neutral atom as given (in Topic 4 we will see how that number is determined by the element's atomic number). To write the electronic arrangement and sketch the atom:\n\n- **Step 1:** Note the total number of electrons.\n- **Step 2:** Put up to **2** electrons in the innermost **K shell**.\n- **Step 3:** Put the remaining electrons in the **L shell**, up to **8**.\n- **Step 4:** Put any remaining electrons in the **M shell**, up to **8**. If 1 or 2 electrons still remain (for 19 or 20 electrons), place them in the **N shell**.\n- **Step 5:** Add the shell counts to verify that their sum equals the total number of electrons.\n\n**Example 1: Hydrogen (1 electron).** K gets 1. Arrangement: **1**. (Just one shell containing 1 electron.)\n\n**Example 2: Helium (2 electrons).** K gets 2. Arrangement: **2**. (The K shell is now completely full.)\n\n**Example 3: Lithium (3 electrons).** K gets 2, leaving 1 for L. Arrangement: **2, 1**. Check: $2 + 1 = 3$ ✅\n\n**Example 4: Carbon (6 electrons).** K gets 2, leaving 4 for L. Arrangement: **2, 4**. Check: $2 + 4 = 6$ ✅\n\n**Example 5: Oxygen (8 electrons).** K gets 2, leaving 6 for L. Arrangement: **2, 6**. Check: $2 + 6 = 8$ ✅\n\n**Example 6: Neon (10 electrons).** K gets 2, leaving 8 for L (L is now completely full). Arrangement: **2, 8**. Check: $2 + 8 = 10$ ✅\n\n```\n   Lithium (Li): 2, 1          Carbon (C): 2, 4\n\n               e                           e\n            .-----.                     .-----.\n           /       \\                   /       \\\n          (  e (+) e )               e (  e (+) e )  e\n           \\       /                   \\       /\n            '-----'                     '-----'\n                                           e\n\n   Neon (Ne): 2, 8\n\n               e\n        e   .-----.   e\n           /       \\\n      e   (  e (+) e )   e\n           \\       /\n        e   '-----'   e\n               e\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.4** – Shell diagrams for Lithium (2, 1), Carbon (2, 4), and Neon (2, 8). At the centre is the nucleus (+), surrounded by the inner K shell holding 2 electrons (`e`), and outside it the L shell holding 1, 4, and 8 electrons respectively. Check the totals: Li $= 2 + 1 = 3$, C $= 2 + 4 = 6$, Ne $= 2 + 8 = 10$ electrons. (These sketches are schematic diagrams for counting shells.)\n\n**Example 7: Sodium (11 electrons).** K gets 2 ($11 - 2 = 9$ left), L gets 8 ($9 - 8 = 1$ left), M gets 1. Arrangement: **2, 8, 1**. Check: $2 + 8 + 1 = 11$ ✅\n\n**Example 8: Chlorine (17 electrons).** K gets 2, L gets 8, leaving $17 - 10 = 7$ for M. Arrangement: **2, 8, 7**. Check: $2 + 8 + 7 = 17$ ✅\n\n**Example 9: Argon (18 electrons).** K gets 2, L gets 8, leaving 8 for M. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8**. Notice M holds 8 here, because as the outermost shell it cannot exceed 8 (Rule 2).\n\n**Example 10: Potassium (19 electrons).** K gets 2, L gets 8, M gets 8, leaving 1 electron. Because M cannot hold 9 as the outermost shell, the 19th electron enters the **N shell**. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8, 1**. Check: $2 + 8 + 8 + 1 = 19$ ✅\n\n**Example 11: Calcium (20 electrons).** K gets 2, L gets 8, M gets 8, leaving 2 for the N shell. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8, 2**. Check: $2 + 8 + 8 + 2 = 20$ ✅\n\n```\n   Atom      K        L                   M                  N\n\n   Na (11)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e\n   Cl (17)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e\n   K  (19)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e e    e\n   Ca (20)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e e    e e\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.5** – Shell-by-shell electron counts for larger atoms: Na (2, 8, 1), Cl (2, 8, 7), K (2, 8, 8, 1), and Ca (2, 8, 8, 2). Each `e` represents one electron in that shell. Notice how in K and Ca, the M shell stops at 8 and the N shell begins filling.\n\nOne more reminder: all of these diagrams are built from **identical electrons**. Sodium does not have \"sodium-flavoured electrons\"—what distinguishes sodium from chlorine or neon is simply the total number of protons and electrons, and how those electrons are distributed across the shells.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Yahan hum kisi tatva ke electrons ki **kul sankhya** ko \"di hui\" maan kar chalte hain (ye sankhya kaise tay hoti hai, wo agle topic ka vishay hai). Phir:\n\n**Step 1:** Kul electrons ginte hain.\n**Step 2:** K shell mein 2 rakho (ya jitne hon, agar 2 se kam hon).\n**Step 3:** Bache hue electrons L shell mein rakho, 8 tak.\n**Step 4:** Bache hue M shell mein rakho, 8 tak. Phir N mein.\n**Step 5:** Jodkar jaanch karo: shells ka kul = kul electrons.\n\n**Example 1: Hydrogen (1 electron).** K mein 1. Arrangement: **1**. Bas ek shell, usmein ek electron.\n\n**Example 2: Helium (2 electrons).** K mein 2. Arrangement: **2**. K shell poori bhar gayi (capacity 2).\n\n**Example 3: Lithium (3 electrons).** K mein 2, bacha 1 L mein. Arrangement: **2, 1**.\n\n**Example 4: Carbon (6 electrons).** K mein 2, bache 4 L mein. Arrangement: **2, 4**. Jaanch: 2 + 4 = 6 ✅\n\n**Example 5: Oxygen (8 electrons).** K mein 2, bache 6 L mein. Arrangement: **2, 6**. Jaanch: 2 + 6 = 8 ✅\n\n**Example 6: Neon (10 electrons).** K mein 2, bache 8 L mein (L poori bhar gayi). Arrangement: **2, 8**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 = 10 ✅\n\n```\n   Lithium (Li): 2, 1          Carbon (C): 2, 4\n\n               e                           e\n            .-----.                     .-----.\n           /       \\                   /       \\\n          (  e (+) e )               e (  e (+) e )  e\n           \\       /                   \\       /\n            '-----'                     '-----'\n                                           e\n\n   Neon (Ne): 2, 8\n\n               e\n        e   .-----.   e\n           /       \\\n      e   (  e (+) e )   e\n           \\       /\n        e   '-----'   e\n               e\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.4** – Atom ke chitra. Har chitra mein beech mein nucleus (+), uske chaaron taraf chhota gola K shell (usmein 2 electron \"e\"), aur uske bahar L shell ke electron. Lithium: 2, 1. Carbon: 2, 4. Neon: 2, 8. Gino aur milao: Li mein 2 + 1 = 3, C mein 2 + 4 = 6, Ne mein 2 + 8 = 10 electrons. (Sketch ek yojana hai: asli shells ka fasla aur electron ki jagah aisi nahi hoti.)\n\n**Example 7: Sodium (11 electrons).** K mein 2 (11 − 2 = 9 bache), L mein 8 (9 − 8 = 1 bacha), M mein 1. Arrangement: **2, 8, 1**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 ✅\n\n**Example 8: Chlorine (17 electrons).** K 2, L 8, bache 17 − 10 = 7 M mein. Arrangement: **2, 8, 7**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 7 = 17 ✅\n\n**Example 9: Argon (18 electrons).** K 2, L 8, bache 8 M mein. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8**. M mein 8 hi hain, 18 nahi: kyunki sabse bahari shell mein 8 se zyada nahi (niyam 2).\n\n**Example 10: Potassium (19 electrons).** K 2, L 8, M 8, bacha 1 electron. Wo M mein 9wan nahi baith sakta (M sabse bahari shell hai to uska 8 se zyada nahi). Isliye N mein jaata hai. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8, 1**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 8 + 1 = 19 ✅\n\n**Example 11: Calcium (20 electrons).** K 2, L 8, M 8, bache 2 N mein. Arrangement: **2, 8, 8, 2**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 8 + 2 = 20 ✅\n\n```\n   Atom      K        L                   M                  N\n\n   Na (11)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e\n   Cl (17)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e\n   K  (19)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e e    e\n   Ca (20)   e e      e e e e e e e e     e e e e e e e e    e e\n```\n\n> **Figure 3.5** – Teen-chaar shell wale atoms ko shell-wise gina: Na 2, 8, 1; Cl 2, 8, 7; K 2, 8, 8, 1; Ca 2, 8, 8, 2. Har column mein \"e\" ki ginti hi us shell ke electron hain. Dhyan do ki K aur Ca mein M shell mein 8 par ruk kar N shell shuru hui hai.\n\nEk aur dhyan do: ye saare chitra **ek hi tarah ke electron** se bane hain. Kisi atom ka koi alag \"special\" electron nahi hota; bas kitne electron hain aur shells mein kaise baithe hain, ye alag hota hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Confusing the \"K shell\" with the element potassium (K).**\n   The **K shell** is the innermost electron shell ($n = 1$) in every atom, whereas **K** is the chemical symbol for the element potassium (from *kalium*). Always check the context!\n\n2. **Mistake 2: Assuming a shell must always be filled to its maximum capacity.**\n   The M shell has a capacity of 18, yet in sodium it holds only 1 electron (2, 8, 1) and in argon it holds 8 (2, 8, 8). Capacity is an upper limit, not a requirement.\n\n3. **Mistake 3: Writing potassium's arrangement as 2, 8, 9 (or calcium's as 2, 8, 10).**\n   By Bohr-Bury Rule 2, the outermost shell can never contain more than 8 electrons. Potassium is **2, 8, 8, 1** and calcium is **2, 8, 8, 2**.\n\n4. **Mistake 4: Filling outer shells before inner shells are full.**\n   Electrons always fill the lowest-energy inner shells first. Lithium (3 electrons) is **2, 1**, never 1, 2 or 0, 3.\n\n5. **Mistake 5: Calculating $(2n)^2$ instead of $2n^2$.**\n   In $2n^2$, square $n$ first and then multiply by 2. For $n = 3$, $2 \\times (3^2) = 2 \\times 9 = 18$, NOT $(2 \\times 3)^2 = 36$.\n\n6. **Mistake 6: Thinking the Class 9 shell rules apply unchanged past calcium.**\n   The simple 2, 8, 8, 2 rule is exact for the first 20 elements; from element 21 onward, inner shells resume filling up to 18, which is covered in senior classes.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: Shell ke K ko potassium ka K samajhna**\n\n**K shell** nucleus ke sabse paas wali shell ka naam hai, aur **K** potassium ka symbol hai. Dono alag cheezein hain. Dhyan se padho ki kahan shell ki baat ho rahi hai aur kahan tatva ki.\n\n**Galti 2: Capacity ko \"hamesha poori bhari hoti hai\" samajhna**\n\nM shell ki capacity 18 hai, par sodium mein M mein sirf 1 electron hai. Capacity sirf \"zyada se zyada\" batati hai.\n\n**Galti 3: Potassium ko 2, 8, 9 likhna**\n\nNiyam 2 ke hisaab se sabse bahari shell mein 8 se zyada nahi. Isliye 2, 8, 8, 1 (N shell mein 1).\n\n**Galti 4: Pehle bahar ki shell bharna**\n\nElectron pehle andar ki (kam energy ki) shell bharta hai. Lithium **2, 1** hai, 3 ya 0, 3 nahi.\n\n**Galti 5: 2n² mein n ko double karna**\n\n2n² ka matlab 2 × (n × n) hai. Jaise n = 3 ke liye 2 × 9 = 18, na ki (2 × 3)² = 36.\n\n**Galti 6: Bohr ke model ko \"poora aur aakhri\" maan lena**\n\nYe Class 9 ke liye bahut kaam ka model hai, lekin atom ki poori kahani aage ki class mein aur badi hai. Calcium ke baad bhi ye niyam seedhe nahi lagte."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Rutherford's model could not explain why orbiting electrons don't lose energy and spiral into the nucleus. **Niels Bohr (1913)** proposed that electrons move in **fixed shells (energy levels)** and **do not lose energy** while remaining in a shell.\n- **Elevator analogy:** An elevator stops only at fixed floors, never between floors; similarly, an electron occupies only fixed shells, never the space between shells.\n- The shells are named **K, L, M, N** ($n = 1, 2, 3, 4$) moving outward from the nucleus.\n- Maximum capacity of a shell $= \\mathbf{2n^2}$: **K = 2, L = 8, M = 18, N = 32**.\n- **Bohr-Bury rules:** (1) Maximum capacity of shell $n$ is $2n^2$; (2) the outermost shell holds at most **8** electrons (2 if K is outermost); (3) inner shells fill first.\n- Sample arrangements: H (1), He (2), Li (2, 1), C (2, 4), O (2, 6), Ne (2, 8), Na (2, 8, 1), Cl (2, 8, 7), Ar (2, 8, 8), K (2, 8, 8, 1), Ca (2, 8, 8, 2).\n- After argon (2, 8, 8), the next electron enters the **N shell** (potassium: 2, 8, 8, 1; calcium: 2, 8, 8, 2).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Rutherford ke model ki samasya: ghoomta electron energy kho dega. **Niels Bohr (1913)** ne kaha: electron **tay shells (energy levels)** mein rehte hain aur jab tak ek shell mein hain, **energy nahi khote**.\n- Lift ka analogy: lift sirf floors par rukti hai, beech mein nahi; electron sirf shells mein rehta hai, beech mein nahi.\n- Shells: **K, L, M, N** (n = 1, 2, 3, 4), nucleus se bahar ki taraf.\n- Shell ki capacity = **2n²**: K = 2, L = 8, M = 18, N = 32.\n- **Bohr-Bury niyam:** (1) capacity 2n² se zyada nahi, (2) sabse bahari shell mein zyada se zyada 8 (K ho to 2), (3) pehle andar ki shell bharo.\n- Pehle 20 tatvon ke arrangement: H 1; He 2; Li 2, 1; C 2, 4; O 2, 6; Ne 2, 8; Na 2, 8, 1; Cl 2, 8, 7; Ar 2, 8, 8; K 2, 8, 8, 1; Ca 2, 8, 8, 2.\n- Argon ke baad agla electron M mein 9wan nahi, **N shell** mein jaata hai.\n- Class 9 ki seema: calcium ke baad pattern ulajha ho jaata hai: aage ki class mein."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. What flaw in Rutherford's model of the atom did Bohr's model resolve, and how did it resolve it?\n\n2. State the three main postulates of Bohr's model of the atom in your own words.\n\n3. Using the elevator analogy, explain why an electron cannot stay between two shells. Where does the elevator analogy break down compared to a real atom?\n\n4. Using the formula $2n^2$, calculate the maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in the shells with $n = 1, 2, 3,$ and $4$. Name each shell.\n\n5. State the three Bohr-Bury rules for the distribution of electrons in the first 20 elements.\n\n6. Draw shell diagrams (or write shell-by-shell counts) and state the electronic arrangement for neutral atoms having: (a) 5 electrons, (b) 9 electrons, (c) 12 electrons, (d) 16 electrons.\n\n7. An atom has 19 electrons. A student writes its electronic arrangement as `2, 8, 9`. Identify the mistake and write the correct arrangement.\n\n8. Write the electronic arrangement for atoms having: (a) 15 electrons, (b) 18 electrons, (c) 20 electrons. In each case, state which shell is the outermost shell and how many electrons it contains.\n\n9. The M shell has a maximum capacity of 18 electrons, yet an argon atom (18 electrons) has only 8 electrons in its M shell. Explain why.\n\n10. A classmate claims, \"A sodium atom (11 electrons) has 8 electrons in its K shell and 3 in its L shell.\" Using the Bohr-Bury rules, explain what is wrong and give the correct arrangement.\n\n11. State **True** or **False**, giving a reason for each:\n    (a) \"K shell\" and the symbol \"K\" for potassium mean the same thing.\n    (b) Every occupied shell in an atom must always be filled to its $2n^2$ capacity.\n\n12. Why do we note that the simple Class 9 filling pattern (`2, 8, 8, 2`) applies cleanly only up to calcium (20 electrons)?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. In Rutherford's model, a revolving electron should continuously radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus. Bohr resolved this by proposing that electrons revolve only in **discrete, fixed shells** and **do not radiate energy** as long as they remain in a given shell, keeping the atom stable.\n\n2. (i) Electrons revolve around the nucleus only in certain fixed circular orbits called shells or energy levels. (ii) While in an allowed shell, an electron does not lose or radiate energy. (iii) An electron jumps from one shell to another only by absorbing or emitting the exact energy difference between the two shells, never remaining between shells.\n\n3. Just as an elevator can stop only at built floors (Floor 3 or Floor 4, never 3.5), an electron can exist only in allowed energy shells and never in the gap between them. The analogy breaks down because an elevator is a large everyday object moving up a straight shaft with equally spaced floors, whereas electrons are subatomic particles moving in three-dimensional shells whose energy spacings are not equal.\n\n4. $n = 1$: $2 \\times 1^2 = \\mathbf{2}$ (**K shell**). $n = 2$: $2 \\times 2^2 = \\mathbf{8}$ (**L shell**). $n = 3$: $2 \\times 3^2 = \\mathbf{18}$ (**M shell**). $n = 4$: $2 \\times 4^2 = \\mathbf{32}$ (**N shell**).\n\n5. (1) The maximum number of electrons in shell $n$ is $2n^2$. (2) The outermost shell can hold at most 8 electrons (or at most 2 if K is the outermost shell). (3) Inner shells must be filled before outer shells begin filling.\n\n6. (a) 5 electrons: K = 2, L = 3 → **2, 3**. (b) 9 electrons: K = 2, L = 7 → **2, 7**. (c) 12 electrons: K = 2, L = 8, M = 2 → **2, 8, 2**. (d) 16 electrons: K = 2, L = 8, M = 6 → **2, 8, 6**. Check totals: $2+3=5$, $2+7=9$, $2+8+2=12$, $2+8+6=16$ ✅\n\n7. Writing `2, 8, 9` violates Rule 2, which states that the outermost shell cannot hold more than 8 electrons. Once the M shell reaches 8, the 19th electron goes into the N shell, giving **2, 8, 8, 1**. Check: $2 + 8 + 8 + 1 = 19$ ✅\n\n8. (a) 15 electrons: **2, 8, 5** (outermost shell is **M**, with **5** electrons). (b) 18 electrons: **2, 8, 8** (outermost shell is **M**, with **8** electrons). (c) 20 electrons: **2, 8, 8, 2** (outermost shell is **N**, with **2** electrons).\n\n9. First, the K (2) and L (8) shells fill up first, taking 10 of argon's 18 electrons and leaving 8 for the M shell. Second, by Rule 2, the outermost shell can never hold more than 8 electrons anyway; 18 is the M shell's maximum capacity when inner shells of even larger atoms fill later.\n\n10. The K shell ($n = 1$) has a maximum capacity of $2(1^2) = 2$ electrons, so it can never hold 8 electrons. Filling K with 2 and L with 8 leaves 1 electron for the M shell, giving sodium's correct arrangement: **2, 8, 1**. Check: $2 + 8 + 1 = 11$ ✅\n\n11. Both statements are **False** ❌. (a) The \"K shell\" is the innermost shell ($n = 1$) of any atom, whereas \"K\" is the chemical symbol for the element potassium. (b) An occupied shell does not have to be completely full—for example, sodium's M shell holds just 1 electron even though its capacity is 18.\n\n12. Up to calcium (`2, 8, 8, 2`), the rule of pausing the M shell at 8 and starting the N shell works directly. Beyond calcium (from element 21 onward), additional electrons begin filling the remaining spaces in the inner M shell (up to 18), which requires subshell rules taught in higher classes.\n\n---\n\nAwesome work! As Aria stepped out of the elevator, she smiled at her aunt. \"From now on, every time I ride an elevator, I'm going to think of K, L, M, and N shells.\" Ms. Chen laughed. \"And on the ride home, think about one more question: how do we know how many electrons a carbon atom or a sodium atom has in the first place?\"\n\n**What's next?** We now know how to seat any given number of electrons into shells, but what determines how many protons, neutrons, and electrons an atom of a particular element actually has? In the next topic, **\"Atomic Number and Mass Number,\"** we will meet the two numbers that define every atom's identity and nuclear count.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. What problem of Rutherford's model did Bohr's model solve, and how?\n\n2. List the three main ideas of Bohr's model in your own words.\n\n3. Explain with the lift analogy why an electron cannot stay between two shells. Where does the analogy stop being exact?\n\n4. Using the formula 2n², calculate the maximum number of electrons in the shell with n = 1, 2, 3 and 4. Name each shell.\n\n5. State the Bohr-Bury rules for the first 20 elements.\n\n6. Draw (in words or ASCII) the shell diagrams and write the electronic arrangement for atoms having these numbers of electrons: (a) 5 (b) 9 (c) 12 (d) 16.\n\n7. An atom has 19 electrons. A student writes its arrangement as 2, 8, 9. Find the mistake and write the correct arrangement.\n\n8. Write the arrangement for atoms with (a) 15 electrons (b) 18 electrons (c) 20 electrons. How many electrons are in the outermost shell in each case, and which shell is it?\n\n9. The M shell can hold 18 electrons, yet argon has only 8 in it. Why?\n\n10. A friend says, \"Sodium has 3 electrons in its L shell and 8 in K.\" Explain what is wrong, using the rules, and give the correct arrangement of sodium (11 electrons).\n\n11. State True or False, with reasons: (a) \"K shell\" and \"potassium\" mean the same thing. (b) A shell must always be completely full.\n\n12. Why do we say that the Class 9 scheme works only up to calcium?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Rutherford ke model mein ghoomte electron ko energy khoni chahiye thi aur use nucleus mein gir jaana chahiye tha. Bohr ne kaha ki electron **tay shells** mein hi rehte hain aur ek shell mein rehte hue **energy nahi khote**, isliye atom stable hai.\n\n2. (i) Electron nucleus ke chaaron taraf tay gol shells (energy levels) mein rehte hain. (ii) Ek shell mein rehte hue electron energy nahi khota. (iii) Electron ek shell se doosri shell mein tabhi jaata hai jab use utni energy mile ya de; beech mein nahi rukta.\n\n3. Lift sirf floors par ruk sakti hai, floor 3 aur 4 ke beech mein koi jagah hi nahi, usi tarah electron ke liye do shells ke beech koi tay jagah nahi hai. Analogy yahan tak kaam karti hai ki dono mein \"tay jagahein\" hain aur upar jaane ke liye energy lagti hai. Analogy asli nahi hai kyunki lift ek bhaari, dikhne wali cheez hai jabki electron ka vyavhaar kuch alag hai; aur shells ka fasla floors jaisa barabar nahi hota.\n\n4. n = 1: 2 × 1² = **2** (K shell). n = 2: 2 × 4 = **8** (L shell). n = 3: 2 × 9 = **18** (M shell). n = 4: 2 × 16 = **32** (N shell).\n\n5. (1) Shell ki capacity 2n² se zyada nahi. (2) Sabse bahari shell mein zyada se zyada 8 electron (agar sabse bahari shell K ho to 2). (3) Pehle andar ki shells bharo: K, phir L, phir M aur aage. Ye niyam hydrogen se calcium tak ke liye hain.\n\n6. (a) 5 electrons: K 2, L 3, arrangement **2, 3**. (b) 9 electrons: K 2, L 7, **2, 7**. (c) 12 electrons: K 2, L 8, M 2, **2, 8, 2**. (d) 16 electrons: K 2, L 8, M 6, **2, 8, 6**. Har case mein jaanch karo: 2 + 3 = 5, 2 + 7 = 9, 2 + 8 + 2 = 12, 2 + 8 + 6 = 16 ✅. Chitra mein centre mein nucleus (+), K ke gole mein 2 \"e\", aur baaki \"e\" aage ki shells mein.\n\n7. Ye 2, 8, 9 galat hai, kyunki sabse bahari shell mein 8 se zyada electron nahi ho sakte. Sahi arrangement **2, 8, 8, 1** (naya electron N shell mein). Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 8 + 1 = 19 ✅\n\n8. (a) 15: K 2, L 8, M 5: **2, 8, 5**; sabse bahari shell M mein 5 electron. (b) 18: **2, 8, 8**; sabse bahari M mein 8. (c) 20: **2, 8, 8, 2**; sabse bahari N mein 2. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 5 = 15, 2 + 8 + 8 = 18, 2 + 8 + 8 + 2 = 20 ✅\n\n9. Kyunki Bohr-Bury ke niyam 2 ke hisaab se sabse bahari shell mein 8 se zyada electron nahi hote (pehle 20 tatvon ke liye). 18 sirf M shell ki **capacity** (zyada se zyada) hai, har haal mein bharna zaroori nahi. Argon (2, 8, 8) mein M sabse bahari shell hai, to wahan 8 par ruk jaata hai.\n\n10. K shell mein sirf 2 electron aa sakte hain, 8 nahi, aur L shell mein 8 tak: \"K mein 8, L mein 3\" niyamon ke khilaf hai. Sahi: K 2, L 8, bacha 1 M mein, yaani **2, 8, 1**. Jaanch: 2 + 8 + 1 = 11 ✅\n\n11. Dono baatein galat hain. (a) **False** ❌. \"K shell\" nucleus ke sabse paas wali shell hai, jabki K potassium ka symbol hai, dono alag cheezein hain. (b) **False** ❌. Shell ko poori bhari hona zaroori nahi; capacity sirf zyada se zyada hai (jaise sodium ke M mein sirf 1 electron).\n\n12. Kyunki calcium (2, 8, 8, 2) tak \"sabse bahari shell mein 8\" wala niyam seedhe kaam karta hai. Uske baad ke tatvon mein pattern zyada ulajha ho jaata hai aur ye saral niyam kaafi nahi rehte. Wo sab aage ki class mein.\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Nayra ne lift se bahar nikalte hue muskura kar kaha, \"Mama, ab lift mein baithte hi mujhe shells yaad aayenge.\" Mr. Iyengar ne kaha, \"Aur shaam ko jab hum ghar pahunchenge, to ek aur sawaal sochna: har tatva ke atom mein electrons ki alag-alag ginti kaun tay karta hai?\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Humne is topic mein electrons ko shells mein bitha diya, lekin ek sawaal baaki hai: kisi tatva ke atom mein kitne electron hote hain, aur ye ginti kaise pata chalegi? Agle topic **\"Atomic Number aur Mass Number\"** mein hum us khaas number ko dekhenge jo har tatva ki asli pehchan hai, aur ye bhi dekhenge ki nucleus mein proton ke saath neutron kitne hote hain."
          }
