            "heading": "Introduction",
            "headingHinglish": "Introduction",
            "body": "In our study of atomic structure, we discovered that every atom possesses electrons arranged in energy shells, and that a **completely filled outermost shell** (a duplet or an octet) confers extraordinary stability. We paused our story right when sodium surrendered one electron and chlorine accepted one. That raised an essential fundamental question: **Why do atoms bond together in the first place?** A raindrop, a grain of table salt, the oxygen circulating in our lungs—all of these consist of chemically bonded atoms. Today, we will uncover the **\"why\"** behind chemical bonding and master electron-dot diagrams, a vital tool for all upcoming topics. The complete story of **\"how\"** they bond will unfold across the next parts.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Pichhle chapter ke Topic 5 mein humne dekha tha ki har atom ki baahari shell mein kuch electrons hote hain, aur **poori bhari baahari shell** (duplet ya octet) stable hoti hai. Wahan hum ek jagah ruk gaye the: Na ne 1 electron diya, Cl ne 1 electron liya, aur kahani adhoori chhod di gayi. Ab sawaal ye hai ki **atoms aapas mein judte kyun hain?** Paani ki ek boond, namak ka ek dana, hawa mein oxygen, ye sab judey hue atoms hi to hain. Aaj hum sirf **\"kyun\"** ka jawab dhoondenge, aur kuch electron-dot ka kaam seekhenge jo aane wale saare topics mein kaam aayega. **\"Kaise\"** ki poori kahani agle topics mein aayegi."
