          {
            "heading": "The House of Cards",
            "headingHinglish": "Taash ka ghar",
            "body": "On a quiet afternoon, Liam decided to build an elaborate three-story house of playing cards on the living room table. He balanced each card with immense concentration, creating arches that leaned against one another in delicate equilibrium. He held his breath as he laid the final roof cards.\n\nJust then, his father, Mr. Davis, walked into the room carrying two mugs of tea. The gentle vibration of his footsteps traveled across the hardwood floor, and in an instant—*whoosh!*—the entire structure collapsed into a flat, scattered heap of cards.\n\n\"Oh no!\" Liam groaned, throwing his hands in the air.\n\nMr. Davis placed the mugs on the counter, smiled sympathetically, and asked, \"Liam, look at the cards lying flat on the table. Did anyone push them down into that exact flat position?\"\n\n\"No,\" Liam replied. \"They just fell.\"\n\n\"And if someone bumps the table right now, will they fall over again?\"\n\nLiam chuckled. \"Of course not! They're already flat on the tabletop. There's nowhere lower for them to fall.\"\n\n\"Exactly,\" Mr. Davis explained. \"The standing house of cards was an unstable, high-energy arrangement held together by delicate balance. The slightest disturbance brought it down. The flat pile on the table, however, is a **stable, low-energy arrangement**—it is at complete rest and cannot fall any further. Nature behaves the same way: physical and chemical systems naturally move toward arrangements that are **more stable**, where they have lower potential energy and need no effort to stay that way.\"\n\nLiam thought for a moment. \"Do atoms do that too?\"\n\n\"They certainly do,\" Mr. Davis replied. \"Most atoms have incomplete outermost electron shells, which is like a precariously balanced house of cards. By **bonding** with one another, atoms rearrange their electrons to achieve a deeply stable, complete outer shell. That chemical handshake holding them together is called a **chemical bond**.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Batra ke bete Saksham ko taash ke patton ka ghar banane ki dhun chadhi thi. Ek ghanta laga kar usne teen manzil ka ghar khada kiya. Patte ek doosre se tike hue the, bilkul santulan mein. Usne saans rok rakhi thi.\n\nTabhi Mr. Batra chai lekar kamre mein aaye. Unke kadmon se zameen thodi si hili, aur **phat se** poora ghar dhah gaya. Saksham chillaya, \"Papa!\" Mr. Batra ne maafi maangi, par ek baat poochhi: \"Beta, ek kaam kar. Ab jo patte zameen par bikhre hain, unhe kya kisi ne gira diya?\" Saksham ne kaha, \"Nahi, wo to khud hi gire.\" \"Aur ab ye patte bikhre hue hain, ab inhe koi chhoo de to wo dobara gir jaayenge?\" Saksham hans pada, \"Nahi, ab to ye zameen par pade hain, ab kahan girenge?\"\n\nMr. Batra ne samjhaya, \"Yahi hai asli baat. Khada hua ghar **mehnat** se tika tha: har patta thoda sa santulan sambhal raha tha, aur zara si hulchal par sab gir gaya. Zameen par pada hua dher **aaraam se** pada hai, usse neeche jaane ki jagah hi nahi. Prakriti ki zyadatar cheezein aisi hi hoti hain: wo us arrangement ki taraf jaati hain jahan wo **zyada sthir** (stable) hon, jahan unhe kam 'mehnat' karni pade.\"\n\nSaksham ne socha, \"To kya atoms bhi aisa karte hain?\" Mr. Batra muskuraye. \"Bilkul. Bahut se atoms ki baahari shell adhoori hoti hai, aur wo adhoori shell taash ke khade ghar jaisi 'khichi hui' arrangement hai. Atoms **judkar** ek zyada sthir arrangement mein pahunchna chahte hain. Is judne ka naam hi **chemical bond** hai.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning (Plain English) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chemical bond** | The attractive force that holds atoms or ions together in a compound | The force binding Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in table salt |\n| **Stable** | An arrangement of minimum energy that does not tend to change spontaneously | A flat pile of cards at rest on a table |\n| **Valence electrons** | Electrons present in the outermost shell of an atom | Chlorine has 7 valence electrons |\n| **Electron-dot symbol (Lewis symbol)** | A representation of an element using its chemical symbol surrounded by dots (•) for valence electrons | •Na, or F surrounded by 7 dots |\n| **Duplet / Octet** | Having 2 electrons (in the first shell) or 8 electrons in the outermost shell | He: duplet (2); Ne: octet (8) |\n| **Noble gas** | An unreactive gas (such as He, Ne, Ar) with a completely filled outer shell | Neon: configuration 2, 8 |\n| **Metal** | An element typically having 1 to 3 valence electrons that readily loses electrons | Sodium (Na), Magnesium (Mg), Aluminium (Al) |\n| **Non-metal** | An element typically having 4 to 7 valence electrons that gains or shares electrons | Oxygen (O), Chlorine (Cl), Carbon (C) |",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Shabd | Matlab (simple bhasha mein) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Chemical bond (rasayanik bandhan)** | Wo aakarshan (kheench) jo atoms ya ions ko aapas mein joda rakhta hai | Namak mein Na aur Cl ko jodne wala |\n| **Stable (sthir)** | Jis arrangement ko badalne ki zaroorat nahi; kam \"khichav\" wali | Zameen par pada taash ka dher |\n| **Valence electrons** | Atom ki baahari shell ke electrons | Cl mein 7 |\n| **Electron-dot symbol (Lewis symbol)** | Tatva ke symbol ke aas-paas valence electrons ko dots (•) se dikhane ka tareeka | •Na, F ke chaaron taraf 7 dots |\n| **Duplet / Octet** | Baahari shell mein 2 (pehli shell) ya 8 electrons | He: duplet, Ne: octet |\n| **Noble gas** | He, Ne, Ar jaisi gas jinki baahari shell poori bhari hai | Ne: 2, 8 |\n| **Metal (dhatu)** | Tatva jinke baahari 1 se 3 electron hote hain aur jo electron dena pasand karte hain | Na, Mg, Al |\n| **Non-metal (adhatu)** | Tatva jinke baahari 4 se 7 electron hote hain aur jo electron lete ya share karte hain | O, Cl, C |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part A: What Does \"Stable\" Mean?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Stable matlab kya?",
            "body": "The house of cards and the flat pile illustrate a key principle: the very same 52 cards can exist in two distinctly different states—one **strained and unstable**, and the other **settled and stable**. The first requires continuous balance to remain upright; the second remains at rest effortlessly.\n\n```\n    Standing House (High Energy)         Collapse                Flat Pile (Stable)\n\n            /\\\n           /  \\                         \\  /  /                 ___ ___ ___\n          /\\  /\\                         /  \\ /                 \\_\\_/_/_/_/\n         /  \\/  \\                       /  /\\ \\                  cards resting flat\n        /\\  /\\  /\\                                               on the table\n       /  \\/  \\/  \\\n\n     cards in delicate balance;      the slightest tremor         cannot fall any\n     ready to fall at any moment     topples everything           further; fully settled\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – A standing house of cards represents a high-energy, unstable state that readily collapses into a flat, stable, low-energy pile. Similarly, atoms interact and bond to attain a stable, low-energy electron configuration. Note that this is an everyday analogy; atoms themselves are particles, not paper cards.\n\nIn chemistry, we express this by stating that a more stable arrangement corresponds to a **state of lower energy**. For now, remember this core rule: **Stable = settled in an arrangement of minimum energy that has no tendency to change on its own.** And what represents the ultimate stable arrangement for an atom? The electron configurations of the noble gases.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Taash ka ghar aur zameen ka dher ek baat sikhate hain: ek hi cheez (wahi 52 patte) do arrangement mein ho sakti hai, ek **khichi hui** aur ek **aaraam wali**. Pehla arrangement apne aap tikta nahi; doosra tika rehta hai.\n\n```\n    Khada ghar (mehnat se)              Dhah gaya                Dher (stable)\n\n            /\\\n           /  \\                         \\  /  /                 ___ ___ ___\n          /\\  /\\                         /  \\ /                 \\_\\_/_/_/_/\n         /  \\/  \\                       /  /\\ \\                  patte zameen par\n        /\\  /\\  /\\                                               pade hain\n       /  \\/  \\/  \\\n\n     patte santulan mein,           zara si hulchal               ab aur neeche\n     par kisi bhi pal gir           se saare gire                 jaane ki jagah\n     sakte hain                                                   nahi\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Taash ka ghar (khada, khichi hui arrangement) zara si hulchal se dhah jaata hai aur patte zameen par bikhre dher (aaraam wali, stable arrangement) mein pahunch jaate hain. Atoms bhi aisi arrangement ki taraf jaate hain jo zyada sthir ho: kam khichav, zyada aaraam. Ye bas ek upma (analogy) hai, atom taash ke patte nahi hote.\n\nChemistry ki bhasha mein hum kehte hain ki jo arrangement zyada stable hota hai uski **energy kam** hoti hai. Abhi itna samajh lo: **stable = sthir, jise badalne ka dil na kare.** Aur kisi bhi atom ke liye sabse jaana-pehchana stable arrangement kya hai? Wohi jo humne pichhle chapter mein dekha tha."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Noble Gases: The Most Stable Atoms",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Noble gas: sabse aaraam mein baithe atoms",
            "body": "Recall the electron configurations of the first three noble gases: **Helium (2), Neon (2, 8), and Argon (2, 8, 8)**. Their valence shells are completely full: helium holds a complete **duplet** (2 electrons filling the K shell), while neon and argon hold complete **octets** (8 electrons in their outermost shells). Because their outer shells are full, these elements **rarely react with anything**. They already exist in the atomic equivalent of a flat pile of cards at rest. They are our **benchmarks of stability**.\n\nOther atoms—such as sodium, chlorine, oxygen, and magnesium—possess incomplete valence shells. To attain the enviable, stable configuration of a noble gas, an atom has three pathways:\n\n1. **Losing electrons:** Give away outermost electrons so that the underlying, completely filled shell becomes the new outer shell.\n2. **Gaining electrons:** Take in electrons from another atom until the outermost shell contains 8 electrons.\n3. **Sharing electrons:** Pool valence electrons with another atom so that the shared electrons count toward both atoms' outer shells.\n\n```\n    1. LOSING (transfer)        2. GAINING (transfer)        3. SHARING (covalent)\n\n    Na  ----->  Na⁺             Cl  <-----  e⁻               Cl  ::  Cl\n    (2, 8, 1)   (2, 8)          (2, 8, 7)                    both share an\n     loses 1 e⁻  neon-like       gains 1 e⁻                   electron pair to\n                                Cl⁻ (2, 8, 8)                complete both octets\n                                argon-like\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Three pathways to achieve a complete valence shell. (1) Sodium loses its single valence electron to form Na⁺ with a stable neon-like configuration (2, 8). (2) Chlorine gains one electron to form Cl⁻ with a stable argon-like configuration (2, 8, 8). (3) Two chlorine atoms share a pair of electrons so that both count eight valence electrons. The full mechanism of sharing will be explored in Topic 3.\n\nHere is a quick preview of where this leads: when one atom **gives up** an electron and another **accepts** it, an **ionic bond** forms (the focus of Topic 2). When two atoms **share** electrons, a **covalent bond** forms (the focus of Topic 3). You do not need to memorize every detail of these bonds yet; simply keep the central driving principle in mind: **chemical bonds form so that atoms can achieve stable, noble-gas-like electronic configurations.**",
            "bodyHinglish": "Pichhle chapter mein humne dekha ki **He (2), Ne (2, 8) aur Ar (2, 8, 8)** ki baahari shell poori bhari hai: **duplet** ya **octet**. Ye teeno gas **lagbhag kisi se nahi judti**; unhe judne ki zaroorat hi nahi, wo pehle se \"zameen par pade taash ke dher\" jaisi hain. Ye hi hamare **reference (aadarsh)** hain.\n\nBaaki atom (jaise Na, Cl, O, Mg) ki baahari shell adhoori hai. Unke paas teen raaste hain apni shell ko noble gas jaisi poori karne ke liye:\n\n1. **Electron de dena (lose):** baahari ke kuch electron kisi aur ko de do. Neeche wali bhari hui shell baahari ban jaati hai.\n2. **Electron le lena (gain):** kisi doosre se kuch electron le lo taaki baahari shell poori ho jaaye.\n3. **Electron share karna:** kisi doosre atom ke saath electron saajha karo, taaki dono ki shell poori ginti mein aaye.\n\n```\n    1. DENA (lose)            2. LENA (gain)            3. SHARE KARNA\n\n    Na  ----->  Na⁺           Cl  <-----  e⁻            Cl  ::  Cl\n    (2, 8, 1)   (2, 8)        (2, 8, 7)                 dono ke beech\n     1 e⁻ gaya   neon jaisa    1 e⁻ aaya                 ek jodi electron\n                              Cl⁻ (2, 8, 8)             dono ki ginti mein\n                              argon jaisa               aati hai\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Shell poori karne ke teen raaste. (1) Na apna 1 baahari electron de deta hai, Na⁺ ki configuration 2, 8 (neon jaisi). (2) Cl 1 electron le leta hai, Cl⁻ ki configuration 2, 8, 8 (argon jaisi). (3) Do Cl atom ek jodi electron saajha karte hain, aur har Cl ko apni octet poori milti hai. Teesre raaste ki poori kahani Topic 3 mein.\n\nEk chhota preview (taki aage ka rasta pata rahe): jab ek atom electron **deta** hai aur doosra **leta** hai, to ek **ionic bond** banta hai (Topic 2 ka vishay). Jab do atom electron **share** karte hain, to ek **covalent bond** banta hai (Topic 3 ka vishay). Abhi in dono ko na seekhna hai na yaad rakhna hai; bas itna dhyan rakho ki **bond banne ka kaaran ek hi hai: stable, noble-gas jaisi arrangement.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: What Is a Chemical Bond Really?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Bond asal mein hai kya?",
            "body": "A chemical bond is not a physical stick, glue, or piece of string connecting two atoms. **A chemical bond is an attractive electrical force.** In our study of matter, we learned that particles experience attractive forces that determine whether substances are solids, liquids, or gases. In chemical bonding, these forces are **far stronger**, arising from the redistribution or sharing of valence electrons between atoms.\n\n> **Key Takeaway:** A **chemical bond** is the strong attractive force that holds atoms or ions together, driven by the tendency of atoms to attain a stable, low-energy electron configuration.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Bond koi dhaaga ya takwa nahi hai jo atoms ko baandh de. **Bond ek aakarshan (kheench) hai.** Chapter 1 mein humne dekha tha ki particles ke beech aakarshan hota hai, aur wahi tay karta tha ki cheez thos hai, tarl hai ya gas. Yahan bhi wahi baat hai, bas ye aakarshan **bahut mazboot** hai aur electrons ki lena-dena ya sajhedari se paida hota hai. Is topic mein itna kaafi hai: **chemical bond = atoms (ya ions) ko jodkar rakhne wala aakarshan, jo electrons ke karan banta hai, aur jiska maqsad stable arrangement paana hai.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Electron-Dot Symbols (Lewis Symbols)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Electron-dot symbols (Lewis symbols)",
            "body": "Because only the **outermost (valence) electrons** participate in chemical bonding, drawing every inner shell for every atom would be cumbersome. Instead, chemists use a convenient shorthand introduced by the American chemist G. N. Lewis: write the **element's symbol** and represent its **valence electrons as dots (•)** placed around it. This is known as an **electron-dot symbol** or **Lewis symbol**.\n\n**How to Draw an Electron-Dot Symbol (4 Simple Steps):**\n\n1. Write the chemical symbol of the element (e.g., Na, Cl, O). The symbol represents the nucleus together with all inner, completely filled electron shells.\n2. Determine the **number of valence electrons** from the atom's electron configuration.\n3. Place dots on the **four sides** of the symbol (top, right, bottom, left). Distribute dots **one at a time** on each side before pairing them up.\n4. If there are more than 4 valence electrons, begin **pairing the dots**, starting from the top, then right, bottom, and left until all valence electrons are represented.\n\n*Special Note:* Helium is an exception. Because its only shell (K shell) has a maximum capacity of 2, its 2 valence electrons form a stable **duplet** and are conventionally drawn as a pair together on one side.\n\nBelow is the complete reference table for the first 20 elements, verified using the formula: **(Pairs × 2) + Unpaired dots = Valence electrons.**\n\n| Z | Element | Symbol | Electron Configuration | Valence Electrons | Dot Pairs (••) | Unpaired Dots (•) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Hydrogen | H | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 2 | Helium | He | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n| 3 | Lithium | Li | 2, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 4 | Beryllium | Be | 2, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 5 | Boron | B | 2, 3 | 3 | 0 | 3 |\n| 6 | Carbon | C | 2, 4 | 4 | 0 | 4 |\n| 7 | Nitrogen | N | 2, 5 | 5 | 1 | 3 |\n| 8 | Oxygen | O | 2, 6 | 6 | 2 | 2 |\n| 9 | Fluorine | F | 2, 7 | 7 | 3 | 1 |\n| 10 | Neon | Ne | 2, 8 | 8 | 4 | 0 |\n| 11 | Sodium | Na | 2, 8, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 12 | Magnesium | Mg | 2, 8, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 13 | Aluminium | Al | 2, 8, 3 | 3 | 0 | 3 |\n| 14 | Silicon | Si | 2, 8, 4 | 4 | 0 | 4 |\n| 15 | Phosphorus | P | 2, 8, 5 | 5 | 1 | 3 |\n| 16 | Sulphur | S | 2, 8, 6 | 6 | 2 | 2 |\n| 17 | Chlorine | Cl | 2, 8, 7 | 7 | 3 | 1 |\n| 18 | Argon | Ar | 2, 8, 8 | 8 | 4 | 0 |\n| 19 | Potassium | K | 2, 8, 8, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 20 | Calcium | Ca | 2, 8, 8, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n\nHere is how several key elements appear as Lewis dot structures:\n\n```\n        •          ••          •           •            •\n        H          He         Na          Mg •        • C •\n                                                        •\n\n        ••          ••          ••        ••         ••\n      • N •       • O ••      • F ••   •• Ne ••    • Cl ••\n        •           •           ••        ••         ••\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Selected Lewis electron-dot symbols. Top row: H (1 dot), He (1 paired duplet), Na (1 single dot), Mg (2 single dots), C (4 single dots). Bottom row: N (5: 1 pair + 3 singles), O (6: 2 pairs + 2 singles), F (7: 3 pairs + 1 single), Ne (8: 4 pairs, full octet), Cl (7: 3 pairs + 1 single). Notice that F and Cl look identical because both have 7 valence electrons.\n\nThree critical observations to keep in mind:\n\n- **Same valence electrons = identical dot patterns**, regardless of the number of inner shells. Li (2, 1), Na (2, 8, 1), and K (2, 8, 8, 1) all display exactly 1 dot.\n- **Unpaired dots represent potential bonding sites.** Chlorine has 1 unpaired dot, signaling that it needs 1 electron to complete its octet. This simple count predicts how atoms combine.\n- The precise side (top, bottom, left, or right) where a dot is placed is simply a drafting convention. What truly matters is the **total count of valence electrons** and whether they are paired or unpaired.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Bonding ki saari kahaniyon mein bas **baahari electrons** kaam karte hain. Andar ke shells ko kuch nahi karna. To har baar poora atom diagram banane ki jagah hum ek **shorthand** use karte hain: tatva ka **symbol** likho aur uske chaaron taraf **valence electrons** ko **dots (•)** se dikhao. Ise **electron-dot symbol** ya **Lewis symbol** kehte hain.\n\n**Kaise banayein? 4 qadam:**\n\n1. Tatva ka symbol likho (Na, Cl, O ...). Symbol atom ka nucleus aur andar ke saare shells maana jaata hai.\n2. Configuration se **baahari (valence) electrons ki ginti** nikalo. (Pichhle chapter ki table se: K 2, L 8, M 8, phir N.)\n3. Symbol ke **chaar taraf** (upar, daayen, neeche, baayen) dots rakho. Pehle **har taraf ek-ek** dot rakho.\n4. Agar 4 se zyada dots hain, to 5vein dot se shuru karke dots ko **jodi mein** karo: pehle upar wali taraf, phir daayen, phir neeche, phir baayen.\n\nHelium ek khaas case hai: uski sirf ek shell (K) hai jo 2 se poori hoti hai, isliye uske 2 dots ek jodi mein dikhate hain (duplet).\n\nNeeche pehle 20 tatvon ki poori table hai. Har valence electron ginti configuration se li gayi hai (K 2, L 8, M 8, phir N), aur har tatva ki dots ki jodi aur akele dots ginkar jaanch ki gayi hai: **jodi x 2 + akele = valence electrons.**\n\n| Z | Tatva | Symbol | Configuration | Valence electrons | Dots ki jodi (••) | Akele dots (•) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Hydrogen | H | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 2 | Helium | He | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n| 3 | Lithium | Li | 2, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 4 | Beryllium | Be | 2, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 5 | Boron | B | 2, 3 | 3 | 0 | 3 |\n| 6 | Carbon | C | 2, 4 | 4 | 0 | 4 |\n| 7 | Nitrogen | N | 2, 5 | 5 | 1 | 3 |\n| 8 | Oxygen | O | 2, 6 | 6 | 2 | 2 |\n| 9 | Fluorine | F | 2, 7 | 7 | 3 | 1 |\n| 10 | Neon | Ne | 2, 8 | 8 | 4 | 0 |\n| 11 | Sodium | Na | 2, 8, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 12 | Magnesium | Mg | 2, 8, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 13 | Aluminium | Al | 2, 8, 3 | 3 | 0 | 3 |\n| 14 | Silicon | Si | 2, 8, 4 | 4 | 0 | 4 |\n| 15 | Phosphorus | P | 2, 8, 5 | 5 | 1 | 3 |\n| 16 | Sulphur | S | 2, 8, 6 | 6 | 2 | 2 |\n| 17 | Chlorine | Cl | 2, 8, 7 | 7 | 3 | 1 |\n| 18 | Argon | Ar | 2, 8, 8 | 8 | 4 | 0 |\n| 19 | Potassium | K | 2, 8, 8, 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 20 | Calcium | Ca | 2, 8, 8, 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n\nAb kuch tatvon ki asli drawing dekho. Dhyan do: **ek hi column ke tatvon (H, Na, K ya O, S ya F, Cl) ke dots ek jaise** dikhte hain.\n\n```\n        •          ••          •           •            •\n        H          He         Na          Mg •        • C •\n                                                        •\n\n       ••          ••          ••        ••         ••\n     • N •       • O ••      • F ••   •• Ne ••    • Cl ••\n       •           •           ••        ••         ••\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Kuch electron-dot symbols. Upar: H (1 dot), He (2 dots ki jodi, duplet), Na (1 dot), Mg (2 akele dots), C (4 akele dots). Neeche: N (5: ek jodi + 3 akele), O (6: do jodi + 2 akele), F (7: teen jodi + 1 akela), Ne (8: chaar jodi, octet), Cl (7: teen jodi + 1 akela). F aur Cl ke dots ek jaise hain kyunki dono ke 7 valence electrons hain.\n\nTeen baatein notice karo:\n\n- **Ek jaise valence electrons, ek jaisa dot symbol**, chahe shell kitni bhi ho. Li (2, 1), Na (2, 8, 1) aur K (2, 8, 8, 1) teeno ke 1 dot hain.\n- **Akele dots** wo hain jinhe **jodi** ki talash hai. Cl ke paas 1 akela dot hai, matlab usko ek electron chahiye. Isi se aage sharing aur lena-dena samajh aayega.\n- Dots ki **jagah** (kaun sa dot kis taraf) sirf ek picture hai; usse atom ka swabhav nahi badalta. Aage ke topics ke bond diagrams mein dots kabhi doosri taraf bhi dikhenge. Zaroori sirf **ginti** hai: kitne baahari electrons hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Who Donates, Who Accepts, and Who Shares?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Kaun dega, kaun lega, kaun share karega?",
            "body": "How do we predict which pathway an atom will take? Remember: every atom seeks a stable octet (or duplet) via the **path of least resistance** (requiring the least energy):\n\n- **Elements with 1 to 3 valence electrons** (Li, Na, K: 1; Be, Mg, Ca: 2; Al: 3): It is far easier to **lose** 1, 2, or 3 electrons than to capture 5 to 7 electrons. Once lost, the underlying full shell becomes the stable valence shell. These elements are **metals**, and they act as **electron donors**.\n- **Elements with 5 to 7 valence electrons** (N, P: 5; O, S: 6; F, Cl: 7): These atoms are just 3, 2, or 1 electron short of an octet. It is much easier to **gain** or **share** electrons. These elements are **non-metals**, and they act as **electron acceptors or sharers**.\n- **Elements with 4 valence electrons** (C, Si): Gaining 4 electrons or losing 4 electrons requires prohibitive amounts of energy. Therefore, these elements almost always **share** electrons to form covalent bonds. (This unique property of carbon forms the basis of organic chemistry.)\n- **Elements with 8 valence electrons (or 2 for He)** (He, Ne, Ar): Their outer shells are already complete. They have no chemical urge to gain, lose, or share electrons: **noble gases**.\n- **Hydrogen:** A unique case with 1 electron in the K shell. It needs just 1 more electron to complete its duplet and almost always bonds by **sharing**.\n\n| Valence Electrons ($n$) | Examples | Electrons Needed for Octet ($8 - n$) | Preferred Pathway |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Na, K | Gain 7 or Lose 1 | **Lose 1** (forms $+1$ cation) |\n| 2 | Mg, Ca | Gain 6 or Lose 2 | **Lose 2** (forms $+2$ cation) |\n| 3 | Al | Gain 5 or Lose 3 | **Lose 3** (forms $+3$ cation) |\n| 4 | C, Si | Gain 4 or Lose 4 | **Share 4** (covalent bonds) |\n| 5 | N, P | Gain 3 | **Gain 3 or share** |\n| 6 | O, S | Gain 2 | **Gain 2 or share** |\n| 7 | F, Cl | Gain 1 | **Gain 1 or share** |\n| 8 | Ne, Ar | 0 | **Inert** (does not bond) |\n\n*(Note: Certain compounds of beryllium and aluminium involve covalent sharing, while silicon and boron exhibit metalloid behavior. These nuances will be explored in advanced classes.)*\n\nA fundamental pattern emerges from this table:\n- **Metals** have few valence electrons (1 to 3) and tend to lose them.\n- **Non-metals** have many valence electrons (4 to 7) and tend to gain or share them.\n- When a **metal meets a non-metal**, electron transfer takes place $\\rightarrow$ **ionic bond** (Topic 2).\n- When a **non-metal meets another non-metal**, electron sharing takes place $\\rightarrow$ **covalent bond** (Topic 3).\nBoth routes lead to the same destination: an octet or duplet for every participating atom.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab asli sawaal: kisi atom ke baahari electrons dekh kar kaise pata chale ki wo kaun sa raasta chunega? Yaad rakho, atom ko apni baahari shell **octet (ya duplet)** tak pahunchani hai, aur wo **kam se kam mehnat** wala raasta chunega.\n\n- **1 se 3 baahari electrons wale** (Li, Na, K: 1; Be, Mg, Ca: 2; Al: 3): electron **dena** aasan hai. 1 se 3 electron de do to neeche wali bhari hui shell baahari ban jaati hai. Lena to 5 se 7 electrons lene padte, jo bahut mushkil. Ye **metals** (dhatu) hain, aur ye electron **dene** ka raasta pasand karte hain.\n- **5 se 7 baahari electrons wale** (N, P: 5; O, S: 6; F, Cl: 7): octet mein sirf 3, 2 ya 1 electron kam hain. **Lena** (ya share karna) aasan hai. Ye **non-metals** (adhatu) hain.\n- **4 baahari electrons wale** (C, Si): 4 dena ya 4 lena dono barabar mushkil hain, isliye ye aksar **share** karte hain. (Carbon ki is baat ka bahut bada kaam aage dikhega.)\n- **8 (ya He ke 2) wale** (He, Ne, Ar): kuch karne ki zaroorat nahi: noble gas.\n- **Hydrogen** thoda alag hai: uska 1 electron hai aur duplet ke liye 1 aur chahiye. Wo aksar share karta hai.\n\n| Baahari electrons | Misaal | Octet ke liye kitna chahiye (8 − n) | Aam raasta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Na, K | 7 lena ya 1 dena | **Dena** |\n| 2 | Mg, Ca | 6 lena ya 2 dena | **Dena** |\n| 3 | Al | 5 lena ya 3 dena | **Dena** |\n| 4 | C, Si | 4 lena ya 4 dena | **Share karna** |\n| 5 | N, P | 3 lena | **Lena ya share karna** |\n| 6 | O, S | 2 lena | **Lena ya share karna** |\n| 7 | F, Cl | 1 lena | **Lena ya share karna** |\n| 8 | Ne, Ar | kuch nahi | Judte nahi |\n\n(Be aur Al ke kuch compounds share karne wale bhi hote hain; Si aur B ko dhatu aur adhatu ke beech ka tatva maana jaata hai. Ye sab aage ki class mein.)\n\nEk aam pattern jo is table se dikhta hai: **dhatu ka valence electron kam (1 se 3)** aur **adhatu ka valence electron zyada (4 se 7).** Aur bond banta kaise hai? **Dhatu + adhatu** ke beech lena-dena hota hai (ionic bond, Topic 2). **Adhatu + adhatu** ke beech sharing hoti hai (covalent bond, Topic 3). Dono ka maqsad ek hi: sabke paas octet (ya duplet)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Solved examples",
            "body": "### Example 1. Construct the Lewis electron-dot symbol for nitrogen.\n\n- Nitrogen has atomic number $Z = 7$, with electron configuration $(2, 5)$.\n- Valence electrons $= 5$.\n- Step 1: Place 1 dot on each of the 4 sides (top, right, bottom, left). That accounts for 4 dots.\n- Step 2: Place the 5th dot on the top side to form a pair.\n- **Result:** 1 pair on top and 3 unpaired single dots on the remaining sides.\n\n*Verification:* $2 \\text{ (paired)} + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 \\text{ valence electrons}$ ✅\n\n### Example 2. Construct the Lewis electron-dot symbol for sulphur.\n\n- Sulphur has $Z = 16$, with configuration $(2, 8, 6)$.\n- Valence electrons $= 6$.\n- Place 1 dot on each of the 4 sides (4 dots), then pair up the 5th dot on top and the 6th on the right.\n- **Result:** 2 pairs of dots (top and right) and 2 single dots (bottom and left).\n\n*Verification:* $2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6$ ✅ Oxygen $(2, 6)$ has the identical dot pattern because both share 6 valence electrons ✅\n\n### Example 3. Determine the valence electrons from a Lewis symbol.\n\nAn unknown element's Lewis symbol displays **three pairs of dots and one single dot**. How many valence electrons does it possess, and which element from the first 20 could it be?\n\n- 3 pairs $= 3 \\times 2 = 6$ dots.\n- 1 single dot $= 1$ dot.\n- Total valence electrons $= 6 + 1 = \\mathbf{7}$.\n- Elements in the first 20 with 7 valence electrons: **Fluorine** $(2, 7)$ or **Chlorine** $(2, 8, 7)$.\n\n*Verification:* Both need $8 - 7 = 1$ electron to complete their octet ✅\n\n### Example 4. Determine electrons gained or lost for sodium and chlorine.\n\n- Chlorine: configuration $(2, 8, 7)$. To complete its octet, it needs $8 - 7 = \\mathbf{1\\text{ electron to gain}}$.\n- Sodium: configuration $(2, 8, 1)$. By losing 1 electron, its filled L shell (8) becomes outermost, so it is easiest to $\\mathbf{lose\\ 1\\text{ electron}}$. (Gaining 7 would require excessive energy.)\n\n*Verification:* Sodium donates 1 electron, and chlorine requires 1 electron. The count matches perfectly ($1 = 1$), foreshadowing their $1:1$ combination as NaCl in Topic 2 ✅\n\n### Example 5. Why do magnesium and calcium have identical dot patterns?\n\n- Magnesium has configuration $(2, 8, 2)$ and calcium has $(2, 8, 8, 2)$.\n- Both possess **2 valence electrons**.\n- Therefore, both Lewis symbols display 2 single dots.\n\n*Verification:* Though they have different numbers of inner shells, their chemical behavior is governed entirely by their 2 valence electrons, making both reactive metals that lose 2 electrons ✅\n\n### Example 6. Why are helium and beryllium chemically different despite both having 2 valence electrons?\n\n- Helium has configuration $(2)$. Its only shell (K shell) has a maximum capacity of 2 electrons and is **completely filled** (a duplet). Its 2 dots are drawn paired, and it is chemically inert.\n- Beryllium has configuration $(2, 2)$. Its valence L shell contains 2 electrons but has a capacity of 8. Its outer shell is **incomplete**. Its 2 dots are unpaired, and it actively forms chemical bonds.\n\n*Verification:* Helium possesses a filled outer shell and is stable ✅. Beryllium possesses an incomplete valence shell and forms bonds to reach stability ✅.\n\n### Example 7. How many electrons are needed to complete the valence shell?\n\nState how many electrons each atom must gain or share to achieve a stable octet (or duplet): (a) O, (b) P, (c) H.\n\n- (a) O $(2, 6)$: Needs $8 - 6 = \\mathbf{2\\text{ electrons}}$.\n- (b) P $(2, 8, 5)$: Needs $8 - 5 = \\mathbf{3\\text{ electrons}}$.\n- (c) H $(1)$: Needs $2 - 1 = \\mathbf{1\\text{ electron}}$ to complete its duplet.\n\n*Verification:* These numbers match the standard valencies from our study of chemical formulas: O = 2, P = 3, H = 1 ✅\n\n### Example 8. Which atom has the least tendency to form a chemical bond?\n\nAmong Lithium (1 valence electron), Neon (8 valence electrons), and Oxygen (6 valence electrons), which has the least tendency to react?\n\n- Neon has a completely filled octet $(2, 8)$, so it has **no tendency** to lose, gain, or share electrons.\n- Lithium and Oxygen have incomplete valence shells and react readily.\n\n*Verification:* Neon's Lewis symbol shows 4 complete pairs (no unpaired dots), meaning no vacancies exist for bonding ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1. Nitrogen ka dot symbol banao\n\n- Nitrogen: Z = 7, configuration 2, 5. Valence electrons = 5.\n- Pehle 4 taraf 4 dots (upar, daayen, neeche, baayen). 5vaan dot upar wale ke saath jodi banata hai.\n- Natija: upar **jodi** (2 dots) aur baaki teen taraf **1-1 dot**.\n\nJaanch: 2 (jodi) + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 = valence electrons ✅\n\n### Example 2. Sulphur ka dot symbol\n\n- Sulphur: Z = 16, 2, 8, 6. Valence electrons = 6.\n- 4 taraf 1-1, phir 5vaan upar (jodi), 6th daayen (jodi).\n- Natija: upar aur daayen **jodi**, neeche aur baayen **akela dot**.\n\nJaanch: 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6 ✅ Oxygen (2, 6) ka dot symbol bhi bilkul yahi hoga, kyunki dono ke valence electrons 6 hain ✅\n\n### Example 3. Dot symbol dekh kar tatva ka valence electron batao\n\nEk tatva ke symbol ke aas-paas **teen jodi aur ek akela dot** hain. Valence electrons kitne?\n\n- 3 jodi = 6 dots. 1 akela dot = 1. Kul = 6 + 1 = **7**.\n- 7 valence electrons wale tatva: F (2, 7) ya Cl (2, 8, 7).\n\nJaanch: 3 × 2 + 1 = 7 ✅ Dono ko 1 electron chahiye octet ke liye (8 − 7 = 1) ✅\n\n### Example 4. Chlorine ko kitne electron chahiye, sodium ko kitne dene hain?\n\n- Cl: 2, 8, 7. Octet ke liye 8 − 7 = **1 electron lena** hai.\n- Na: 2, 8, 1. 1 dene se L shell (8) baahari ban jaati hai, to **1 electron dena** aasan hai. (Lena hota to 7 lene padte.)\n\nJaanch: Na ko dene wala 1 electron, aur Cl ko lene wala 1 electron: dono ki ginti **barabar** hai ✅ Is wajah se ek Na aur ek Cl ki jodi banegi, aur ye baat Topic 2 mein poori dikhayenge.\n\n### Example 5. Magnesium aur calcium ke dot symbols ek jaise kyun?\n\n- Mg: 2, 8, 2 aur Ca: 2, 8, 8, 2. Dono ke valence electrons **2**.\n- Dono ke dot symbol: do akele dots (upar aur daayen).\n\nJaanch: Dono ke baahari 2 ✅ Shell alag hain par dots wahi hain, isliye dono ka swabhav ek jaisa: 2 electron **dene** wale metal.\n\n### Example 6. Helium aur beryllium: dono ke 2 valence electrons, phir bhi alag kyun?\n\n- He: configuration 2. Uski ek hi shell K hai, aur wo 2 se **poori bhari** hai (duplet). Dots ki **jodi**.\n- Be: configuration 2, 2. Baahari L shell mein sirf 2 hain, jabki L ko 8 chahiye. Shell **adhoori** hai. Dots **akele-akele**.\n\nJaanch: He ki baahari shell poori hai, isliye wo stable aur nishkriya ✅. Be ki L shell adhoori hai, isliye wo bond banata hai ✅. Dots ki ginti ek jaisi hone par bhi pata chalta hai ki shell ki **kitni jagah** hai.\n\n### Example 7. Octet ke liye kitni kami?\n\nNeeche diye atoms ko octet (H ke liye duplet) ke liye kitne electron lene padenge: (a) O, (b) P, (c) H.\n\n- (a) O: 2, 6. 8 − 6 = **2**.\n- (b) P: 2, 8, 5. 8 − 5 = **3**.\n- (c) H: 1. Duplet 2 ka hai, 2 − 1 = **1**.\n\nJaanch: Pichhle chapter ki valency table se: O 2, P 3, H 1 ✅\n\n### Example 8. Kaun sa atom sabse zyada \"judne ko betab\"?\n\nLi (1 baahari), Ne (8 baahari), O (6 baahari): kaun judne ko sabse kam betab hai?\n\n- Ne ki octet poori hai, wo sabse **kam** betab (lagbhag koi bond nahi).\n- Li aur O dono ki shell adhoori hai, dono bond banate hain.\n\nJaanch: Ne ka dot symbol chaar jodi hai, akela dot koi nahi, matlab koi electron nahi chahiye ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Imagining a chemical bond as a physical stick or string**\n   A chemical bond is not a mechanical link. It is an **electrostatic attractive force** resulting from the interaction and rearrangement of valence electrons.\n\n2. **Mistake 2: Assuming atoms \"desire\" or \"consciously seek\" an octet**\n   Saying \"an atom wants 8 electrons\" is simply a convenient classroom shorthand. Atoms do not have minds or intentions. Nature naturally favors states of lower potential energy, and an octet arrangement happens to represent a state of maximum electrostatic stability and minimum energy.\n\n3. **Mistake 3: Including inner-shell electrons in Lewis dot diagrams**\n   Sodium has 11 electrons in total, but its Lewis symbol has only **1 dot**. Inner core electrons are represented by the chemical symbol itself. Only valence electrons are drawn as dots.\n\n4. **Mistake 4: Miscounting valence electrons**\n   Chlorine must be drawn with 7 dots, never 17. Valence electrons are the electrons in the **outermost shell only**: for Cl $(2, 8, \\mathbf{7})$, that number is 7.\n\n5. **Mistake 5: Assuming all elements follow the exact same bonding route**\n   Metals with 1 to 3 outer electrons lose electrons easily. Non-metals with 5 to 7 outer electrons gain or share electrons easily. A single rule does not apply identically to every element.\n\n6. **Mistake 6: Confusing helium's 2 valence electrons with beryllium's**\n   Both have 2 valence electrons, but helium's shell is complete (capacity 2), whereas beryllium's L shell is incomplete (capacity 8). Always check shell capacity alongside dot count.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: Bond ko dhaaga samajhna**\n\nBond ek **aakarshan** hai, jo electrons ke karan paida hota hai. Atoms ke beech koi asli dhaaga nahi bandha hota.\n\n**Galti 2: Ye maanna ki atom \"chahta\" hai, yaani wo soch kar kaam karta hai**\n\n\"Atom ko octet chahiye\" sirf samajhne ka aasaan tareeka hai. Atom soch nahi sakta. Sach ye hai ki octet wali arrangement zyada stable hoti hai (kam energy), isliye wahi arrangement banti hai.\n\n**Galti 3: Dot symbol mein andar ke electrons bhi dikhana**\n\nNa ke dot symbol mein 11 dots nahi, sirf **1** dot hota hai (valence). Andar ke 10 electrons symbol mein hi samaaye hain.\n\n**Galti 4: Dots ki ginti galat karna**\n\nCl ke 7 dots hone chahiye, 17 nahi. Valence electrons configuration ki **aakhri** ginti hain: Cl 2, 8, **7**.\n\n**Galti 5: Sab atoms ko dhatu ya sab ko adhatu samajhna**\n\n1 se 3 baahari electrons wale (dhatu) **dene** ko aasan samajhte hain. 4 se 7 wale (adhatu) **lene** ya **share** karne ko. Ek hi raasta sab par laagu nahi hota.\n\n**Galti 6: He ko Be jaisa samajhna**\n\nDono ke dots 2 hain, par He ki shell poori hai aur Be ki adhoori. Dots ke saath hamesha **shell ki jagah** bhi dekho."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Atoms form chemical bonds to achieve a **stable, low-energy arrangement** (just as a standing house of cards collapses into a flat, stable pile at rest).\n- The universal benchmarks of stability are the **noble gases**: Helium (duplet of 2) and Neon/Argon (octet of 8).\n- Atoms achieve stability via three distinct routes: **losing electrons**, **gaining electrons**, or **sharing electrons**. Transferring electrons produces **ionic bonds** (Topic 2); sharing electrons produces **covalent bonds** (Topic 3).\n- A **chemical bond** is the attractive force holding atoms or ions together, driven by electron redistribution to minimize potential energy.\n- In a **Lewis electron-dot symbol**, valence electrons are placed around the element's symbol: distribute 1 dot on each of four sides first, then pair them up. Helium is shown with 1 pair (duplet).\n- **Metals** (1–3 valence electrons) tend to lose electrons; **non-metals** (4–7 valence electrons) tend to gain or share electrons; **noble gases** (8 valence electrons) remain chemically unreactive.\n- Electrons needed for an octet $= 8 - (\\text{valence electrons})$ (or $2 - 1 = 1$ for hydrogen).",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Atoms **zyada stable arrangement** paane ke liye judte hain (taash ka ghar dhah kar dher ban jaata hai, jo zyada sthir hai).\n- Stable arrangement ka reference **noble gas** hai: He (2, duplet), Ne (2, 8) aur Ar (2, 8, 8) (octet).\n- Teen raaste: electron **dena**, **lena**, ya **share karna**. Dena + lena se **ionic bond** (Topic 2), share karne se **covalent bond** (Topic 3).\n- **Chemical bond** = atoms (ya ions) ko jodkar rakhne wala aakarshan, jo electrons ke karan banta hai.\n- **Electron-dot symbol** = symbol ke chaaron taraf valence electrons ke dots: pehle 1-1 dot chaaron taraf, phir jodi banao. He mein 2 dots ki jodi.\n- **Dhatu** (baahari 1 se 3): dene wale. **Adhatu** (baahari 4 se 7): lene ya share karne wale. C, Si (4): aksar share. **Noble gas**: judte nahi.\n- Octet ke liye kami = 8 − (valence electrons) (H ke liye 2 − 1 = 1)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Why do atoms combine to form chemical bonds? Frame your answer using the concept of a stable arrangement.\n\n2. What is a chemical bond? Is it a physical connection like a thread? Explain clearly.\n\n3. Write the electron configuration and the number of valence electrons for each element: Li, C, O, Cl, and Ca. Draw the Lewis electron-dot symbol for each.\n\n4. Draw the electron-dot symbols for H, He, N, Ne, Al, and S. Verify each diagram by showing that the dot count equals the valence electron count.\n\n5. The electron-dot symbol of an unknown element features two pairs of dots and two single dots. How many valence electrons does it have? Name two elements from the first 20 that share this Lewis structure.\n\n6. Why do Li, Na, and K share the same dot symbol? Why do F and Cl share the same dot symbol?\n\n7. State the three pathways an atom can use to complete its valence shell. Which pathway will sodium most likely choose, and why? Which pathway will chlorine choose, and why?\n\n8. Both helium and beryllium have 2 valence electrons. Why is helium chemically inert while beryllium readily forms chemical bonds?\n\n9. How many electrons must each of the following atoms gain or share to achieve a stable configuration: (a) F, (b) S, (c) N, (d) H?\n\n10. Classify each of the following elements as an electron donor (likely to lose) or an electron acceptor/sharer (likely to gain/share): K, O, Mg, P, Al, F. State the number of valence electrons for each.\n\n11. Match each role with the corresponding species: (a) loses an electron, (b) gains an electron, (c) shares an electron pair: Sodium (Na), Chlorine atom (Cl), the pair of chlorine atoms in $\\text{Cl}_2$.\n\n12. State whether each statement is True or False, and correct the false statements:\n    (a) Neon has 8 dots in its Lewis symbol and is extremely reactive.\n    (b) The Lewis dot symbol for sodium contains 11 dots.\n    (c) The Lewis dot symbols for carbon and silicon both contain 4 single dots.\n    (d) A noble gas atom has no natural tendency to form chemical bonds.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Atoms combine because most have incomplete valence shells, which represent a relatively unstable, higher-energy state. By transferring or sharing electrons, atoms attain completely filled outer shells (duplets or octets) identical to noble gases, reaching a deeply stable, lower-energy state.\n\n2. A chemical bond is an attractive electrostatic force holding atoms or ions together. It is not a physical thread or rod; rather, it arises from the electrical forces between positive atomic nuclei and negatively charged electrons.\n\n3. - $\\text{Li}$: $(2, 1)$, 1 valence electron $\\rightarrow$ $\\text{Li} \\bullet$\n   - $\\text{C}$: $(2, 4)$, 4 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ $\\bullet \\dot{\\text{C}} \\bullet$ (4 single dots)\n   - $\\text{O}$: $(2, 6)$, 6 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ 2 pairs and 2 single dots\n   - $\\text{Cl}$: $(2, 8, 7)$, 7 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ 3 pairs and 1 single dot\n   - $\\text{Ca}$: $(2, 8, 8, 2)$, 2 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ 2 single dots\n\n4. - $\\text{H}$: 1 dot (valence $= 1$) ✅\n   - $\\text{He}$: 1 pair of 2 dots (valence $= 2$, duplet) ✅\n   - $\\text{N}$: 1 pair and 3 single dots (valence $= 5$) ✅\n   - $\\text{Ne}$: 4 pairs, 8 dots total (valence $= 8$) ✅\n   - $\\text{Al}$: 3 single dots (valence $= 3$) ✅\n   - $\\text{S}$: 2 pairs and 2 single dots (valence $= 6$) ✅\n\n5. Two pairs ($2 \\times 2 = 4$) plus two single dots ($2$) equals **6 valence electrons**. From the first 20 elements, **Oxygen** $(2, 6)$ and **Sulphur** $(2, 8, 6)$ both have this structure.\n\n6. $\\text{Li}$ $(2, 1)$, $\\text{Na}$ $(2, 8, 1)$, and $\\text{K}$ $(2, 8, 8, 1)$ all have exactly 1 valence electron, so each has 1 dot. Similarly, $\\text{F}$ $(2, 7)$ and $\\text{Cl}$ $(2, 8, 7)$ both have 7 valence electrons, giving each 3 pairs and 1 single dot. The Lewis symbol depends only on the outermost electrons, regardless of inner shells.\n\n7. The three pathways are: **losing electrons**, **gaining electrons**, and **sharing electrons**.\n   - $\\text{Na}$ $(2, 8, 1)$ will **lose 1 electron**, leaving its filled second shell $(2, 8)$ as the outer shell; gaining 7 electrons would require too much energy.\n   - $\\text{Cl}$ $(2, 8, 7)$ will **gain 1 electron** (or share), because it needs only 1 electron to complete its octet $(8 - 7 = 1)$.\n\n8. Helium has only one shell (the K shell), which is completely filled with 2 electrons (a duplet), making it stable and inert. Beryllium's outer shell is the L shell, which requires 8 electrons for stability; with only 2 electrons, its shell is incomplete, so it readily reacts.\n\n9. (a) $\\text{F}$ $(2, 7)$: needs **1 electron**.\n   (b) $\\text{S}$ $(2, 8, 6)$: needs **2 electrons**.\n   (c) $\\text{N}$ $(2, 5)$: needs **3 electrons**.\n   (d) $\\text{H}$ $(1)$: needs **1 electron** (to reach a duplet of 2).\n\n10. - $\\text{K}$: 1 valence electron $\\rightarrow$ **Electron donor** (loses 1)\n    - $\\text{O}$: 6 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Electron acceptor/sharer** (gains/shares 2)\n    - $\\text{Mg}$: 2 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Electron donor** (loses 2)\n    - $\\text{P}$: 5 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Electron acceptor/sharer** (gains/shares 3)\n    - $\\text{Al}$: 3 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Electron donor** (loses 3)\n    - $\\text{F}$: 7 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Electron acceptor/sharer** (gains/shares 1)\n\n11. (a) $\\text{Na}$ $(2, 8, 1)$ **loses** an electron.\n    (b) A neutral $\\text{Cl}$ atom $(2, 8, 7)$ **gains** an electron when forming salt.\n    (c) The two $\\text{Cl}$ atoms in $\\text{Cl}_2$ **share** an electron pair.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. Neon has 8 dots (an octet) and is chemically unreactive (inert), not reactive.\n    (b) **False** ❌. Sodium's Lewis dot symbol has only **1 dot** (its single valence electron).\n    (c) **True** ✅. Both have 4 valence electrons and are drawn with 4 single dots.\n    (d) **True** ✅. With completely filled valence shells, noble gases have no tendency to bond.\n\n---\n\nWell done! You now understand the fundamental reason **why atoms bond**: by combining, they transition from an unstable state to a settled, low-energy electron configuration, just like cards resting flat on a table. You can confidently construct the Lewis electron-dot symbol for any of the first 20 elements and predict whether an atom will donate, accept, or share electrons.\n\n**What's next?** In the next topic, **\"Ionic Bond: Transfer of Electrons\"**, we will explore what happens when an electron-donating metal meets an electron-accepting non-metal. We will trace the transfer of electrons between sodium and chlorine, magnesium and oxygen, and discover how crystal lattices and chemical formulas naturally emerge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Why do atoms form chemical bonds? Use the idea of a stable arrangement in your answer.\n\n2. What is a chemical bond? Is it a physical thread joining two atoms? Explain.\n\n3. Write the electron configuration and the number of valence electrons of each: Li, C, O, Cl, Ca. Draw the electron-dot symbol of each.\n\n4. Draw the electron-dot symbols of: H, He, N, Ne, Al, S. Check each by counting that the dots equal the valence electrons.\n\n5. The electron-dot symbol of an element shows two pairs of dots and two single dots. How many valence electrons does it have? Name two possible elements from the first 20.\n\n6. Why are the dot symbols of Li, Na and K alike? Why are the dot symbols of F and Cl alike?\n\n7. Name the three ways an atom can reach a stable outer shell. Which of these will Na probably choose, and why? Which will Cl probably choose, and why?\n\n8. Both He and Be have 2 valence electrons. Why is He stable while Be is not?\n\n9. How many electrons must each of these gain to complete its octet (or duplet): (a) F, (b) S, (c) N, (d) H?\n\n10. Classify these as likely electron donors (give away) or likely to gain or share: K, O, Mg, P, Al, F. Give the number of valence electrons for each.\n\n11. Match the type to the example: (a) gives electrons (b) takes electrons (c) shares electrons: Na, Cl, the pair of Cl atoms in Cl₂.\n\n12. State True or False and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Neon has 8 dots in its dot symbol and is very reactive.\n    (b) Sodium's dot symbol has 11 dots.\n    (c) The dot symbols of C and Si both have 4 single dots.\n    (d) A noble gas has no strong urge to form bonds.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Kyunki bahut se atoms ki baahari shell adhoori hoti hai, aur adhoori shell kam stable hai. Atoms electron dekar, lekar ya share karke noble gas jaisi poori shell (duplet ya octet) pa lete hain, jo zyada stable (kam energy wali) arrangement hai. Bond isi se banta hai.\n\n2. Chemical bond atoms (ya ions) ko jodkar rakhne wala **aakarshan** hai, jo electrons ke karan banta hai. Ye koi asli dhaaga nahi hai.\n\n3. Li: 2, 1 (1 valence). C: 2, 4 (4). O: 2, 6 (6). Cl: 2, 8, 7 (7). Ca: 2, 8, 8, 2 (2). Dot symbols: Li ke 1 dot; C ke chaaron taraf 1-1 (4 akele); O ke 2 jodi + 2 akele; Cl ke 3 jodi + 1 akela; Ca ke 2 akele dots.\n\n4. H: 1 dot. He: 1 jodi (2 dots). N: 1 jodi + 3 akele (5). Ne: 4 jodi (8). Al: 3 akele dots (3). S: 2 jodi + 2 akele (6). Har ek mein ginti valence electrons ke barabar hai (1, 2, 5, 8, 3, 6).\n\n5. 2 jodi = 4 dots, 2 akele dots = 2 dots, kul **6** valence electrons. Pehle 20 tatvon mein O (2, 6) aur S (2, 8, 6).\n\n6. Li (2, 1), Na (2, 8, 1) aur K (2, 8, 8, 1) mein valence electrons 1 hain, isliye dot symbol mein 1 dot. F (2, 7) aur Cl (2, 8, 7) mein 7 hain, isliye 3 jodi + 1 akela. Dot symbol sirf valence electrons ginti par tikta hai, andar ke shell par nahi.\n\n7. Electron **dena**, **lena**, ya **share karna**. Na (2, 8, 1): **dena**, kyunki 1 dene se 2, 8 bachta hai (neon jaisa), jabki lena ho to 7 lene padte. Cl (2, 8, 7): **lena** (ya share karna), kyunki sirf 1 kam hai octet ke liye (8 − 7 = 1).\n\n8. He (2) ki ek hi shell K hai aur wo 2 se poori bhari hai (duplet). Be (2, 2) ki baahari L shell mein sirf 2 hain jabki 8 chahiye, shell adhoori hai.\n\n9. (a) F (2, 7): **1**. (b) S (2, 8, 6): **2**. (c) N (2, 5): **3**. (d) H (1): duplet ke liye **1**.\n\n10. K: 1 valence, **dene wala**. O: 6, **lene ya share karne wala**. Mg: 2, **dene wala**. P: 5, **lene ya share karne wala**. Al: 3, **dene wala**. F: 7, **lene ya share karne wala**.\n\n11. (a) Na (2, 8, 1) electron **deta** hai. (b) Cl (2, 8, 7) electron **leta** hai. (c) Cl₂ mein dono Cl atom ek jodi electron **share** karte hain.\n\n12. (a) **False** ❌. Ne ke 8 dots (octet) hain, par wo stable hai aur lagbhag kisi se nahi judta. (b) **False** ❌. Na ke sirf **1** dot hote hain (valence electron). (c) **True** ✅. Dono ke 4 valence electrons hain, 4 akele dots. (d) **True** ✅. Unki shell poori hai.\n\n---\n\nShaabash! Ab tumhe pata hai ki atoms **judte kyun hain**: kyunki jud kar wo taash ke dhahe hue dher ki tarah zyada stable arrangement paa lete hain. Tum kisi bhi pehle-20 tatva ka electron-dot symbol bana sakte ho, aur dekh kar bata sakte ho ki wo dene, lene ya share karne wala hai. Saksham ne us din ek aur ghar nahi banaya, lekin patton ka dher dekh kar bola, \"Papa, ye dher bhi kamaal ka hai.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Agle topic **\"Ionic Bond: Electron Dene-Lene Ka Rishta\"** mein dekhenge ki jab ek dene wala dhatu aur ek lene wala adhatu milte hain, to Na aur Cl ka 1-1 electron ka lena-dena kaise unhe ek mazboot rishte mein baandh deta hai, aur isi se namak (NaCl), MgO aur kai compounds ke formula kaise nikalte hain."
          }
