            "headingHinglish": "Chips ka phoola hua packet",
            "body": "Daniel picked up a sealed bag of potato chips at the grocery store and held it up to his uncle Mr. Parker. The bag was puffed up like a pillow. \"Uncle, this bag is half air and only half chips! Are they short-changing us?\"\n\nMr. Parker laughed. \"That isn't ordinary air inside the bag, Daniel. It's pure **nitrogen gas**. And it has a very important job: keeping those crispy chips from going bad.\"\n\nWhen they got home, Daniel opened an old tin of roasted nuts and fried crisps that had been sitting at the back of the pantry with a loose lid. A sharp, stale, unpleasant smell wafted out. \"Ugh! These smell awful.\" Mr. Parker nodded: \"And right there is today's chemistry question: why do potato chips stay fresh for months inside a nitrogen-flushed bag, while snacks in a loosely closed tin turn stale and smelly? Let's look at three big oxidation reactions in everyday life: combustion, rusting, and rancidity.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Kelkar ke bhanje Ashwin ne dukaan se chips ka packet liya. Packet hawa se phoola hua tha. \"Mama, isme chips kam aur hawa zyada hai! Dukaandar ne thaga kya?\"\n\nMr. Kelkar hanse. \"Wo hawa nahi hai, beta. Usme **nitrogen gas** bhari hai. Aur uska ek bada kaam hai: chips ko kharab hone se bachana.\"\n\nGhar aakar Ashwin ne rasoi ke shelf se ek purana namkeen ka dabba khola. Dhakkan thoda dheela tha. Andar se ajeeb, khatti-si, baasi gandh aayi. \"Chhi! Ye to kharab ho gaya.\" Mr. Kelkar ne kaha, \"Yehi to hai aaj ka sawaal: ek packet mein gas bhar ke chips bachte hain, aur dheele dabbe mein namkeen kharab ho jaati hai. Kya fark hai? Chalo, roz ki chemistry mein dekhte hain: jalna, zang aur ye baasi gandh.\""
