            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Combustion (burning)** | A reaction in which a fuel burns in oxygen, releasing heat and light | A gas stove flame, burning wood |\n| **Fuel** | A substance that is burned to provide heat or power | Natural gas, LPG, wood, coal, petrol |\n| **Fire triangle** | The three things needed for a fire: fuel, oxygen, and heat | Remove any one of the three and the fire goes out |\n| **Complete combustion** | Burning in plenty of oxygen so that all the carbon in the fuel turns into CO₂ | A clean blue stove flame |\n| **Incomplete combustion** | Burning in a limited supply of oxygen, producing toxic carbon monoxide (CO) gas and soot | A charcoal brazier in a closed room |\n| **Rusting** | The slow conversion of iron into flaky, reddish-brown hydrated iron(III) oxide in the presence of air and moisture | A rusty bicycle chain |\n| **Corrosion** | The gradual deterioration of a metal surface by reaction with air, moisture, or other gases | Green patina on copper, black tarnish on silver |\n| **Rancidity** | The spoilage of fats and oils by slow oxidation in air, producing an unpleasant smell and taste | Stale fried snacks or old cooking oil |\n| **Antioxidant** | A substance added to food to slow down oxidation | Vitamin E or food-grade preservatives |",
