          {
            "heading": "Part A: Combustion (burning)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Jalna (Combustion)",
            "body": "**Combustion** is a chemical reaction in which a **fuel** reacts rapidly with **oxygen**, giving off **heat and light**. It is both an exothermic reaction (Topic 4) and an oxidation reaction (Topic 5), because the fuel combines with oxygen.\n\nThe simplest example of a gaseous fuel burning on a kitchen stove is methane (the main component of natural gas):\n\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O  (with heat and light)\n\nBottled LPG is a **mixture** of hydrocarbon gases (mainly propane and butane); you do not need to memorise their formulas. Simply remember the big pattern: when a hydrocarbon fuel burns in plenty of air, its carbon and hydrogen combine with oxygen to form **carbon dioxide (CO₂)** and **water vapour (H₂O)**. Atom count: left has 1 C, 4 H, 4 O; right has 1 C, 4 H, and 2 + 2 = 4 O ✅.\n\nSolid carbon (such as charcoal or coal) also burns by combining directly with oxygen: C + O₂ → CO₂.\n\n### The three requirements for fire: the fire triangle\n\nFor a fire to start and keep burning, **all three** corners of the **fire triangle** must be present: **fuel**, **oxygen**, and **heat** (enough heat to bring the fuel to its ignition temperature). If you remove **even one** of the three, the fire goes out immediately.\n\n```\n                      HEAT\n                       /\\\n                      /  \\\n                     /    \\\n                    / FIRE \\\n                   /        \\\n                  /__________\\\n               FUEL          OXYGEN\n\n    Three ways to put out a fire: remove any one corner\n      remove fuel   : turn off the gas valve; clear away dry brush\n      remove oxygen : smother with a fire blanket, lid, or CO₂ extinguisher\n      remove heat   : spray water on burning wood or paper to cool it below ignition\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – The fire triangle: a fire needs fuel, oxygen, and heat all at once. Removing any one of the three extinguishes the fire.\n\nLet us match everyday fire-safety actions to the fire triangle:\n\n- **Turning off the knob on a gas stove:** cuts off the supply of fuel, so the flame dies instantly. (Fuel removed.)\n- **Sliding a metal lid or damp fire blanket over a pan of burning cooking oil:** blocks air from reaching the oil. (Oxygen removed.) **Never pour water onto burning oil**: water sinks beneath hot oil, flashes into steam explosively, and sprays burning oil droplets into a dangerous fireball.\n- **Pouring water onto a wood or paper campfire:** cools the burning wood below the temperature needed to sustain combustion. (Heat removed.)\n- **Using a CO₂ fire extinguisher:** carbon dioxide is denser than air, so it settles over the fire like an invisible blanket and pushes oxygen away. (Oxygen removed.)\n\nStudents should **never experiment with fire**; all combustion demonstrations are conducted only by teachers or adults.\n\n### Complete vs incomplete combustion: the danger of carbon monoxide\n\nWhen there is **plenty of oxygen**, carbon burns completely to form carbon dioxide (CO₂). When **oxygen is limited** (for example, inside a poorly ventilated room or a clogged burner), carbon does not burn completely and instead forms **carbon monoxide (CO)**:\n\n2C + O₂ → 2CO  (incomplete combustion)\n\nFor methane burning in limited oxygen: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O. Atom count: left has 2 C, 8 H, 6 O; right has 2 C, 8 H, and 2 + 4 = 6 O ✅.\n\n**Carbon monoxide (CO) is a deadly poisonous gas.** Worse still, it is **colourless and completely odourless**, so you cannot see it or smell it building up in a room. When inhaled, CO binds tightly to haemoglobin in the blood in place of oxygen, starving the brain and body of oxygen and causing dizziness, headache, unconsciousness, and death. That is why every household should follow these life-saving precautions:\n\n- **Never burn a charcoal grill, coal brazier, or portable camp stove inside a closed room, tent, or garage.** Every winter, tragic poisonings happen when people bring glowing coals indoors for warmth with the windows shut.\n- **Ensure proper ventilation for gas water heaters and furnaces:** gas water heaters must be properly vented to the outside and used only where fresh air can circulate freely.\n- **Check the colour of your kitchen stove flame:** a healthy gas flame burns **clean blue** (complete combustion). A **yellow, smoky, sooty flame** means incomplete combustion is happening—have an adult get the burner ports cleaned and checked.\n- If anyone ever shows signs of CO poisoning, **move them into fresh outdoor air immediately** and call emergency medical services.\n\n(Notice too that carbon monoxide can burn further to CO₂ if given more oxygen: 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ ✅, with 2 C and 4 O on both sides.)\n\n> **Quick check:** A gas stove burner is burning with a yellow flame and leaving black soot on the bottom of pots. Is this complete combustion? ❌ No—it is incomplete combustion caused by insufficient air mixing with the gas, which wastes fuel and can produce CO.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Combustion matlab koi padarth (**fuel**) **oxygen** mein jal kar **garmi aur roshni** deta hai. Ye ek exothermic reaction hai (Topic 4) aur ek oxidation bhi (Topic 5): fuel oxygen se judta hai.\n\nChulhe mein jalne wali gas ka sabse simple example methane hai:\n\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O  (garmi aur roshni ke saath)\n\nGhar ka LPG ek **mixture** hai (kai gas ka), uska formula yaad rakhne ki zaroorat nahi. Bas itna samjho: jo fuel mein carbon aur hydrogen hain, wo jal kar **CO₂** aur **paani (bhaap)** dete hain. Atom count: baayein taraf 1 C, 4 H, 4 O; daayein taraf 1 C, 4 H, 2 + 2 = 4 O ✅.\n\nSeedha carbon ka jalna bhi dekho: C + O₂ → CO₂. Koyla jalta hai to yehi hota hai.\n\n### Aag ke liye teen cheezein: fire triangle\n\nAag lagne ke liye **teeno** chahiye: **fuel**, **oxygen** aur **garmi** (itni garmi ki fuel jalna shuru kar de). Ek bhi hataya to aag nahi jalegi, ya bujh jayegi.\n\n```\n                    GARMI\n                      /\\\n                     /  \\\n                    /    \\\n                   /  AAG \\\n                  /        \\\n                 /__________\\\n              FUEL          OXYGEN\n\n   Aag bujhane ke teen tareeke: teeno mein se ek kona hatao\n     fuel hatao   : gas ka valve band karo, jalti lakdi alag karo\n     oxygen hatao : kambal ya gili bori se dhako, CO₂ wala extinguisher\n     garmi hatao  : paani daalo (thanda karne ke liye)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – Fire triangle: aag ke liye fuel, oxygen aur garmi teeno chahiye. Inme se ek bhi hata do to aag bujh jaati hai.\n\nKuch real example, sirf samajhne ke liye:\n\n- **Gas ka chulha band karna:** valve band karne par fuel milna band, aag bujh gayi. (Fuel hataya.)\n- **Kadhai mein tel ki aag par gili bori ya dhakkan:** oxygen ruk gaya. (Oxygen hataya.) Yahan **paani kabhi nahi** daalna: jalta tel chheente ki tarah bikhar kar aur phail jaata hai.\n- **Lakdi ki aag par paani:** garmi kam hui. (Garmi hatayi.)\n- **CO₂ wala extinguisher:** CO₂ bhaari gas hai, aag par kambal ki tarah failkar oxygen ko door karti hai.\n\nAag ka koi bhi prayog bachche **sirf bade ya teacher ke saath** dekhein, khud kabhi nahi.\n\n### Poora jalna aur adhura jalna: carbon monoxide ka khatra\n\nJab oxygen **kaafi** hota hai, carbon poora jal kar CO₂ banata hai. Jab oxygen **kam** hota hai (band kamra, dhakka hua chulha), carbon poora nahi jalta aur **carbon monoxide (CO)** banta hai:\n\n2C + O₂ → 2CO  (adhura jalna)\n\nMethane ke liye bhi: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O. Atom count: baayein 2 C, 8 H, 6 O; daayein 2 C, 8 H, 2 + 4 = 6 O ✅.\n\n**CO ek zehreeli gas hai.** Ye **bina rang aur bina gandh** ki hai, to dikhti bhi nahi aur pata bhi nahi chalta. Ye khoon mein oxygen ki jagah le leti hai, isliye chakkar, sar dard aur behoshi ho sakti hai, aur band jagah mein jaan ka khatra hai. Isi liye:\n\n- **Band kamre mein angithi ya koyle ki sigri jala kar kabhi mat sona.** Har saal sardiyon mein aisi durghatnayein hoti hain.\n- **Gas geyser** bathroom ke andar band jagah mein ho to ventilation (hawa ka raasta) zaroor ho, aur geyser chalate waqt dwar aur khidki ke paas hawa aane do.\n- Rasoi mein chulhe ki lau **neeli** honi chahiye. Peeli, dhuaan wali lau ka matlab oxygen kam hai aur adhura jalna ho raha hai: burner saaf karwao.\n- Kisi ko CO ki zehar lage to turant use **khuli hawa mein** le jao aur doctor ko bulao.\n\nBachche ye saari cheezein sirf jaan lein aur bado ko bataayein; khud koi aag ya geyser nahi chalayein.\n\nEk aur baat: CO khud bhi jal sakti hai aur zyada oxygen mile to CO₂ ban jaati hai: 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ ✅ (2 C, 4 O dono taraf).\n\n> **Jaanch:** Gas ki lau peeli aur dhuaan wali hai. Kya ye poora jalna hai? ❌ Nahi, ye adhura jalna hai (oxygen kam), aur isme CO bhi ban sakti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Rusting of iron",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Zang (Rusting)",
            "body": "When **iron (Fe)** is left exposed to both **air (oxygen)** and **moisture (water)**, its surface slowly turns into a **flaky, reddish-brown coating** called **rust**. Rust is not metallic iron—it is a brittle new compound that crumbles away instead of holding weight.\n\nSimplified equation:\n\n4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃\n\nAtom count: left has 4 Fe and 6 O; right has 2 × 2 = 4 Fe and 2 × 3 = 6 O ✅.\n\n**Honest chemistry note:** Real rust is *hydrated* iron(III) oxide (meaning water molecules are attached inside the solid crystal), so 4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃ is a **simplified** equation. Even so, it clearly shows the core redox process: iron combines with oxygen, meaning iron is **oxidised** (Topic 5).\n\nRusting requires **both** oxygen and water at the same time. In bone-dry desert air, or submerged in boiled water sealed away from air, iron barely rusts at all. Near the sea, where the air is humid and carries salt spray, iron rusts much faster.\n\n```\n          AIR (oxygen)          WATER / MOISTURE\n               \\                     /\n                v                   v\n           ,~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~,\n           |  thin film of moisture       |\n           '~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~'\n           ################################   <- iron (Fe)\n                   ||        ||\n             rust: flaky reddish-brown Fe₂O₃ forms\n           ##########xxxxxx################\n                    (rust flakes off, exposing fresh\n                     iron underneath to air and water)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – When iron, oxygen from the air, and water meet, iron oxidises into rust (Fe₂O₃). Because rust is porous and flaky, it peels off and exposes fresh iron underneath, allowing rusting to eat deeper and deeper into the metal.\n\n### Safe home investigation: what does rusting need? (at home with an adult)\n\nSet up three clean glass jars, each containing one **shiny new iron nail**:\n\n1. **Jar A:** Stand the nail half-submerged in **ordinary tap water** (exposed to both **air and water**).\n2. **Jar B:** Submerge the nail completely in **water that an adult has boiled and cooled** (boiling drives out dissolved air), and pour a thin layer of cooking oil on top to keep air out (**water only, no air**).\n3. **Jar C:** Place the nail in a **dry jar** (with a small packet of silica gel desiccant from a shoebox if available) and close the lid (**air only, no moisture**).\n\nHave an adult handle the boiled water. Leave the three jars undisturbed for 4 to 7 days and compare. **What you will see:** the nail in Jar A develops obvious reddish-brown rust, while the nails in Jar B and Jar C show little or no rust. **Conclusion:** rusting requires **both** oxygen (air) and water (moisture). Wash your hands after handling the nails.\n\n### How to prevent rusting\n\nThe core strategy for stopping rust is simple: **keep air and moisture from reaching the iron surface.**\n\n| Method | How it works | Everyday example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Painting** | Forms a dry barrier coat that blocks air and moisture | Iron gates, park benches, bridges |\n| **Oiling or greasing** | Forms a water-repellent film on moving parts | Bicycle chains, garden tools, machine gears |\n| **Galvanising (zinc coating)** | Coats iron with a thin layer of zinc; because zinc is more reactive than iron, the zinc oxidises first and protects the iron even if scratched | Galvanised roofing sheets, water pipes, buckets |\n| **Tin or chromium plating** | Plates a thin, corrosion-resistant metal layer onto steel | Tin food cans, shiny chrome bicycle handlebars |\n| **Alloying (stainless steel)** | Mixes iron with chromium and nickel to make an alloy that resists rusting | Kitchen sinks, cutlery, cooking pots |\n\n> **Quick check:** If the paint on an iron garden gate chips off in a patch, will that spot start to rust? ✅ Yes, because the bare iron is once again exposed to air and rain.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Loha (iron)** agar **hawa (oxygen)** aur **nami (paani)** dono ke sampark mein rahe, to dheere-dheere uspar **bhoora-laal bhurbhura coat** ban jaata hai. Ise **zang** kehte hain. Zang loha nahi hai: ye ek naya padarth hai, jo kamzor aur jhadne wala hai.\n\nSimplified equation:\n\n4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃\n\nAtom count: baayein 4 Fe, 6 O; daayein 2 × 2 = 4 Fe, 2 × 3 = 6 O ✅.\n\n**Sacchi baat:** asli zang mein paani ke kuch kan bhi judte hain (hydrated), isliye ye equation sirf **simplified** hai. Par ye mool baat dikhati hai: loha oxygen se judta hai, yaani **oxidise** hota hai (Topic 5).\n\nZang tab hi lagti hai jab **dono** hon. Sukhe mein ya bina hawa ke loha jaldi zang nahi khata. Samundar ke paas, jahan hawa mein nami aur namak hai, zang aur tez lagti hai.\n\n```\n        HAWA (oxygen)          PAANI / NAMI\n              \\                     /\n               v                   v\n          ,~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~,\n          |  paani ki patli parat        |\n          '~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~'\n          ################################   <- loha (Fe)\n                  ||        ||\n            zang: bhoora-laal, bhurbhura Fe₂O₃ ban raha hai\n          ##########xxxxxx################\n                   (zang jhadkar neeche ka loha\n                    phir hawa-paani ke saamne laati hai)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Loha, hawa ki oxygen aur paani saath mein aate hain to loha oxidise hokar zang (Fe₂O₃) banata hai. Zang bhurbhuri hai, isliye jhad jaati hai aur neeche ka loha dobara khulta jaata hai, aur zang aage badhti hai.\n\n### Safe jaanch: zang ke liye kya zaroori hai (ghar par adult ke saath)\n\nTeen saaf glass ya jar lo, har ek mein ek **naya loha ka keel**:\n\n1. Jar A: keel ko **paani** mein aadha doobo (hawa aur paani dono).\n2. Jar B: keel ko **ubale hue, thanda kiye hue paani** mein dalo aur upar thoda tel ki parat daal do (paani hai, par hawa nahi).\n3. Jar C: keel ko **sukhe** jar mein rakho (hawa hai, nami nahi).\n\nGaram ubla paani adult hi handle kare. Dhakkan dheela rakho aur 4 se 7 din dekho. **Dekhoge:** keel A par zang, B aur C mein bahut kam ya nahi. Natija: zang ke liye hawa **aur** nami dono chahiye. Keel haath se chhoo kar haath dho lo.\n\n### Zang se bachne ke tareeke\n\nZang rokne ka mool tareeka hai: loha ko hawa aur paani se **door** rakho.\n\n| Tareeka | Kaise kaam karta hai | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Paint** | Loha ke upar parat, hawa-paani nahi pahunchte | Gate, railing |\n| **Tel ya grease** | Patli chikni parat | Cycle ki chain, tools |\n| **Galvanising (zinc ki parat)** | Zinc ki parat chadhao; zinc loha se zyada reactive hai, wahi pehle oxidise hota hai aur loha bachta hai | Loha ki chaadar, paani ki tanki |\n| **Tin ya chromium plating** | Ek patli parat chadhao | Dabbe, bumper |\n| **Alloy (stainless steel)** | Loha mein chromium, nickel milakar mishran, jisme zang nahi lagti | Rasoi ke bartan, chamach |\n\n> **Jaanch:** Paint chhil jaaye to loha zang khayega. ✅ Kyunki loha dobara hawa aur nami ke sampark mein aa jaata hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Corrosion of other metals",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Dusre metals ka corrosion",
            "body": "We use the word *rusting* specifically for iron. The gradual wearing away of any metal by reaction with air, moisture, or gases in the environment is called **corrosion**.\n\n- **Green coating on copper:** Over time, copper roofs, statues (like the Statue of Liberty), and old copper vessels react slowly with oxygen, carbon dioxide, and moisture in the air to form a pale **green patina** (basic copper carbonate). (At home with an adult, rubbing tarnished copper with a little lemon juice and salt dissolves that basic coating and restores the bright copper shine.)\n- **Black tarnish on silver:** Trace amounts of sulphur compounds in the air (such as hydrogen sulphide, H₂S) react with silver spoons and jewellery to form a thin black coating of silver sulphide (Ag₂S): 4Ag + 2H₂S + O₂ → 2Ag₂S + 2H₂O. Atom count: left has 4 Ag, 4 H, 2 S, 2 O; right has 4 Ag, 2 S, 4 H, 2 O ✅. (That is also why silver tarnishes quickly if left near eggs or onions, which contain sulphur compounds.)\n- **Aluminium's self-protecting oxide layer:** Aluminium sits high on the reactivity ladder (above zinc and iron!), yet aluminium window frames, pans, and foil do not crumble away. Why? Because the instant fresh aluminium touches air, it reacts with oxygen to form an ultra-thin, tough, tightly clinging layer of **aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃)**: 4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃ ✅ (4 Al and 6 O on both sides). Unlike flaky rust on iron, this Al₂O₃ film seals the metal underneath completely against further air and water attack! Here, surface oxidation actually **protects** the metal.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Zang sirf loha ke liye kehte hain. Dusre metals bhi dheere-dheere kharab hote hain, ise **corrosion** kehte hain.\n\n- **Taambe (copper) par hara coat:** purane taambe ke bartan ya mandir ki moorti par hawa ki oxygen, CO₂ aur nami se hara parat jam jaati hai. Nimbu-imli se ragadne par chamak wapas aati hai (ghar par adult ke saath).\n- **Chaandi (silver) ka kaala hona:** hawa mein bahut kam sulphur wali gas (jaise H₂S) hoti hai. Isse chaandi par kaala parat Ag₂S ka banta hai: 4Ag + 2H₂S + O₂ → 2Ag₂S + 2H₂O. Atom count: baayein 4 Ag, 4 H, 2 S, 2 O; daayein 4 Ag, 2 S, 4 H, 2 O ✅. (Ande ya pyaaz ke paas rakhi chaandi isi liye kaali padti hai.)\n- **Aluminium:** hawa mein turant oxygen se judkar bahut patli, kasi hui **Al₂O₃ ki parat** banata hai: 4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃ ✅ (4 Al, 6 O dono taraf). Ye parat paani aur hawa ko andar nahi jaane deti, isliye aluminium ka bartan aur foil ko aage corrosion nahi hota. Yahan oxide **madad karta hai**, loha ke zang se ulta."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Rancidity (when fats and oils turn stale)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Rancidity (tel-ghee ka baasi hona)",
            "body": "Now let us return to Daniel's pantry tin of stale snacks. Mr. Parker pointed to the tin and said, \"Fried crisps and roasted nuts contain fats and oils. When **fats and oils** stay exposed to oxygen in the air, they slowly **oxidise** into smaller compounds that have a sour, stale smell and unpleasant taste. Chemists call this spoilage **rancidity**.\"\n\nNotice the connection: rancidity is yet another everyday **oxidation** reaction (Topic 5). Heat, sunlight, and moisture all speed it up.\n\n**How we prevent or slow down rancidity:**\n\n- **Airtight containers:** Keeping lids tightly sealed stops fresh oxygen from continuously reaching the food.\n- **Refrigeration:** Storing butter, nuts, and leftovers in a cool fridge slows down the rate of oxidation (Topic 4).\n- **Cool, dark storage:** Keeping cooking oils in dark bottles or closed cupboards away from sunlight and stove heat slows photolytic and thermal oxidation.\n- **Adding antioxidants:** Food manufacturers add small amounts of safe **antioxidants** (such as vitamin E / tocopherols) that react with oxygen first, just like the vitamin C on Maya's apple slice.\n- **Flushing packets with nitrogen gas (N₂):** Before sealing bags of potato chips or snack crisps, factories flush out the air (and its reactive O₂) and fill the bag with **nitrogen gas**. Because N₂ has a very strong triple bond (Chapter 6), it is unreactive at room temperature, so the oil in the chips does not oxidise. As a bonus, the puffed-up nitrogen cushion stops the thin chips from getting crushed during shipping!\n\n```\n    Loosely closed tin             Nitrogen-flushed chip bag\n    (air keeps entering)           (oxygen replaced by nitrogen)\n\n    +----------------+            ,-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~-.\n    |  O₂   O₂   O₂  |           (  N₂   N₂   N₂   N₂  )\n    |  oily snacks   |           (   N₂  chips  N₂     )\n    | oxidised by O₂ |            ( N₂   N₂   N₂   N₂ )\n    +----------------+            '-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~-'\n\n    oxygen oxidises fats:          no oxygen, so no oxidation:\n    stale, rancid smell            chips stay crisp and fresh\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – In a loosely closed container, oxygen from the air oxidises the fats and oils in snacks, causing rancidity. In a sealed potato chip bag flushed with nitrogen gas (N₂), oxygen is excluded so the chips stay fresh and uncrushed.\n\n> **Quick check:** If a bottle of cooking oil is left uncapped on a sunny windowsill all summer, will it turn rancid faster? ✅ Yes—oxygen, warmth, and sunlight all speed up the oxidation of oils.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Kelkar ne namkeen ke dabbe ki taraf ishara kiya. \"Is namkeen mein tel hai. Tel ya ghee jaise **fats** hawa ki oxygen se dheere-dheere **oxidise** hote hain. Isse naye chhote padarth bante hain jinki gandh aur swaad kharab hota hai. Ise **rancidity** kehte hain.\"\n\nDhyan do: ye wahi oxidation hai jo Topic 5 mein tha. Garmi, roshni aur nami isko tez kar dete hain.\n\n**Rancidity se bachne ke tareeke:**\n\n- **Airtight dabba:** hawa (oxygen) andar nahi jaati. Isliye dhakkan kas kar band karo.\n- **Fridge mein rakhna:** thandak se reaction dheema hota hai (Topic 4 ka rate wala niyam).\n- **Antioxidants:** khane mein milaye jaane wale chhote padarth, jo oxidation ko dheema karte hain.\n- **Nitrogen gas bharna:** chips ke packet mein hawa ki jagah nitrogen bhari jaati hai. Nitrogen bahut kam reactive hai, to oxidation nahi hota. Gas phoolkar packet ka kushan bhi banati hai, isliye chips toot nahi paate.\n\n```\n    Dheela dabba                  Nitrogen wala packet\n    (hawa andar aati hai)         (hawa ki jagah nitrogen)\n\n    +----------------+            ,-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~-.\n    |  O₂   O₂   O₂  |           (  N₂   N₂   N₂   N₂  )\n    |   chips / tel  |           (   N₂  chips  N₂     )\n    |  O₂  se oxidise|            ( N₂   N₂   N₂   N₂ )\n    +----------------+            '-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~-'\n\n    oxygen se tel ka               oxygen nahi, to\n    oxidation: baasi gandh         oxidation nahi: chips taaze\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – Dheele dabbe mein hawa ki oxygen tel ko oxidise karti hai (rancidity). Chips ke packet mein oxygen ki jagah nitrogen bhari hoti hai, isliye chips taaze rehte hain aur packet phoola dikhta hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Ghee ke dabbe ko dhoop mein khula chhod diya. Ghee jaldi baasi hoga. ✅ Hawa, roshni aur garmi teeno oxidation tez karte hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Cellular respiration: slow combustion inside our body",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Saans: sharir ke andar dheema jalna",
            "body": "Every time we eat carbohydrates (which our body digests into glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆) and breathe in oxygen, our **cells** carry out a controlled oxidation reaction that releases the **energy** we need to live:\n\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy\n\nAtom count: left has 6 C, 12 H, and 6 + 12 = 18 O; right has 6 C, 12 H, and 12 + 6 = 18 O ✅.\n\nChemists often describe **cellular respiration** as **slow combustion**: the fuel (glucose) reacts with oxygen to produce the exact same products as burning—CO₂, water, and energy—but **without flames or smoke**, step by step with the help of enzymes at a gentle 37 °C. That is why our body stays warm and why we breathe out carbon dioxide and water vapour!",
            "bodyHinglish": "Hum jo khana khate hain (jaise glucose), hamari **cells** mein oxygen ke saath dheere-dheere \"jalta\" hai aur sharir ko **energy** deta hai:\n\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy\n\nAtom count: baayein 6 C, 12 H, 6 + 12 = 18 O; daayein 6 C, 12 H, 12 + 6 = 18 O ✅.\n\nYe **respiration** hai, aur ise **slow combustion** (dheema jalna) bhi kaha ja sakta hai: fuel (glucose) oxygen ke saath CO₂, paani aur energy deta hai, bas **aag aur lau ke bina**, sharir ke taapman par, aur aaram se. Isliye saans lene par hum garam rehte hain aur CO₂ bahar chhodte hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Everyday reasoning examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Packaging ke reasoning examples",
            "body": "**Example 1: Why are potato chip bags flushed with nitrogen gas?**\nChips are fried in oil, which turns rancid if oxidised by oxygen in the air. Flushing the bag with unreactive nitrogen gas removes oxygen, preventing oxidation and cushioning the chips from breaking. ✅\n\n**Example 2: Why should cooking oil and butter be stored in airtight containers in a cool, dark place?**\nAirtight lids keep out oxygen, while cool temperatures and darkness slow down the rate of oxidation (Topic 4), keeping the fats fresh much longer.\n\n**Example 3: Why are kitchen sinks and cutlery made of stainless steel while cast-iron skillets are seasoned with oil?**\nStainless steel is an alloy of iron with chromium and nickel that resists rusting even in constant contact with water and salt. Plain cast iron would rust if left wet, so a thin baked-on film of oil is used to keep air and moisture away from the iron.\n\n**Example 4: How does galvanising protect an iron roof sheet even if the zinc layer gets a scratch?**\nZinc sits higher than iron on the reactivity ladder (Topic 3). Even if a scratch exposes both metals, the more reactive zinc oxidises first in place of the iron, protecting the iron from rusting.\n\n**Example 5: A student says, \"Aluminium oxide is just like rust—it destroys the aluminium pan.\" Is that right?**\nNo. Iron rust (Fe₂O₃) is flaky and peels off, exposing more iron to corrode. Aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) forms a tough, microscopic, waterproof skin that seals the aluminium underneath and **protects** it from further corrosion.\n\n**Example 6: Why must a gas water heater or charcoal grill never be used in an unventilated space?**\nIn a closed space, oxygen runs low and the fuel undergoes incomplete combustion, producing colourless, odourless, deadly carbon monoxide (CO) gas. Proper ventilation and outdoor exhausting are essential.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 1: Chips ke packet mein nitrogen kyun?**\nChips mein tel hai, jo oxygen se rancid hota hai. Nitrogen kam reactive hai, to oxygen hat gayi, oxidation ruka, chips taaze. ✅\n\n**Example 2: Ghee ka jar kaanch ka, airtight aur andhere shelf par kyun?**\nAirtight: oxygen nahi. Andhera aur thanda: roshni aur garmi oxidation tez karte hain. Isliye ghee der tak chalta hai.\n\n**Example 3: Rasoi ke bartan stainless steel ke kyun?**\nStainless steel ek alloy hai, jisme zang nahi lagti, aur roz paani, namak aur nami ke baad bhi sahi rehta hai. Aam loha ka tawa ya kadhai mein tel ki patli parat se zang rukti hai.\n\n**Example 4: Loha ki chaadar par zinc ki parat kyun?**\nZinc loha se zyada reactive hai. Parat chhil bhi jaaye to pehle zinc oxidise hota hai aur loha bachta hai. (Topic 3 ke reactivity series se milti baat.)\n\n**Example 5: Ek bacche ne kaha, \"Aluminium ka oxide bura hota hai, jaise loha ka zang.\"** Sahi ya galat?\nGalat. Zang corrosion hai, par aluminium ka oxide ek patli surakshit parat banata hai jo metal ko bachati hai.\n\n**Example 6: Band bathroom mein gas geyser ka CO ka khatra.**\nGeyser mein oxygen kam pade to gas adhuri jalti hai aur CO banti hai. CO bina rang-gandh ki zehreeli gas hai, to dikhti nahi. Isliye ventilation ya bahar ki taraf nikasi zaroori hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake 1: \"Fire only needs fuel and heat\"**\nFire needs all **three** corners of the fire triangle: fuel, heat, **and oxygen**. Without oxygen, a fuel cannot burn.\n\n**Mistake 2: \"Pour water on a grease or cooking-oil fire\"**\nNever! Water sinks under burning oil and vaporises explosively, splattering flaming oil everywhere. Turn off the burner and smother the pan with a metal lid or fire blanket to cut off oxygen.\n\n**Mistake 3: \"You can smell carbon monoxide if it builds up in a room\"**\nNo—carbon monoxide (CO) has **no smell, no taste, and no colour**. Good ventilation and CO alarms are the only protection.\n\n**Mistake 4: \"Rust is just dirty iron\"**\nRust (hydrated Fe₂O₃) is a completely **new chemical compound** formed when iron oxidises; it has lost the strength and metallic properties of iron.\n\n**Mistake 5: \"Water alone causes iron to rust\"**\nIron needs **both** water (moisture) **and** oxygen (air) to rust.\n\n**Mistake 6: \"Rancidity has nothing to do with chemical reactions\"**\nRancidity is the chemical **oxidation** of fats and oils by oxygen in the air.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Aag ke liye bas fuel aur garmi chahiye\"**\nTeen cheezein chahiye: fuel, oxygen **aur** garmi. Oxygen na ho to aag nahi jalegi.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Tel ki aag par paani daalna chahiye\"**\nNahi. Paani jalte tel ko bikher kar aag ko phailata hai. Dhakkan ya gili bori se oxygen roko, ya gas band karo.\n\n**Galti 3: \"CO ki gandh se pata chal jayega\"**\nCO ki koi gandh aur rang nahi hota. Ventilation hi bachaav hai.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Zang aur loha ek hi cheez hain\"**\nZang (Fe₂O₃) naya, kamzor padarth hai, loha ko ghata kar banta hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Zang sirf paani se lagti hai\"**\nPaani **aur** oxygen dono chahiye.\n\n**Galti 6: \"Rancidity matlab ghee ka kharab hona, kisi reaction ki wajah se nahi\"**\nYe oxygen se hone wala oxidation hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Combustion:** a fuel reacts with oxygen to release heat and light (CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O).\n- **Fire triangle:** fuel, oxygen, and heat. Removing any one of the three puts out the fire.\n- Limited oxygen causes **incomplete combustion**, producing **carbon monoxide (CO)**—a colourless, odourless, deadly gas. Never burn charcoal indoors, keep gas appliances vented, and make sure stove flames burn blue.\n- **Rusting:** iron + oxygen + moisture → flaky reddish-brown rust (simplified: 4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃). Prevented by painting, oiling/greasing, galvanising (zinc coating), plating, or alloying (stainless steel).\n- Green patina on copper, black tarnish (Ag₂S) on silver, and the protective Al₂O₃ film on aluminium are all forms of **corrosion**.\n- **Rancidity:** spoilage of fats and oils by slow oxidation in air. Prevented by airtight containers, refrigeration, cool/dark storage, antioxidants, and flushing snack bags with nitrogen gas (N₂).\n- **Cellular respiration:** slow, enzyme-controlled combustion inside our cells: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Combustion** mein fuel oxygen mein jal kar garmi aur roshni deta hai: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.\n- **Fire triangle:** fuel, oxygen, garmi. Inme se ek hatao to aag bujhti hai.\n- Oxygen kam ho to **adhura jalna** aur **CO** (zehreeli, rang-gandh rahit): band jagah mein aag ya geyser se hamesha ventilation rakho.\n- **Zang:** loha + hawa + nami → bhoora Fe₂O₃ (simplified: 4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃). Bachaav: paint, tel, galvanising, plating, stainless steel.\n- Taambe par hara coat, chaandi ka kaala hona, aluminium ki surakshit oxide parat: ye corrosion ke alag-alag roop hain.\n- **Rancidity:** tel-ghee ka hawa se oxidise hona. Bachaav: airtight dabba, fridge, antioxidants, chips mein nitrogen.\n- **Respiration:** sharir mein dheema jalna: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Define combustion. Write the balanced equation for the complete burning of methane.\n\n2. Name the three things needed for a fire (the fire triangle). Name which side of the triangle is removed in each case: (a) closing a gas valve, (b) covering burning oil in a pan with a lid, (c) pouring water on burning wood.\n\n3. Why should a burning pan of oil never be put out with water?\n\n4. What is incomplete combustion? Write one balanced equation for it using carbon. Why is carbon monoxide so dangerous?\n\n5. A family keeps a charcoal brazier burning in a closed room at night for warmth. Explain the danger and what should be done instead. State two other precautions at home to avoid CO poisoning.\n\n6. A gas flame is yellow and smoky instead of blue. What does this suggest, and what should the family do?\n\n7. What is rusting? What two things are needed? Write the simplified equation and check that it is balanced.\n\n8. Explain the experiment with three nails in three different jars. Which nail rusts most and why?\n\n9. Give four methods to prevent rusting, with one example each. Explain how galvanising protects iron even when the zinc layer is scratched.\n\n10. Explain each in one or two lines: (a) the green coating on copper, (b) the black coating on silver, (c) the oxide layer on aluminium. Which one protects the metal?\n\n11. What is rancidity? Why does a loosely closed snack jar smell stale after some weeks?\n\n12. Give three ways to prevent rancidity. Why is nitrogen flushed into potato chip packets?\n\n13. Why is respiration called slow combustion? Write its equation and verify it is balanced.\n\n14. State True or False, and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Carbon monoxide has a strong smell, so we can easily detect it.\n    (b) Rust is a new substance, not iron.\n    (c) The oxide layer of aluminium makes it corrode faster.\n    (d) Keeping cooking oil or butter in an airtight container in a cool, dark place slows rancidity.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Combustion** is a chemical reaction in which a fuel burns in oxygen, releasing heat and light. For methane: **CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.** Check: 1 C, 4 H, and 4 O on both sides ✅.\n\n2. The fire triangle: **fuel, oxygen, and heat.** (a) Closing a gas valve removes **fuel**. (b) Covering burning oil with a lid removes **oxygen**. (c) Pouring water on burning wood removes **heat** (cools it).\n\n3. Water sinks beneath hot oil and flashes into steam instantaneously, splattering burning oil droplets into the air and spreading the fire dangerously. Instead, turn off the heat and smother the pan with a metal lid or fire blanket to cut off oxygen.\n\n4. When oxygen is limited, carbon does not burn completely and forms carbon monoxide: **2C + O₂ → 2CO** (2 C and 2 O on both sides ✅). CO is a colourless, odourless, poisonous gas that binds to haemoglobin in the blood in place of oxygen, which can cause unconsciousness and death.\n\n5. In a closed room, oxygen is gradually used up, so the charcoal burns incompletely and releases invisible, odourless carbon monoxide (CO) that can poison sleeping occupants. Charcoal or coal braziers must never be burned indoors; use safe heating methods and ensure proper ventilation. Two other precautions: make sure gas water heaters/furnaces are properly vented to the outside, and keep kitchen gas burners clean so flames burn blue. If CO poisoning is suspected, move the person into fresh outdoor air immediately and call a doctor.\n\n6. A yellow, smoky flame indicates incomplete combustion (insufficient air mixing with the gas, often due to clogged burner ports). Have an adult get the burner cleaned and inspected by a technician, and ensure the kitchen is well ventilated.\n\n7. **Rusting** is the slow oxidation of iron into flaky reddish-brown iron(III) oxide. Two things are required: **oxygen (air) and water (moisture).** Simplified equation: **4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃.** Check: 4 Fe and 6 O on both sides ✅. (Actual rust is hydrated, meaning water molecules are also attached.)\n\n8. The nail in **Jar A** (exposed to both air and water) rusts the most because both required reactants are present. The nails in Jar B (boiled water under oil, no air) and Jar C (dry air, no moisture) do not rust. This proves that both air and water are necessary for rusting.\n\n9. Painting (iron gates); oiling or greasing (bicycle chains); galvanising with zinc (roofing sheets, buckets); tin or chromium plating (food cans, bicycle handlebars); alloying into stainless steel (cutlery, sinks). In galvanising, because zinc is more reactive than iron, the zinc oxidises first even if the coating is scratched, protecting the underlying iron.\n\n10. (a) Copper reacts slowly with oxygen, CO₂, and moisture in air to form a green basic carbonate patina. (b) Silver reacts with trace sulphur compounds (H₂S) in air to form black silver sulphide (Ag₂S). (c) Aluminium reacts with oxygen in air to form a thin, tough film of Al₂O₃. **Aluminium's oxide layer** seals and protects the metal underneath from further corrosion.\n\n11. **Rancidity** is the spoilage of fats and oils by slow oxidation in air, producing an unpleasant smell and taste. In a loosely closed jar, fresh oxygen keeps entering and oxidising the oil in the snacks over several weeks.\n\n12. Store in airtight containers, keep in the refrigerator (or a cool, dark place), add antioxidants, or flush packaging with nitrogen gas. Nitrogen (N₂) is unreactive and replaces oxygen inside the chip bag so the cooking oil cannot oxidise, while also cushioning the chips against breaking.\n\n13. In cellular respiration, glucose reacts slowly with oxygen inside our cells to produce CO₂, water, and energy—the exact same products as burning, but at body temperature (37 °C) without flames: **C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy.** Check: 6 C, 12 H, and 18 O on both sides ✅.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. Carbon monoxide is completely odourless and colourless, which is why it is so dangerous. (b) **True** ✅. Rust is a new compound (hydrated Fe₂O₃) with properties very different from iron. (c) **False** ❌. The tough Al₂O₃ layer protects aluminium from further corrosion. (d) **True** ✅. Excluding oxygen and keeping fats cool and out of light slows down oxidation.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Define combustion. Write the balanced equation for the complete burning of methane.\n\n2. Name the three things needed for a fire (the fire triangle). Name which side of the triangle is removed in each case: (a) closing a gas valve, (b) covering burning oil in a pan with a lid, (c) pouring water on burning wood.\n\n3. Why should a burning pan of oil never be put out with water?\n\n4. What is incomplete combustion? Write one balanced equation for it using carbon. Why is carbon monoxide so dangerous?\n\n5. A family keeps a coal sigri burning in a closed room at night. Explain the danger and what should be done instead. State two other precautions at home to avoid CO poisoning.\n\n6. A gas flame is yellow and smoky instead of blue. What does this suggest, and what should the family do?\n\n7. What is rusting? What two things are needed? Write the simplified equation and check that it is balanced.\n\n8. Explain the experiment with three nails in three different jars. Which nail rusts most and why?\n\n9. Give four methods to prevent rusting, with one example each. Explain how galvanising protects iron even when the zinc layer is scratched.\n\n10. Explain each in one or two lines: (a) the green coating on copper, (b) the black coating on silver, (c) the oxide layer on aluminium. Which one protects the metal?\n\n11. What is rancidity? Why does a loosely closed namkeen jar smell stale after some weeks?\n\n12. Give three ways to prevent rancidity. Why is nitrogen flushed into chips packets?\n\n13. Why is respiration called slow combustion? Write its equation and verify it is balanced.\n\n14. State True or False, and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Carbon monoxide has a strong smell, so we can easily detect it.\n    (b) Rust is a new substance, not iron.\n    (c) The oxide layer of aluminium makes it corrode faster.\n    (d) Keeping ghee in an airtight container in a cool, dark place slows rancidity.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Combustion** wo reaction hai jisme fuel oxygen mein jal kar garmi aur roshni deta hai. Methane ke liye: **CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.** Check: 1 C, 4 H, 4 O dono taraf ✅.\n\n2. Fire triangle: **fuel, oxygen, garmi.** (a) Gas valve band karna: **fuel** hataya. (b) Tel par dhakkan: **oxygen** hataya. (c) Lakdi par paani: **garmi** kam ki.\n\n3. Paani jalte tel mein girkar achanak bhaap banta hai aur jalta tel chheente ki tarah bikhar kar aag aur failata hai. Isliye dhakkan ya gili bori se oxygen roko, ya gas band karo.\n\n4. Oxygen kam ho to carbon poora nahi jalta aur CO banti hai: **2C + O₂ → 2CO** (2 C, 2 O dono taraf ✅). CO bina rang aur gandh ki zehreeli gas hai, jo khoon mein oxygen ki jagah le leti hai, isliye behoshi aur jaan ka khatra hai.\n\n5. Band kamre mein oxygen kam hoti hai, to sigri adhura jalti hai aur CO banti hai, jo dikhti nahi aur sote hue vyakti ko jaan ka khatra de sakti hai. Sigri band kamre mein kabhi mat jalao; kamra garam karne ke liye surakshit tareeka lo ya khidki-dwar se ventilation rakho. Do aur savdhaniyan: gas geyser ko ventilated jagah mein lagao aur chalate waqt hawa aane do; chulhe ki lau neeli rakho aur burner saaf rakho. CO ki zehar lage to turant khuli hawa mein le jao aur doctor ko bulao.\n\n6. Peeli, dhuaan wali lau ka matlab adhura jalna (oxygen kam ya burner band). Burner saaf karwao, ventilation badhao, aur kisi expert se jaanch karwao.\n\n7. **Zang** mein loha hawa aur nami se bhoora Fe₂O₃ ban jaata hai. Do cheezein chahiye: **oxygen (hawa) aur paani (nami).** Simplified: **4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃.** Check: 4 Fe, 6 O dono taraf ✅. (Asli zang mein paani bhi judta hai.)\n\n8. Jar A (hawa aur paani) mein keel sabse zyada zang khata hai, kyunki dono maujood hain. Jar B (bina hawa ka paani) aur Jar C (sukha, bina nami) mein kam ya nahi. Natija: zang ke liye hawa aur nami dono chahiye.\n\n9. Paint (gate); tel ya grease (cycle chain); galvanising (zinc ki parat, loha ki chaadar); tin ya chromium plating (dabbe, bumper); stainless steel alloy (bartan). Galvanising: zinc loha se zyada reactive hai, to scratch hone par bhi pehle zinc oxidise hota hai aur loha bachta hai.\n\n10. (a) Taambe par hawa ki oxygen, CO₂ aur nami se hara coat banta hai. (b) Chaandi hawa ki sulphur wali gas (H₂S) se judkar kaala Ag₂S banati hai. (c) Aluminium oxygen se judkar patli Al₂O₃ ki parat banata hai. **Aluminium ki parat** metal ko bachati hai (hara aur kaala coat nahi bachate).\n\n11. **Rancidity** tel ya ghee ke hawa ki oxygen se oxidise hone par gandh aur swaad ka kharab hona hai. Dheele dabbe mein oxygen andar aati rehti hai, isliye namkeen ka tel dheere-dheere oxidise hota hai aur baasi gandh aati hai.\n\n12. Airtight dabba, fridge, antioxidants (ya nitrogen bharna). Chips ke packet mein nitrogen kam reactive hai aur oxygen ko hata deti hai, to tel oxidise nahi hota aur chips taaze rehte hain (packet bhi phoola rehta hai, chips toot-te nahi).\n\n13. Respiration mein hamari cells mein glucose oxygen ke saath dheere-dheere jalta hai aur energy, CO₂ aur paani deta hai: **C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy.** Check: 6 C, 12 H, 18 O dono taraf ✅. Combustion jaisa, par bina lau ke aur sharir ke taapman par.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. CO ki koi gandh nahi hoti, isliye khatarnak hai. (b) **True** ✅. Zang Fe₂O₃ jaisa naya padarth hai. (c) **False** ❌. Oxide ki parat aluminium ko aage corrosion se bachati hai. (d) **True** ✅. Airtight, thanda aur andhera oxidation ko dheema karte hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary: Chemical Changes and Reactions",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter Ka Saar: Chemical Changes and Reactions",
            "body": "Here is our complete journey through this chapter at a glance—from what happens to chemical bonds during a reaction, through the four main reaction patterns, energy and rate, oxidation and reduction, and finally the chemistry happening around us every day:\n\n| Topic | What we learned | In one sentence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topic 1 | What a chemical reaction is | Old bonds in reactants break and new bonds form in products as atoms rearrange without any atom being created or destroyed, conserving mass. |\n| Topic 2 | Combination and decomposition | In combination, two or more substances join into one product (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O); in decomposition, one reactant breaks down into two or more products by heat, electricity, or light (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). |\n| Topic 3 | Displacement and double displacement | A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution (Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu) according to the reactivity ladder; in double displacement, two compounds swap ionic partners to form a precipitate or water. |\n| Topic 4 | Energy and rate of reaction | Exothermic reactions release heat while endothermic reactions absorb energy; reaction rate increases with higher temperature, higher concentration, larger surface area (smaller pieces), and catalysts (which are not used up). |\n| Topic 5 | Oxidation and reduction | Oxidation is gaining oxygen (or losing hydrogen) and reduction is losing oxygen (or gaining hydrogen); both always happen together in a redox reaction driven by oxidising and reducing agents. |\n| Topic 6 | Everyday chemistry | Combustion (the fire triangle and the hazard of CO), rusting and its prevention, corrosion of metals, rancidity of oils, and cellular respiration are all real-world oxidation processes. |\n\nDaniel tore open the nitrogen-flushed bag of potato chips and crunched into a crisp chip. \"Uncle, now I get it. That puffed-up bag of nitrogen isn't a trick—it's smart chemistry keeping oxygen away from the oil. And from now on, every snack jar in our pantry gets closed airtight!\" Mr. Parker smiled. \"Well done, Daniel. Now you can spot chemical reactions everywhere you look.\"\n\n---\n\nCongratulations! You have completed all 6 topics of **Chemical Changes and Reactions**: you can now explain how bonds break and form, classify reactions, predict displacements, reason about heat and reaction rates, trace redox transfers, and apply chemistry to everyday safety and food preservation.\n\n**What's next?** Up to now, we have seen how different elements bond and react. In the next chapter, **\"Periodic Table,\"** we will ask a grand question: with more than a hundred different elements showing so many varied behaviours, how did scientists organise all of them into a single, predictive chart?",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ek nazar mein poore chapter ka safar: reaction kya hai, uske prakaar, energy aur raftaar, oxidation-reduction, aur aaj roz ki zindagi mein inka asar.\n\n| Topic | Kya seekha | Ek line |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topic 1 | Chemical reaction kya hai | Reaction mein purane bond tootte hain aur naye bante hain, atoms sirf naye tareeke se jud jaate hain (na bante, na khatam hote), isliye mass wahi rehta hai; reactants aur products equation mein dikhte hain. |\n| Topic 2 | Combination aur decomposition | Combination mein do ya zyada padarth milkar ek banate hain (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O); decomposition mein ek padarth tootkar kai banta hai, garmi, bijli ya roshni se (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). |\n| Topic 3 | Displacement aur double displacement | Zyada reactive metal kam reactive ko uske salt ke ghol se nikalta hai (Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu), reactivity series se pehle hi andaaza; double displacement mein ions saathi badalte hain, aur precipitate ya paani banta hai. |\n| Topic 4 | Energy aur raftaar | Exothermic reaction garmi deta hai, endothermic leta hai; raftaar badhti hai garmi, concentration, chhote tukde aur catalyst se (catalyst khud khatam nahi hota). |\n| Topic 5 | Oxidation aur reduction | Oxidation matlab oxygen judna ya hydrogen nikalna, reduction ulta; dono saath hote hain (redox), aur oxidising aur reducing agent ye kaam karwate hain. |\n| Topic 6 | Roz ki chemistry | Jalna (fire triangle, CO ka khatra), zang aur uski roktham, dusre metals ka corrosion, rancidity aur respiration, sab oxidation ke hi roop hain. |\n\nAshwin ne chips ka packet khola aur ek chips khaya. \"Mama, ab main samajh gaya. Nitrogen ka phoola packet koi thagi nahi, chemistry ka kamaal hai. Aur ye namkeen? Ye ab bahar jayegi, aur agli baar dabba airtight.\" Mr. Kelkar muskuraye. \"Shabaash! Ab tumhe har jagah reaction dikhegi.\"\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Tumne chapter ke saare 6 topics poore kar liye: ab tum reaction ko pehchan sakte ho, uska type aur energy bata sakte ho, aur roz ki zindagi mein jalna, zang aur rancidity ko samajh sakte ho.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab tak humne dekha ki atoms kaise reaction karte hain. Agle chapter **\"Periodic Table\"** mein dekhenge ki itne saare tatva, jinke vyavhaar itne alag hain, kya ek vyavasthit chart mein sajaaye ja sakte hain?"
          }
