          {
            "heading": "Part A: Reading Everything from Coordinates (Position)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Position se sab kuch padho",
            "body": "We can synthesize all the principles of this chapter into a single, foolproof **6-step master recipe**. For any main-group element among the first 20 elements:\n\n1. **Write the electron configuration:** Distribute electrons across shells ($K, L, M, N$) following Bohr-Bury capacities ($2, 8, 8, 2$).\n2. **Find the Period (Row):** $\\mathbf{\\text{Period Number}} = \\text{Total number of occupied shells}$.\n3. **Find the Group (Column):**\n   - For 1 or 2 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Group 1 or Group 2**.\n   - For 3 to 8 valence electrons $\\rightarrow \\mathbf{\\text{Group}} = \\text{Valence electrons} + 10$ (Groups 13 to 18).\n   - *Helium exception:* 2 electrons in its complete $K$-shell $\\rightarrow$ **Group 18**.\n4. **Determine the Valency:**\n   - For 1 to 4 valence electrons: $\\text{Valency} = \\text{Valence electrons}$.\n   - For 5 to 7 valence electrons: $\\text{Valency} = 8 - \\text{Valence electrons}$.\n   - For noble gases (8 valence electrons, or 2 for He): $\\text{Valency} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n5. **Classify the Elemental Type:**\n   - Left side of the table: **Metals** (Group 1, Group 2, Aluminium in Group 13).\n   - Right side of the table: **Non-metals** (Groups 14 to 18, plus Hydrogen).\n   - Diagonal staircase boundary: **Metalloids** (**Boron [$\\text{B}$]** and **Silicon [$\\text{Si}$]**).\n6. **Predict the Chemical Bond Type:**\n   - $\\text{Metal} + \\text{Non-metal} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{\\text{Ionic Bond}}$ (electron transfer).\n   - $\\text{Non-metal} + \\text{Non-metal} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{\\text{Covalent Bond}}$ (electron sharing).\n\nLet us test this master recipe on two contrasting elements:\n\n### Case 1: Sulphur ($\\text{S}$, Atomic Number $Z = 16$)\n- Configuration: $K = 2, L = 8, M = 6 \\rightarrow \\mathbf{2, 8, 6}$.\n- Shell count: 3 occupied shells $\\rightarrow$ **Period 3**.\n- Group: 6 valence electrons $\\rightarrow 6 + 10 = \\mathbf{\\text{Group 16}}$.\n- Valency: $8 - 6 = \\mathbf{2}$.\n- Location: Right-hand side of staircase $\\rightarrow$ **Non-metal**.\n- Compound with Hydrogen: Non-metal + Non-metal $\\rightarrow$ **Covalent bond**. Applying criss-cross ($\\text{H}$ valency 1, $\\text{S}$ valency 2) yields **$\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ (Hydrogen Sulfide)**.\n\n### Case 2: Calcium ($\\text{Ca}$, Atomic Number $Z = 20$)\n- Configuration: $K = 2, L = 8, M = 8, N = 2 \\rightarrow \\mathbf{2, 8, 8, 2}$.\n- Shell count: 4 occupied shells $\\rightarrow$ **Period 4**.\n- Group: 2 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Group 2**.\n- Valency: $\\mathbf{2}$.\n- Location: Left-hand side $\\rightarrow$ **Alkaline earth metal**.\n- Compound with Chlorine: Metal + Non-metal $\\rightarrow$ **Ionic bond**. Applying criss-cross ($\\text{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Cl}^-$) yields **$\\text{CaCl}_2$ (Calcium Chloride)**.\n\n```\n   Group:    1    2      13   14   15   16   17   18\n            +---------------------------------------+\n   P1        H                                  He\n   P2        Li   Be     B    C    N    O    F    Ne\n   P3        Na   Mg     Al   Si   P    S    Cl   Ar\n   P4        K    Ca\n            +---------------------------------------+\n   Valency:  1    2      3    4    3    2    1    0\n   Type:     METALS (Left) ... METALLOIDS ... NON-METALS (Right)\n             (Boron and Silicon on the staircase; Al is metal; H is non-metal)\n\n   Reading Coordinates:\n     Row (Period)    = Number of occupied shells\n     Column (Group)  = Governed by valence electrons\n     Valency Cycle   = 1, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – The complete predictive master grid for the first 20 elements. Period coordinates indicate energy shells, while group coordinates dictate valence electrons, valency, elemental classification, and bonding behaviour.\n\nRemember: **you never need to memorize 118 random valency facts**. Once you know an element's position on the periodic grid, its entire chemical profile unfolds automatically.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter ke sab topics ko ek **recipe** mein jodte hain. Kisi bhi element ke liye (pehle 20 mein se):\n\n1. **Electron configuration** likho (K, L, M, N mein 2, 8, 8, 2 ke hisaab se).\n2. **Period** = shells ki ginti.\n3. **Group:** group 1 aur 2 mein group number = valence electrons; group 13 se 18 mein group number = valence electrons + 10 (helium 2 electron ke saath group 18 mein).\n4. **Valency:** valence electrons 4 tak ho to wahi; 4 se zyada ho to 8 minus valence electrons; noble gas ka 0.\n5. **Type:** table mein left side metal, right side non-metal, seedhi staircase par metalloid (B aur Si).\n6. **Bond:** metal + non-metal = ionic; non-metal + non-metal = covalent.\n\nDo udaharan dekhte hain.\n\n**Sulphur (S, atomic number 16):** configuration 2, 8, 6. Shells 3, to **period 3**. Valence electrons 6, to **group 16**. Valency 8 − 6 = **2**. Right side hai, to **non-metal**. Hydrogen se bond non-metal-non-metal, yaani **covalent**: H₂S (S ki valency 2, H ka 1, to 2 H chahiye).\n\n**Calcium (Ca, atomic number 20):** configuration 2, 8, 8, 2. Shells 4, **period 4**. Valence electrons 2, **group 2**, valency **2**, alkaline earth **metal**. Chlorine se bond metal-non-metal, yaani **ionic**: CaCl₂.\n\n```\n   Group:   1   2   13  14  15  16  17  18\n           +-------------------------------+\n   P1       H                           He\n   P2       Li  Be  B   C   N   O   F   Ne\n   P3       Na  Mg  Al  Si  P   S   Cl  Ar\n   P4       K   Ca\n           +-------------------------------+\n   Valency  1   2   3   4   3   2   1   0\n   Type     metal (left) ... metalloid ... non-metal (right)\n            (B aur Si staircase par, Al metal, H non-metal)\n\n   Position padho:  period = shells,  group = valence electrons\n                    valency = group ke hisaab se (1 2 3 4 3 2 1 0)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – Pehle 20 elements ka chhota table aur pattern ka recap: row (period) shells batati hai, column (group) valence electrons aur valency. Isi se type aur bond ka andaaza lagta hai.\n\nDhyan rakho, **is chapter ka sabse bada fayda** yehi hai: har element ki valency aur swabhav alag-alag ratna nahi padta, kyunki position hi sab kuch bata deti hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Magnesium (period 3, group 2) ki valency 1 hogi. ❌ Nahi, group 2 ka matlab 2 valence electrons, to valency **2**."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: The Clue Card Game",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Clue Card Game",
            "body": "You can play this tabletop deduction game at home using small index cards, paper, and a pencil: **completely safe, educational, and chemical-free!**\n\n```\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   |   CLUE CARD  No. 1                    |\n   |                                       |\n   |   Clue 1: I reside in Period 3.       |\n   |   Clue 2: I reside in Group 17.       |\n   |                                       |\n   |   Who am I?                           |\n   +---------------------------------------+\n                      |\n                      v\n   Period 3 Candidates:   Na  Mg  Al  Si  P  S  Cl  Ar\n   Group 17 Candidates:   F   Cl  Br  I\n   Intersection Match:    Chlorine (Cl)  [The ONLY element fitting both!]\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Mechanism of a Clue Card deduction. Clue 1 isolates the horizontal row (Period 3); Clue 2 isolates the vertical column (Group 17). The intersection pinpoints a single, unambiguous element: Chlorine ($\\text{Cl}$).\n\nHere are 15 mystery puzzles solved step by step. Each puzzle provides clues that eliminate all distractors to leave **exactly one correct answer**:\n\n- **Puzzle 1:** *\"I am in Period 3 and Group 17.\"*\n  - Period 3 candidates: $\\text{Na}$ to $\\text{Ar}$. Group 17 candidates: $\\text{F}, \\text{Cl}$. \n  - Intersection: **Chlorine ($\\text{Cl}$)**, a halogen non-metal.\n\n- **Puzzle 2:** *\"My electron configuration is $2, 8, 1$.\"*\n  - 3 shells (Period 3) + 1 valence electron (Group 1): **Sodium ($\\text{Na}$)**, an alkali metal.\n\n- **Puzzle 3:** *\"I am an alkaline earth metal with exactly 3 electron shells.\"*\n  - Alkaline earth metals (Group 2): $\\text{Be}$ (2 shells), $\\text{Mg}$ (3 shells), $\\text{Ca}$ (4 shells). \n  - Exactly 3 shells isolates: **Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 4:** *\"I am a noble gas with exactly 2 electron shells.\"*\n  - Noble gases (Group 18): $\\text{He}$ (1 shell), $\\text{Ne}$ (2 shells), $\\text{Ar}$ (3 shells). \n  - Exactly 2 shells isolates: **Neon ($\\text{Ne}$)**, famous for reddish-orange advertising signs.\n\n- **Puzzle 5:** *\"I am a metalloid with a valency of 4 residing in Period 3.\"*\n  - The metalloids among the first 20 elements are Boron ($\\text{B}$, Period 2, valency 3) and Silicon ($\\text{Si}$, Period 3, valency 4). \n  - Solution: **Silicon ($\\text{Si}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 6:** *\"I am a non-metal with a valency of 2 and possess 2 electron shells.\"*\n  - Period 2 non-metals: $\\text{C}$ (valency 4), $\\text{N}$ (3), $\\text{O}$ (2), $\\text{F}$ (1), $\\text{Ne}$ (0). \n  - Valency 2 isolates: **Oxygen ($\\text{O}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 7:** *\"I am an alkali metal with 4 electron shells.\"*\n  - Alkali metals (Group 1): $\\text{Li}$ (2 shells), $\\text{Na}$ (3 shells), $\\text{K}$ (4 shells). \n  - Solution: **Potassium ($\\text{K}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 8:** *\"I am a metal belonging to Group 13.\"*\n  - Group 13 contains Boron (a metalloid) and Aluminium (a metal). \n  - Solution: **Aluminium ($\\text{Al}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 9:** *\"I have only 1 electron shell, 1 valence electron, and I am a non-metal.\"*\n  - Period 1 has only $\\text{H}$ and $\\text{He}$. Helium has 2 electrons; Hydrogen has 1. \n  - Solution: **Hydrogen ($\\text{H}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 10:** *\"I am a non-metal in Period 3 with a chemical valency of 3.\"*\n  - In Period 3, elements with valency 3 are Aluminium (a metal) and Phosphorus (a non-metal: $8 - 5 = 3$). \n  - Non-metal isolates: **Phosphorus ($\\text{P}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 11:** *\"I am an element in Period 4 with a chemical valency of 2.\"*\n  - Period 4 elements among the first 20: Potassium (Group 1, valency 1) and Calcium (Group 2, valency 2). \n  - Solution: **Calcium ($\\text{Ca}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 12:** *\"I am a non-metal with 2 shells and a valency of 3.\"*\n  - Period 2 non-metals: $\\text{N}$ has configuration $2, 5$, giving valency $8 - 5 = 3$. \n  - Solution: **Nitrogen ($\\text{N}$)**.\n\n- **Puzzle 13:** *\"I reside in Period 2 and form four covalent bonds with hydrogen.\"*\n  - Period 2 element with valency 4 is Carbon ($Z = 6$, configuration $2, 4$). \n  - Solution: **Carbon ($\\text{C}$)** (forming $\\text{CH}_4$).\n\n- **Puzzle 14:** *\"I am an unreactive noble gas with 3 electron shells.\"*\n  - 3 shells in Group 18 indicates configuration $2, 8, 8$ ($Z = 18$). \n  - Solution: **Argon ($\\text{Ar}$)**, used inside light bulbs.\n\n- **Puzzle 15 (Formula Clue):** *\"I form an ionic chloride of the formula $\\text{XCl}_2$ and reside in Period 4.\"*\n  - Formula $\\text{XCl}_2$ requires $\\text{X}$ to have a valency of 2 ($\\text{X}^{2+}$). In Period 4, the divalent metal is Calcium. \n  - Solution: **Calcium ($\\text{Ca}$)**, forming $\\text{CaCl}_2$ ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ye khel aap bhi kheliye: paper ke chhote cards par clue likho aur doosre se element pakadne ko kaho. **Ye khel sirf kagaz aur pencil se hai, koi chemical nahi.**\n\n```\n   +-------------------------------+\n   |   CLUE CARD  No. 1            |\n   |                               |\n   |   Clue 1: Main period 3 mein  |\n   |           hoon.               |\n   |   Clue 2: Main group 17 mein  |\n   |           hoon.               |\n   |                               |\n   |   Main kaun hoon?             |\n   +-------------------------------+\n          |\n          v\n   Period 3 ki row:  Na Mg Al Si P S Cl Ar\n   Group 17 ka column: F Cl\n   Dono mein common:  Cl   -> Chlorine\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Ek clue card: pehle clue se row chuno, doosre se column; jahan dono milte hain wahi element hai.\n\nAb Samiksha ke solved puzzles. **Har puzzle ka sirf ek hi jawab nikalta hai** (hume dono clues se baaki sab hata kar ek bachana hai).\n\n**Puzzle 1.** Period 3 aur group 17.\nPeriod 3 mein group 17 wala sirf ek: **Cl** (chlorine). Ye halogen hai.\n\n**Puzzle 2.** Meri electron configuration 2, 8, 1 hai.\nTeen shells to period 3, 1 valence electron to group 1: **Na** (sodium), alkali metal.\n\n**Puzzle 3.** Main alkaline earth metal hoon aur mere paas 3 shells hain.\nGroup 2 ke elements: Be (2 shells), Mg (3 shells), Ca (4 shells). 3 shells wala: **Mg** (magnesium).\n\n**Puzzle 4.** Main noble gas hoon aur mere paas sirf 2 shells hain.\nNoble gas: He (1 shell), Ne (2 shells), Ar (3 shells). Do shells wala: **Ne** (neon). Signboard ki laal roshni wala.\n\n**Puzzle 5.** Main metalloid hoon, valency 4, aur period 3 mein hoon.\nMetalloids B aur Si hain. B ki valency 3, period 2; Si ki valency 4, period 3. To **Si** (silicon).\n\n**Puzzle 6.** Main non-metal hoon, valency 2, aur mere paas 2 shells hain.\n2 shells (period 2) non-metals: C (4), N (3), O (2), F (1), Ne (0). Valency 2: **O** (oxygen).\n\n**Puzzle 7.** Main alkali metal hoon aur mere paas 4 shells hain.\nAlkali metals (pehle 20 mein): Li (2 shells), Na (3), K (4). **K** (potassium).\n\n**Puzzle 8.** Main group 13 ka metal hoon.\nGroup 13: B (metalloid) aur Al (metal). Metal wala: **Al** (aluminium).\n\n**Puzzle 9.** Mere paas sirf 1 shell hai, 1 valence electron, aur main non-metal hoon.\n1 shell: H aur He. 1 valence electron wala: **H** (hydrogen). (He ke 2 electron hain.)\n\n**Puzzle 10.** Main non-metal hoon, valency 3, aur period 3 mein hoon.\nPeriod 3 mein valency 3: Al aur P. Al metal hai, to non-metal: **P** (phosphorus).\n\n**Puzzle 11.** Main valency 2 wala element hoon, period 4 mein.\nPeriod 4 mein sirf K aur Ca hain. K ki valency 1, Ca ka 2: **Ca** (calcium).\n\n**Puzzle 12.** Main non-metal hoon, valency 3, aur mere paas 2 shells hain.\nPeriod 2, valency 3: B (metalloid) aur N. Non-metal: **N** (nitrogen).\n\n**Puzzle 13.** Main period 2 mein hoon aur valency 4 hai.\nPeriod 2 mein valency 4 sirf C ka: **C** (carbon).\n\n**Puzzle 14.** Main noble gas hoon aur mere paas 3 shells hain.\n**Ar** (argon). Bulb ke andar bhari gas.\n\n**Puzzle 15 (formula wala).** Main chlorine ke saath XCl₂ jaisa ionic compound banata hoon, aur period 4 mein hoon.\nXCl₂ ka matlab X ki valency 2. Period 4 mein valency 2: **Ca**. CaCl₂ ✅.\n\nSamiksha ne har jawab ke saath khud jaanch bhi ki. \"Papa, dekho, har puzzle mein pehle clue se kuch elements gire, doosre clue se aur, aur aakhir mein ek bacha. Bilkul Guess Who? jaisa.\"\n\n> **Jaanch:** \"Main alkali metal hoon\" sirf is ek clue se element pakda ja sakta hai. ❌ Nahi, Li, Na aur K teeno alkali metals hain. Doosra clue (shells ki ginti) zaroori hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Deducing Formulas and Bond Types from Position",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Position se formula aur bond",
            "body": "When two elements combine, their table positions immediately reveal:\n1. **Bond Type:** Metal + Non-metal forms an **ionic bond**; Non-metal + Non-metal forms a **covalent bond**.\n2. **Formula Subscripts:** Determined by cross-multiplying their periodic valencies and simplifying to the lowest whole-number ratio.\n\n| Combining Pair | Elemental Valencies | Resulting Formula | Bond Type | Underlying Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **$\\text{Na}$ and $\\text{Cl}$** | $\\text{Na} = 1, \\text{Cl} = 1$ | **$\\text{NaCl}$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Na}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{Cl}$) |\n| **$\\text{Mg}$ and $\\text{Cl}$** | $\\text{Mg} = 2, \\text{Cl} = 1$ | **$\\text{MgCl}_2$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Mg}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{Cl}$) |\n| **$\\text{Ca}$ and $\\text{Cl}$** | $\\text{Ca} = 2, \\text{Cl} = 1$ | **$\\text{CaCl}_2$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Ca}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{Cl}$) |\n| **$\\text{Li}$ and $\\text{F}$** | $\\text{Li} = 1, \\text{F} = 1$ | **$\\text{LiF}$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Li}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{F}$) |\n| **$\\text{Mg}$ and $\\text{O}$** | $\\text{Mg} = 2, \\text{O} = 2$ | **$\\text{MgO}$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Mg}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{O}$) [Ratio $2:2 \\rightarrow 1:1$] |\n| **$\\text{Al}$ and $\\text{O}$** | $\\text{Al} = 3, \\text{O} = 2$ | **$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{Al}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{O}$) |\n| **$\\text{K}$ and $\\text{O}$** | $\\text{K} = 1, \\text{O} = 2$ | **$\\text{K}_2\\text{O}$** | Ionic | Metal ($\\text{K}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{O}$) |\n| **$\\text{H}$ and $\\text{Cl}$** | $\\text{H} = 1, \\text{Cl} = 1$ | **$\\text{HCl}$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{H}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{Cl}$) |\n| **$\\text{H}$ and $\\text{O}$** | $\\text{H} = 1, \\text{O} = 2$ | **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{H}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{O}$) |\n| **$\\text{N}$ and $\\text{H}$** | $\\text{N} = 3, \\text{H} = 1$ | **$\\text{NH}_3$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{N}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{H}$) |\n| **$\\text{C}$ and $\\text{H}$** | $\\text{C} = 4, \\text{H} = 1$ | **$\\text{CH}_4$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{C}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{H}$) |\n| **$\\text{H}$ and $\\text{S}$** | $\\text{H} = 1, \\text{S} = 2$ | **$\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{H}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{S}$) |\n| **$\\text{C}$ and $\\text{Cl}$** | $\\text{C} = 4, \\text{Cl} = 1$ | **$\\text{CCl}_4$** | Covalent | Non-metal ($\\text{C}$) + Non-metal ($\\text{Cl}$) |\n\nNotice the remarkable family harmony:\n- Magnesium chloride is $\\text{MgCl}_2$, and Calcium chloride is $\\text{CaCl}_2$ (both Group 2 metals require two halogens).\n- Sodium chloride is $\\text{NaCl}$, and Potassium chloride is $\\text{KCl}$ (both Group 1 metals require one halogen).\n- Water is $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$, and Hydrogen sulfide is $\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ (both Group 16 non-metals require two hydrogens).\n\n### Formula Mass Calculations\nUsing standard atomic masses ($\\text{Mg} = 24, \\text{Cl} = 35.5, \\text{Ca} = 40, \\text{Al} = 27, \\text{O} = 16, \\text{C} = 12, \\text{H} = 1, \\text{K} = 39$):\n- **$\\text{MgCl}_2$:** $24 + 2(35.5) = 24 + 71 = \\mathbf{95\\text{ u}}$\n- **$\\text{CaCl}_2$:** $40 + 2(35.5) = 40 + 71 = \\mathbf{111\\text{ u}}$\n- **$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$:** $2(27) + 3(16) = 54 + 48 = \\mathbf{102\\text{ u}}$\n\n### Electronic Mechanism of Ionic Bond Formation in $\\text{MgCl}_2$\nMagnesium ($2, 8, 2$) possesses 2 valence electrons in its outer $M$-shell. To attain the stable electron configuration of Neon ($2, 8$), it donates both electrons:\n$$\\text{Mg} \\longrightarrow \\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^-$$\nEach of two Chlorine atoms ($2, 8, 7$) requires 1 electron to complete its outer octet ($2, 8, 8$, resembling Argon):\n$$2\\text{Cl} + 2e^- \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Cl}^-$$\nThe resulting $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$ cation and two $\\text{Cl}^-$ anions are held firmly together by omnidirectional electrostatic attraction into a crystalline lattice of **$\\text{MgCl}_2$** ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Cross valency ka niyam: ek element ki valency doosre ke atoms ki ginti ban jaati hai, phir common factor se chhota karte hain. Bond ka type metal aur non-metal dekh kar.\n\n| Jodi | Valency | Formula | Bond | Kyun |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Na aur Cl | 1 aur 1 | NaCl | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| Mg aur Cl | 2 aur 1 | MgCl₂ | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| Ca aur Cl | 2 aur 1 | CaCl₂ | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| Li aur F | 1 aur 1 | LiF | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| Mg aur O | 2 aur 2 | MgO | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| Al aur O | 3 aur 2 | Al₂O₃ | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| K aur O | 1 aur 2 | K₂O | ionic | metal + non-metal |\n| H aur Cl | 1 aur 1 | HCl | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n| H aur O | 1 aur 2 | H₂O | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n| N aur H | 3 aur 1 | NH₃ | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n| C aur H | 4 aur 1 | CH₄ | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n| H aur S | 1 aur 2 | H₂S | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n| C aur Cl | 4 aur 1 | CCl₄ | covalent | non-metal + non-metal |\n\nEk mazedaar baat: **ek hi group ke elements ke formula same dhang ke hote hain.** Mg ke saath Cl: MgCl₂, to Ca ke saath bhi CaCl₂. Na ke saath Cl: NaCl, to K ke saath bhi KCl. O ke saath H: H₂O, to S ke saath H₂S. Yehi parivaar wali baat hai (Topic 5).\n\nYe formula mass bhi nikal sakte hain (Chapter \"Atoms and Molecules\" ke atomic masses se: Mg 24, Cl 35.5, Ca 40, Al 27, O 16):\n\n- MgCl₂ = 24 + 2 × 35.5 = 24 + 71 = **95**\n- CaCl₂ = 40 + 71 = **111**\n- Al₂O₃ = 2 × 27 + 3 × 16 = 54 + 48 = **102**\n\nIonic bond mein electron kaise jaate hain, ek udaharan: Mg (2, 8, 2) ke 2 valence electron chhodta hai, to Mg ke paas 2, 8 bachte hain (Ne jaisa). Do Cl (2, 8, 7) har ek 1 electron leta hai, to 2, 8, 8 (Ar jaisa). Isliye 1 Mg ke saath 2 Cl: MgCl₂ ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Mixed Multi-Chapter Reasoning",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Mixed reasoning (sab chapters ek saath)",
            "body": "### Example 1: Full Profile of Element $Z = 12$\nConstruct the complete chemical profile of an element with atomic number $Z = 12$.\n- Configuration: $K = 2, L = 8, M = 2 \\rightarrow \\mathbf{2, 8, 2}$.\n- Coordinates: 3 shells $\\rightarrow$ **Period 3**; 2 valence electrons $\\rightarrow$ **Group 2**.\n- Identity: **Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)**, an alkaline earth metal.\n- Valency: **2**.\n- Reaction with Chlorine: Forms ionic **$\\text{MgCl}_2$** with formula mass $24 + 71 = 95\\text{ u}$ ✅.\n\n### Example 2: Element $Z = 16$ and Its Hydrogen Compound\nAn element has atomic number $Z = 16$. Deduce its properties and compound with hydrogen.\n- Configuration: $2, 8, 6 \\rightarrow$ Period 3, Group 16.\n- Identity: **Sulphur ($\\text{S}$)**, a non-metal with valency $8 - 6 = \\mathbf{2}$.\n- With Hydrogen (valency 1): Forms covalent **$\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ (Hydrogen Sulfide)**.\n- Formula mass: $2(1) + 32 = \\mathbf{34\\text{ u}}$ ✅.\n\n### Example 3: Reaction Between Lithium and Fluorine\nPredict the compound formed between Lithium and Fluorine.\n- Lithium ($Z = 3$, $2, 1$): Period 2, Group 1 metal (valency 1).\n- Fluorine ($Z = 9$, $2, 7$): Period 2, Group 17 non-metal (valency 1).\n- Electron transfer forms an ionic bond: **$\\text{LiF}$ (Lithium Fluoride)**.\n- Formula mass: $7 + 19 = \\mathbf{26\\text{ u}}$ ✅.\n\n### Example 4: Compound of Aluminium and Oxygen\nExplain the formula of the oxide formed by Aluminium.\n- Aluminium ($Z = 13$, $2, 8, 3$) has valency 3. Oxygen ($Z = 8$, $2, 6$) has valency 2.\n- Applying criss-cross: $\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$.\n- Two $\\text{Al}^{3+}$ ions surrender 6 electrons, which are accepted by three $\\text{O}^{2-}$ ions.\n- The bond is **ionic**, and the formula is **$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$** ✅.\n\n### Example 5: Finding the Odd One Out\nIdentify the odd one out among $\\text{Ne}, \\text{Ar}, \\text{Na}$, and $\\text{He}$. Justify your choice.\n- $\\text{Ne}, \\text{Ar}$, and $\\text{He}$ are chemically inert noble gases belonging to Group 18 with valency 0.\n- $\\text{Na}$ is a fiercely reactive alkali metal belonging to Group 1 with valency 1.\n- The odd one out is **Sodium ($\\text{Na}$)** ✅.\n\n### Example 6: Comparing Reactivity: $\\text{Na}$ vs. $\\text{K}$\nWhich element is more reactive: Sodium or Potassium?\n- Both belong to Group 1. Potassium has 4 shells while Sodium has 3.\n- Potassium's valence electron is farther from the nucleus and shielded by more inner shells, making it easier to lose.\n- Therefore, **Potassium is more reactive than Sodium ($\\text{K} > \\text{Na}$)** ✅.\n\n### Example 7: Halogen Reactivity: $\\text{F}$ vs. $\\text{Cl}$\nWhich halogen is more reactive: Fluorine or Chlorine?\n- In Group 17, reactivity depends on attracting an incoming electron.\n- Fluorine has only 2 shells; its nucleus is closer to the valence shell, pulling incoming electrons with much greater intensity.\n- Therefore, **Fluorine is more reactive than Chlorine ($\\text{F} > \\text{Cl}$)** ✅.\n\n### Example 8: Predicting Properties of Undiscovered Element Rubidium ($\\text{Rb}$)\nRubidium sits directly below Potassium in Group 1 with 5 electron shells. Predict its properties without prior study.\n- Valency: **1**.\n- Physical nature: Soft, silvery metal with very low density.\n- Reactivity: Reacts even more violently with water than Potassium.\n- Chloride formula: **$\\text{RbCl}$**.\n- This is the exact predictive methodology used by Mendeleev ✅.\n\n### Example 9: Deducing Identity of Element $X$\nAn unknown element $X$ has a valency of 3, 3 occupied shells, and is a metal.\n- 3 occupied shells $\\rightarrow$ Period 3. Valency 3 metal $\\rightarrow$ Group 13.\n- The element is **Aluminium ($\\text{Al}$)**. Its oxide is $\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$ ✅.\n\n### Example 10: Nature of Bonding in Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$)\nWhat type of chemical bonding exists in water?\n- Both Hydrogen and Oxygen are non-metals.\n- Non-metals share electrons rather than transferring them, forming **covalent bonds**.\n- Two Hydrogen atoms share electrons with one Oxygen atom: $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 1: Atomic number 12 wale element ki poori kundli.**\nConfiguration 2, 8, 2. Period 3 (3 shells), group 2 (2 valence electrons), valency 2, metal (magnesium). Chlorine ke saath MgCl₂, ionic. Kyunki Mg metal aur Cl non-metal hain. Mass 95 ✅.\n\n**Example 2: Atomic number 16 aur hydrogen.**\nConfiguration 2, 8, 6, period 3, group 16, valency 2 (8 − 6), non-metal (sulphur). Formula H₂S, bond covalent (dono non-metal), formula mass 2 × 1 + 32 = 34 ✅.\n\n**Example 3: Lithium aur fluorine.**\nLi: 2, 1 (period 2, group 1). F: 2, 7 (period 2, group 17). Li ek electron deta hai, F ek leta hai: LiF, ionic, mass 7 + 19 = 26 ✅.\n\n**Example 4: Aluminium aur oxygen.**\nAl: 2, 8, 3, valency 3. O: 2, 6, valency 2. Cross valency se Al₂O₃. Check: 2 Al ke 6 electron nikalte hain, 3 O 6 electron lete hain ✅. Metal aur non-metal, to ionic.\n\n**Example 5: Chaar elements mein odd one out.** Ne, Ar, Na, He mein alag kaun?\nNe, Ar, He noble gases (group 18) hain, Na alkali metal (group 1). Odd one: **Na**.\n\n**Example 6: Na aur K mein zyada reactive kaun?**\nDono group 1 mein hain. K ke 4 shells, Na ke 3. K ka baahari electron nucleus se zyada door, to aasani se nikalta hai: **K zyada reactive** (Topic 4 aur 5).\n\n**Example 7: F aur Cl mein zyada reactive halogen kaun?**\nHalogen mein reactivity group mein neeche jaate hue ghatti hai, to **F zyada reactive**.\n\n**Example 8: Table ki bhavishyavani: Rb ke baare mein.**\nRubidium (Rb) group 1 mein, K ke theek neeche aata hai (5 shells). Bina padhe hum bata sakte hain: valency 1, soft, chaandi jaisa, paani ke saath K se bhi zyada tez reaction, formula RbCl. Hum ye sirf padhne ke liye keh rahe hain, kisi ko reaction karna nahi hai. Mendeleev ne bhi isi tarah gallium ki bhavishyavani ki thi (Topic 2).\n\n**Example 9: Ek anjaan element X ki valency 3 hai, 3 shells, aur ek metal hai.**\nGroup 13 (valency 3), period 3: **Al**. Oxygen ke saath Al₂O₃, ionic.\n\n**Example 10: Kya hum bata sakte hain ki H₂O mein bond kaisa hai?**\nH aur O dono non-metal, to covalent. Aur formula: H ki valency 1, O ki 2, to 2 H + 1 O = H₂O ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Misidentifying Period 3, Group 1 as Lithium instead of Sodium.**\n   Lithium sits in Period 2 (2 shells). Period 3, Group 1 is **Sodium ($\\text{Na}$)** (3 shells). Always count shell rows carefully.\n\n2. **Mistake 2: Stating Group 17 elements have a valency of 7.**\n   Group 17 elements have 7 valence electrons, but their combining capacity (valency) is $8 - 7 = \\mathbf{1}$.\n\n3. **Mistake 3: Assuming valency always equals valence electrons.**\n   Valency equals valence electrons only up to 4 (Groups 1, 2, 13, 14). For Groups 15, 16, and 17, valency equals $8 - \\text{valence electrons}$ (3, 2, and 1 respectively).\n\n4. **Mistake 4: Believing a single clue is always sufficient to isolate an element.**\n   A single clue like \"I am an alkali metal\" leaves $\\text{Li}, \\text{Na}$, and $\\text{K}$ as possibilities. You must cross-reference a second coordinate (such as shell count or period) to isolate a single element.\n\n5. **Mistake 5: Classifying metal-nonmetal bonds as covalent.**\n   Metals transfer electrons to non-metals, forming **ionic bonds**. Covalent bonds form exclusively between non-metals through electron sharing.\n\n6. **Mistake 6: Classifying Hydrogen as an alkali metal.**\n   Hydrogen sits at the top of Group 1 for valence electron consistency, but it is a diatomic non-metal gas.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Period 3, group 1 wala element Li hoga\"**\nLi period 2 mein hai. Period 3 aur group 1: Na.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Group 17 ki valency 7 hai\"**\nGroup 17 mein 7 valence electrons hain, par valency 8 − 7 = **1**.\n\n**Galti 3: \"Valency hamesha valence electrons ke barabar hota hai\"**\nSirf 4 valence electrons tak. 4 se zyada hon to valency = 8 minus valence electrons (aur noble gas ki 0).\n\n**Galti 4: \"Ek clue se hi element mil jaata hai\"**\nKai elements ek group ya period mein hote hain. Do clue lagte hain.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Metal aur non-metal ka bond covalent hota hai\"**\nMetal + non-metal = ionic. Non-metal + non-metal = covalent.\n\n**Galti 6: \"Hydrogen group 1 ka metal hai\"**\nHydrogen non-metal gas hai. Convenience ke liye group 1 ke upar rakha gaya hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Reading coordinates:**\n  - $\\text{Period number} = \\text{number of occupied electron shells}$.\n  - $\\text{Group number} = \\text{dictated by valence electrons}$ (Groups 1, 2, and 13–18).\n  - Main-group valency progression: $1, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0$.\n- **Geography of the table:** Metals dominate the left; non-metals dominate the right; Boron and Silicon are metalloids lining the staircase.\n- **Predicting bonds:**\n  - Metal + Non-metal $\\rightarrow$ **Ionic bond** (transfer of electrons).\n  - Non-metal + Non-metal $\\rightarrow$ **Covalent bond** (sharing of electrons).\n- **Formulas:** Determined via the criss-cross valency method ($\\text{MgCl}_2, \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3, \\text{CH}_4$). Elements within the same group produce identical compound formulas ($\\text{NaCl}$ and $\\text{KCl}$; $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ and $\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$).\n- **The Clue Card Method:** Cross-referencing two independent structural coordinates uniquely isolates any element on the periodic table.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Table padhne ka tarika: configuration, period = shells, group = valence electrons.\n- Valency: 1, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0 (group 1, 2, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18).\n- Metal left mein, non-metal right mein, B aur Si staircase par.\n- Metal + non-metal: ionic; non-metal + non-metal: covalent.\n- Cross valency se formula: MgCl₂, Al₂O₃, NH₃, CH₄.\n- Ek group ke elements ke formula same dhang ke hote hain (NaCl aur KCl).\n- Clue card puzzle mein do clue chahiye, taaki sirf ek jawab bache."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. An element has an electron configuration of $2, 5$. Determine its period, group, valency, elemental classification (metal or non-metal), and write the formula of its compound with hydrogen.\n\n2. Solve the clue card: *\"I reside in Period 3 and belong to Group 2.\"*\n\n3. Solve the clue card: *\"I am a non-metallic gas, I have 2 electron shells, and my chemical valency is 1.\"*\n\n4. Solve the clue card: *\"I have 3 valence electrons, 3 occupied shells, and I am a metal.\"*\n\n5. Solve the clue card: *\"I am an unreactive noble gas possessing only 1 electron shell.\"*\n\n6. Solve the clue card: *\"I am an alkali metal possessing exactly 2 electron shells.\"*\n\n7. Solve the clue card: *\"My chemical valency is 2, I reside in Period 3, and I am classified as a non-metal.\"*\n\n8. Explain why the single clue *\"I am an alkaline earth metal\"* is insufficient to identify a unique element among the first 20 elements. Provide one additional clue that resolves the ambiguity.\n\n9. Write the chemical formula and bond type for the compound formed between:\n   (a) Calcium and Chlorine\n   (b) Aluminium and Oxygen\n   (c) Carbon and Hydrogen\n   (d) Potassium and Oxygen\n   Calculate the formula mass of compounds (c) and (d).\n\n10. Using electron configurations, explain step by step how Calcium Chloride ($\\text{CaCl}_2$) is formed.\n\n11. Strontium ($\\text{Sr}$) sits directly below Calcium in Group 2. Predict its chemical valency, compare its reactivity with Calcium, and predict the formula of its chloride salt.\n\n12. Between Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$) and Calcium ($\\text{Ca}$), which element is more reactive, and which possesses more electron shells? Provide a concise explanation.\n\n13. State the period and group coordinates of the element with atomic number $Z = 19$, and predict the formula of its oxide.\n\n14. State whether each statement is True or False. Correct any false statements:\n    (a) Elements belonging to Group 17 have a chemical valency of 7.\n    (b) Ammonia ($\\text{NH}_3$) is a covalent compound.\n    (c) Sodium and Chlorine combine to form a covalent compound.\n    (d) Elements belonging to the same vertical group possess identical chemical valencies.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Configuration $2, 5$: 2 shells $\\rightarrow$ **Period 2**. 5 valence electrons $\\rightarrow 5 + 10 = \\mathbf{\\text{Group 15}}$. Valency $= 8 - 5 = \\mathbf{3}$. Located on the right-hand side $\\rightarrow$ **Non-metal** (Nitrogen, $Z = 7$). With Hydrogen (valency 1), criss-cross yields **$\\text{NH}_3$ (Ammonia)**, which is **covalent** (non-metal + non-metal) ✅.\n\n2. Period 3 represents elements from $\\text{Na}$ to $\\text{Ar}$. Group 2 indicates 2 valence electrons. Intersection: **Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)**.\n\n3. 2 shells (Period 2) non-metal gas with valency 1 ($8 - 7 = 1$ in Group 17): **Fluorine ($\\text{F}$)**.\n\n4. 3 valence electrons and 3 occupied shells: Period 3, Group 13. The metallic element is **Aluminium ($\\text{Al}$)**.\n\n5. Noble gas with 1 shell: **Helium ($\\text{He}$)** ($K = 2$). (Hydrogen is not a noble gas.)\n\n6. Alkali metal (Group 1) with 2 shells: **Lithium ($\\text{Li}$)** (configuration $2, 1$).\n\n7. In Period 3, elements with valency 2 are Magnesium (metal) and Sulphur ($8 - 6 = 2$, non-metal). Non-metal requirement isolates: **Sulphur ($\\text{S}$)**.\n\n8. Among the first 20 elements, Group 2 contains three distinct alkaline earth metals: Beryllium ($\\text{Be}$), Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$), and Calcium ($\\text{Ca}$). Adding the clue *\"I possess exactly 3 electron shells\"* uniquely isolates **Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)**.\n\n9. \n   (a) **$\\text{CaCl}_2$**, Ionic bond (Metal + Non-metal).\n   (b) **$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$**, Ionic bond (Metal + Non-metal).\n   (c) **$\\text{CH}_4$**, Covalent bond (Non-metal + Non-metal). Formula mass: $12 + 4(1) = \\mathbf{16\\text{ u}}$.\n   (d) **$\\text{K}_2\\text{O}$**, Ionic bond (Metal + Non-metal, $\\text{K}$ valency 1, $\\text{O}$ valency 2). Formula mass: $2(39) + 16 = 78 + 16 = \\mathbf{94\\text{ u}}$ ✅.\n\n10. Calcium ($2, 8, 8, 2$) surrenders its 2 valence electrons to achieve an Argon configuration ($2, 8, 8$), forming $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$. Two Chlorine atoms ($2, 8, 7$) each accept 1 electron to complete their octet ($2, 8, 8$), forming two $\\text{Cl}^-$ anions. Strong electrostatic attraction binds them into ionic **$\\text{CaCl}_2$**.\n\n11. Strontium belongs to Group 2, so its **valency is 2**. Because it sits lower in Group 2 with 5 shells, its valence electrons are farther from the nucleus and lost more easily, making **Strontium more reactive than Calcium**. Its chloride salt has the formula **$\\text{SrCl}_2$** (identical to $\\text{CaCl}_2$).\n\n12. **Calcium is more reactive than Magnesium**, and Calcium possesses **4 shells** (Magnesium has 3). Moving down the group adds a shell, placing outer electrons farther from the nucleus and weakening the nuclear hold, allowing them to be lost with less energy.\n\n13. $Z = 19$ is Potassium ($\\text{K}$): configuration $2, 8, 8, 1 \\rightarrow$ **Period 4, Group 1**. Valency is 1; Oxygen valency is 2. Criss-cross yields **$\\text{K}_2\\text{O}$ (Potassium Oxide)**.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. Group 17 elements possess 7 valence electrons, but their combining valency is $8 - 7 = \\mathbf{1}$.\n    (b) **True** ✅. Nitrogen and Hydrogen are both non-metals that share electron pairs.\n    (c) **False** ❌. Sodium (metal) and Chlorine (non-metal) form an **ionic** compound ($\\text{NaCl}$) via electron transfer.\n    (d) **True** ✅. Elements within a main group share identical valence electron counts, producing identical valencies.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. An element has the electron configuration 2, 5. Find its period, group, valency, whether it is a metal or non-metal, and write its compound with hydrogen.\n\n2. Solve the clue card: \"I am in period 3 and in group 2.\"\n\n3. Solve the clue card: \"I am a non-metal gas, I have 2 shells and my valency is 1.\"\n\n4. Solve the clue card: \"I have 3 valence electrons and 3 shells, and I am a metal.\"\n\n5. Solve the clue card: \"I am a noble gas with 1 shell.\"\n\n6. Solve the clue card: \"I am an alkali metal with 2 shells.\"\n\n7. Solve the clue card: \"My valency is 2, I am in period 3, and I am a non-metal.\"\n\n8. Why does the clue \"I am an alkaline earth metal\" alone not give a single answer among the first 20 elements? Give one more clue to fix it.\n\n9. Write the formula and bond type of the compound between: (a) Ca and Cl, (b) Al and O, (c) C and H, (d) K and O. Calculate the formula mass of (c) and (d).\n\n10. Using the electron configurations, explain how CaCl₂ is formed.\n\n11. Strontium (Sr) comes just below calcium in Group 2. Predict its valency, how its reactivity compares with calcium and the formula of its chloride.\n\n12. Between Mg and Ca, which one is more reactive, and which one has more shells? Explain in one or two lines.\n\n13. Give the position (period and group) of the element with atomic number 19, and predict the formula of its oxide.\n\n14. State True or False, and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Group 17 elements have valency 7.\n    (b) NH₃ is a covalent compound.\n    (c) Na and Cl form a covalent compound.\n    (d) The elements of the same group have the same valency.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Configuration 2, 5: 2 shells, to **period 2**. Valence electrons 5, to group 5 + 10 = **15**. Valency 8 − 5 = **3**. Right side ka, to **non-metal** (nitrogen, Z = 2 + 5 = 7). Hydrogen (valency 1) ke saath criss-cross se **NH₃** (covalent, dono non-metal) ✅.\n\n2. Period 3 ka matlab Na se Ar tak. Group 2 ka matlab 2 valence electron. Dono mila kar: **Mg** (magnesium).\n\n3. 2 shells (period 2), non-metal gas, valency 1: **F** (fluorine) (period 2 mein group 17, valency 8 − 7 = 1).\n\n4. 3 valence electrons aur 3 shells: group 13, period 3. Metal hai, to **Al** (aluminium).\n\n5. 1 shell wale noble gas: **He** (helium). H ek noble gas nahi hai.\n\n6. Alkali metal, 2 shells: **Li** (lithium).\n\n7. Period 3, valency 2: Mg (2, 8, 2) aur S (2, 8, 6, valency 8 − 6 = 2). Mg metal hai, non-metal chahiye, to **S** (sulphur).\n\n8. Alkaline earth metals mein Be, Mg aur Ca teeno aate hain. Ek aur clue: \"mere paas 3 shells hain\" to **Mg**, ya \"mere paas 4 shells hain\" to **Ca**.\n\n9. (a) **CaCl₂**, ionic. (b) **Al₂O₃**, ionic. (c) **CH₄**, covalent, mass 12 + 4 × 1 = **16**. (d) **K₂O**, ionic (K valency 1, O valency 2), mass 2 × 39 + 16 = 78 + 16 = **94**.\n\n10. Ca (2, 8, 8, 2) apne 2 valence electrons chhodta hai, to uske paas 2, 8, 8 bachta hai (Ar jaisa). Do Cl (2, 8, 7) mein se har ek 1 electron leta hai, to dono 2, 8, 8 (Ar jaisa) ban jaate hain. Isliye 1 Ca + 2 Cl = **CaCl₂**, aur ye ionic bond hai.\n\n11. Sr group 2 mein hai, to **valency 2**. Group 2 mein bhi neeche jaate hue reactivity badhti hai (shells zyada, baahari electron door), to Sr, Ca se **zyada reactive**. Chloride: Sr (2) aur Cl (1) se **SrCl₂**, bilkul CaCl₂ jaisa.\n\n12. **Ca zyada reactive** hai, aur uske **4 shells** hain (Mg ke 3). Neeche jaate hue shells badhte hain, baahari electron door hota hai, aur aasani se nikalta hai.\n\n13. Atomic number 19 ka matlab K: configuration 2, 8, 8, 1, to **period 4, group 1**, valency 1. Oxide: **K₂O**.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. Group 17 mein 7 valence electron hain par valency 1 hai (8 − 7). (b) **True** ✅. N aur H dono non-metal hain. (c) **False** ❌. Na metal aur Cl non-metal hai, to NaCl **ionic** hai. (d) **True** ✅. Ek group ke elements ke valence electrons same hote hain, to valency bhi same."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary: The Periodic Table",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter Ka Saar: Periodic Table",
            "body": "Let us take a bird's-eye view of our entire journey across this foundational chapter:\n\n| Topic | Core Subject Explored | Key Conceptual Breakthrough |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Topic 1** | Early Classification: Dobereiner and Newlands | Dobereiner's triads ($\\text{Li}, \\text{Na}, \\text{K}$) and Newlands' Law of Octaves revealed the first signs of order and periodicity, but failed beyond light elements. A great classification must possess **predictive capability**. |\n| **Topic 2** | Mendeleev's Periodic Table | Mendeleev organized elements by atomic mass into periods and groups, boldly left blank spaces, and accurately predicted the properties of Gallium, Scandium, and Germanium. Limitations: Hydrogen, isotopes, and inverted pairs ($\\text{Ar}-\\text{K}$). |\n| **Topic 3** | The Modern Periodic Table | Henry Moseley proved that **atomic number ($Z$)** is the fundamental organizing parameter, eliminating all inverted pairs. The modern table comprises **18 vertical groups** and **7 horizontal periods**, where periods match electron shells and groups match valence electrons. |\n| **Topic 4** | Periodic Trends | Across a period: shells remain constant, nuclear charge rises, valency cycles $1 \\rightarrow 4 \\rightarrow 0$, atomic size decreases, and metallic character decreases. Down a group: shells increase, atomic size increases, metallic character increases, and valency remains constant. |\n| **Topic 5** | Families of Elements | Vertical groups form chemical families: Group 1 (Alkali Metals), Group 2 (Alkaline Earth Metals), Group 17 (Halogens), and Group 18 (Noble Gases). Shared valence electrons govern matching chemical personalities and reactivity trends. |\n| **Topic 6** | Predicting from Coordinates | An element's position on the periodic grid allows instantaneous deduction of its electron configuration, valency, metallic nature, bonding mechanism, and compound formulas. |\n\nMaya placed the final Clue Card down with a triumphant grin. \"I understand now! The periodic table isn't an arbitrary list to be memorized; it's a map that can be read. Once you know an element's coordinates, nature reveals everything about it.\"\n\nLeo looked out the window. \"Look—the rain has finally cleared. Let us head outside and enjoy the fresh air!\"\n\n---\n\n**What lies ahead?** In our next chapter, **\"Water,\"** we will explore the most abundant and vital liquid compound on Earth: a covalent partnership between Hydrogen and Oxygen that possesses unique anomalies without which life could not exist!",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ek nazar mein poore chapter ka safar: elements ko sajaane ki koshish se lekar modern table, pattern aur families tak.\n\n| Topic | Kya seekha | Ek line |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topic 1 | Elements ko sajaane ki zaroorat aur shuruaati koshishein | Dobereiner ke triads (Li, Na, K) aur Newlands ke octaves ne pehli baar pattern dikhaya, par kuch hi elements par chale, aur sabse zaroori baat: ek achhi arrangement ko bhavishyavani bhi karni chahiye. |\n| Topic 2 | Mendeleev ka periodic table | Mendeleev ne atomic mass ke hisaab se groups aur periods banaye, gaps chhode aur gallium jaise elements pehle se bataye, par hydrogen, isotopes aur ulte-jode (Ar aur K) uski kamiyan thi. |\n| Topic 3 | Modern periodic table | Moseley ne dikhaya ki order atomic number se tay hota hai, isliye 18 groups aur 7 periods hain, period shells batata hai aur group valence electrons. |\n| Topic 4 | Periodic trends | Period mein left se right valency 1 se 4 tak chadh kar 0 tak aati hai, size ghatta hai, metallic character ghatta hai; group mein neeche size aur metallic character badhte hain, valency same rehti hai. |\n| Topic 5 | Families of elements | Ek hi group ke elements ek parivaar hain: alkali metals, alkaline earth metals, halogens aur noble gases, jinke valence electrons same hain aur vyavhaar milta-julta hai. |\n| Topic 6 | Table se bhavishyavani | Position se period, group, valency, metal ya non-metal, formula aur bond type pata chalta hai, aur clue card puzzle iska maza hai. |\n\nSamiksha ne aakhri clue card padha, jawab bataya aur copy band ki. \"Papa, ab mujhe samajh aaya ki table ratna nahi, **padhna** hai. Pehle period aur group, phir sab kuch apne aap.\" Mr. Jadhav muskuraye. \"Aur barish ruk gayi. Chalo, khelne bhi chalte hain!\"\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Tumne chapter ke saare 6 topics poore kar liye: ab tum kisi bhi element ki position se uski kundli likh sakte ho, aur clue card puzzle bhi hal kar sakte ho.\n\n**Aage kya?** Agle chapter **\"Water\"** mein hum hydrogen aur oxygen ke sabse zaroori compound, paani, ko dekhenge: ye itna khaas kyun hai ki zindagi iske bina nahi chalti."
          }
