          {
            "heading": "Part A: Introduction to Hydrogen: Atom and Molecule",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Hydrogen ka parichay: atom aur molecule",
            "body": "### The Atom: The Simplest of All\n\nRecall from Chapter 5 that an atom's identity is fixed by its **atomic number Z**, the number of protons in its nucleus. For hydrogen, **Z = 1**: its atom has **1 proton** and **1 electron**. Most remarkably, ordinary hydrogen has **no neutron at all** (0 neutrons) in its nucleus. That is why it is the smallest and lightest atom: mass number A = 1 + 0 = 1, and atomic mass 1 u (Chapter 4).\n\n- **Symbol:** H\n- **Electronic configuration:** 1 (just 1 electron in the K shell)\n- **Valency:** 1. Hydrogen can either **lose** its single electron, or **gain / share** one more electron to fill its K shell as a stable duplet (2 electrons). Either way, its valency is 1.\n\n### The Molecule: H₂\n\nIn nature, a single isolated hydrogen atom rarely stays alone. Two hydrogen atoms **share** one electron each (the covalent bond from Chapter 6) to form **H₂**. Each atom then sees **2 electrons** in its K shell (a complete duplet), making both stable. Between the two atoms lies **one shared pair of electrons**, which is **one single covalent bond** (H–H). That is why hydrogen gas is written as \"H₂\" and not \"H\": it is **diatomic**.\n\n```\n    One atom of hydrogen (H)          One molecule of hydrogen (H₂)\n\n          1 electron                       shared pair\n             o                                (e e)\n         .   .   .                        .--------------.\n       .           .                    (  H     :    H  )\n      .     (+)     .                    '--------------'\n       .   1 proton .\n         .   .   .                      H : H   or   H–H\n                                        (one single bond)\n\n    Only 1 proton in the nucleus,       Two nuclei sharing 2 electrons\n    0 neutrons. 1 electron in           between them. Each atom's K shell\n    the half-filled K shell.            is completed as a \"duplet\".\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – A hydrogen atom (1 proton, 1 electron, 0 neutrons) and an H₂ molecule (one shared pair of electrons between two atoms, forming a single covalent bond). The drawing is purely illustrative; in reality, the electron orbits far from a tiny central nucleus.\n\nCheck: Molar mass of H₂ = 2 × 1 = 2 g per mol, which matches the Gas Laws table (M = 2 for hydrogen) ✅\n\n### How Light Is Hydrogen?\n\nOne mole of hydrogen gas (H₂) has a mass of only **2 g**. That is the lowest of any gas: helium is 4 g, nitrogen is 28 g, and air averages about 29 g (Gas Laws, Topic 5). For this reason, **hydrogen is the lightest gas known**, and it rises faster than any other gas in air.\n\n> **Check:** An atom with 1 proton and 0 neutrons has a mass number of 1: 1 + 0 = 1 ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Atom: sabse sada\n\nChapter 5 se yaad karo: atom ki pehchaan uska **atomic number Z** hai, yaani protons ki ginti. Hydrogen ka **Z = 1**: uske atom mein **1 proton** aur **1 electron** hai. Sabse ajeeb baat: aam hydrogen ke nucleus mein **neutron hai hi nahi** (0 neutron). Isliye ye sabse chhota aur sabse halka atom hai: mass number A = 1 + 0 = 1, atomic mass 1 u (Chapter 4).\n\n- **Symbol:** H\n- **Electronic configuration:** 1 (sirf K shell mein 1 electron)\n- **Valency:** 1. Hydrogen ek electron **kho** sakta hai, ya ek electron **aur le** kar (ya share karke) apni K shell ko duplet (2 electron) se bhar sakta hai. Dono tarah se uski valency 1 hi aati hai.\n\n### Molecule: H₂\n\nPrakriti mein hydrogen akela atom bahut kam tikta hai. Do hydrogen atom apna-apna ek electron **share** karte hain (Chapter 6 ka covalent bond) aur **H₂** banate hain. Har atom ko apni K shell mein **2 electron** dikhte hain (duplet), isliye dono sthir ho jaate hain. Do atoms ke beech **ek shared pair**, yaani **ek single covalent bond** (H–H) hota hai. Isliye hydrogen gas ko \"H₂\" likhte hain, \"H\" nahi: wo **diatomic** hai.\n\n```\n   Hydrogen ka ek atom (H)            Hydrogen ka molecule (H₂)\n\n         1 electron                       shared pair\n            o                              (e e)\n        .   .   .                      .--------------.\n      .           .                  (  H     :    H  )\n     .     (+)     .                  '--------------'\n      .   1 proton .\n        .   .   .                    H : H   yaani   H–H\n                                     (ek single bond)\n\n   Nucleus mein sirf 1 proton,       Do nucleus, beech mein 2 electron\n   neutron 0. K shell mein           saajha. Har atom ki K shell\n   1 electron (aadhi bhari).         \"duplet\" se poori.\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Hydrogen ka atom (1 proton, 1 electron, 0 neutron) aur H₂ molecule (do atoms ke beech ek shared pair, yaani ek single covalent bond). Drawing sirf nidarshan hai; asal mein electron nucleus se bahut door ghoomta hai aur nucleus bahut chhota hota hai.\n\nJaanch: H₂ ka molar mass = 2 × 1 = 2 g per mol, jo Gas Laws wali table (hydrogen ka M = 2) se milta hai ✅\n\n### Hydrogen kitna halka hai?\n\nHydrogen ka 1 mole (H₂) sirf **2 g** ka hota hai. Ye har gas mein sabse kam hai: helium ka 4 g, nitrogen ka 28 g, hawa ka lagbhag 29 g (Gas Laws, Topic 5). Isi liye **hydrogen sabse halki gas** hai, aur hawa mein sabse tezi se upar uthti hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Ek atom mein 1 proton aur 0 neutron ho to mass number 1 hota hai: 1 + 0 = 1 ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Hydrogen in the Universe and on Earth",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Brahmand aur dharti par hydrogen",
            "body": "Now let's return to Leo's question: \"How much hydrogen is in the Sun?\" Scientists split sunlight into a rainbow of colors (a \"spectrum\") to measure how much of each element is present. By **mass**, the Sun is roughly **73 percent hydrogen**, about **25 percent helium**, and only about **2 percent** all other elements combined (oxygen, carbon, iron, and so on). (These figures are **approximate**; different sources may quote slightly different numbers.)\n\n```\n    100 \"parts\" of the Sun (by mass, approximate)\n\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H H H H H H H H\n    H H H E E E E E E E\n    E E E E E E E E E E\n    E E E E E E E E x x\n\n    H = hydrogen (73 parts)    E = helium (25 parts)\n    x = all other elements (2 parts)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Approximate breakdown of the Sun's mass across 100 small squares: 73 hydrogen, 25 helium, and 2 everything else (approximate figures). Hydrogen makes up the bulk of the Sun and most other stars.\n\nAcross the entire **universe**, the picture is very similar: by mass, roughly 75 percent is hydrogen, about 24 percent is helium, and all other elements together make up about 1 percent. That is why hydrogen is called **the most abundant element in the universe**, serving as the primary \"fuel\" and raw building block of stars.\n\n### On Earth, the Story Is Reversed\n\nOn Earth, **free (uncombined)** hydrogen is almost non-existent. In the atmosphere, H₂ makes up only about 0.00005 percent (approximate). Why? Because H₂ is so light that it rises to the top of the atmosphere and escapes into outer space (just as we saw for light gases in Gas Laws). Consequently, almost all hydrogen on Earth is found **combined with other elements in compounds**:\n\n- **Water (H₂O):** every water molecule contains 2 hydrogen atoms—in oceans, rivers, rain, and ice.\n- **Living things:** in plants, animals, and our own bodies (across countless organic compounds).\n- **Hydrocarbons (fuels):** gasoline, diesel, LPG, natural gas, and candle wax (compounds of hydrogen and carbon).\n- **Acids:** such as hydrochloric acid (HCl) in our stomach. You will study the full story of acids in Class 10.\n\nLet's do a quick calculation of how much hydrogen is locked inside water:\n\n### Problem 1. Mass of hydrogen in water\n\nHow many grams of hydrogen are present in 180 g of water? (H = 1, O = 16.)\n\n- M(H₂O) = 2 × 1 + 16 = 18 g per mol.\n- Fraction of hydrogen = 2 ÷ 18 = 0.1111, or about **11.1 percent**.\n- Hydrogen in 180 g of water = 180 × 2 ÷ 18 = **20 g**.\n\nCheck: 20 g hydrogen + 160 g oxygen = 180 g ✅ (oxygen: 180 × 16 ÷ 18 = 160 g)\n\nThis is the exact 1 : 8 mass ratio we met in Chapter 9: 20 : 160 = 1 : 8 ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab Sarthak ke sawaal par aate hain: \"Sooraj mein kitni hydrogen hai?\" Vaigyanik Sooraj ki roshni ko alag-alag rangon mein todkar (ek tarah ka \"spectrum\") dekhte hain aur usse pata lagate hain ki wahan kaun sa tatva kitna hai. Mote taur par Sooraj ke **vazan** ka lagbhag **73 percent hydrogen**, lagbhag **25 percent helium** aur baaki lagbhag **2 percent** doosre tatva (oxygen, carbon, iron wagairah) hain. (Ye ankde **lagbhag** hain; alag-alag source mein thode alag mil sakte hain.)\n\n```\n   Sooraj ke 100 \"hisse\" (vazan ke hisaab se, lagbhag)\n\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H H H H H H H H\n   H H H E E E E E E E\n   E E E E E E E E E E\n   E E E E E E E E x x\n\n   H = hydrogen (73 hisse)    E = helium (25 hisse)\n   x = baaki sab tatva (2 hisse)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Sooraj ke vazan ka lagbhag batwara, 100 chhote dibbon mein: hydrogen 73, helium 25, baaki 2 (lagbhag ankde). Sooraj aur doosre sitaron ka bada hissa hydrogen hi hai.\n\nPoore **brahmand** ke liye bhi lagbhag yahi tasveer hai: vazan ke hisaab se lagbhag 75 percent hydrogen, lagbhag 24 percent helium, aur sab kuch milakar 1 percent. Isliye hydrogen **brahmand ka sabse zyada milne wala tatva** kahlata hai, aur sitaron ka mukhya \"eendhan\" aur \"kachcha maal\" yehi hai.\n\n### Par dharti par ulta hai\n\nDharti par hydrogen **akeli (free)** lagbhag nahi milti. Hawa mein H₂ ka hissa bahut kam hai: lagbhag 0.00005 percent (lagbhag ankda). Wajah: H₂ itni halki hai ki wo hawa mein upar uthte-uthte dharti ke bahar antariksh mein nikal jaati hai (Gas Laws ki halki gas wali baat). Isliye dharti par hydrogen zyadatar **doosre tatvon se jud kar (compound mein)** milti hai:\n\n- **Paani (H₂O):** har paani ke anu mein 2 hydrogen atoms. Samudra, nadi, baarish, sab.\n- **Jeevit cheezein:** pedon, jaanwaron aur hamare sharir mein (kai compounds mein).\n- **Hydrocarbon (fuels):** petrol, diesel, LPG, natural gas, mom (hydrogen + carbon).\n- **Acid:** jaise hamare pet ka acid (hydrochloric acid, HCl). Acid ki poori kahani Class 10 mein.\n\nPaani mein hydrogen kitni hai, ek chhota hisaab:\n\n### Problem 1. Paani mein hydrogen ka vazan\n\n180 g paani mein kitne gram hydrogen hota hai? (H = 1, O = 16.)\n\n- M(H₂O) = 2 × 1 + 16 = 18 g per mol.\n- Hydrogen ka hissa = 2 ÷ 18 = 0.1111, yaani lagbhag **11.1 percent**.\n- 180 g paani mein hydrogen = 180 × 2 ÷ 18 = **20 g**.\n\nJaanch: 20 g hydrogen + 160 g oxygen = 180 g ✅ (oxygen: 180 × 16 ÷ 18 = 160 g)\n\nYe wahi 1 : 8 vazan ka anupaat hai jo Chapter 9 mein mila tha: 20 : 160 = 1 : 8 ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Hydrogen's Unusual Place in the Periodic Table",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Periodic table mein hydrogen ka ajeeb ghar",
            "body": "In Chapter 8, we saw that hydrogen sits **at the top of Group 1, in Period 1**, right above Li, Na, and K. But is it truly one of their family? Let's look at both sides of the story.\n\n| Feature | Like Group 1 metals (Li, Na, K) | What hydrogen is in reality |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outer electron | 1 | 1 (which is why it is placed above Group 1) |\n| Valency | 1 | 1 |\n| Appearance | Shiny, solid metal | Colorless, odorless gas |\n| Type | Metal | **Non-metal** |\n| Molecule | Metallic lattice | H₂, a two-atom molecule (like Cl₂) |\n\nHaving 1 outer electron makes hydrogen resemble Group 1 on paper, but its actual properties (being a gas, a non-metal, and forming covalent H₂ molecules) resemble the halogens (F, Cl), which need **one more electron** to complete a duplet or octet. Thus, hydrogen stands \"between two groups.\" It is placed at the top of Group 1 purely for **convenience**, and you must remember that it is **not a metal** (as noted in periodic-table-part3.md).\n\n> **Check:** Seeing hydrogen in Group 1 and assuming it is a metal is wrong. ❌ Hydrogen is a non-metal gas; its placement in Group 1 is only because it has 1 outer electron. ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter 8 mein dekha tha ki hydrogen table mein **group 1 ke upar, period 1** mein baitha hai, Li, Na, K ke upar. Par kya wo unka \"saathi\" hai? Chalo dono taraf ki sachchai dekhte hain.\n\n| Baat | Group 1 ke metals (Li, Na, K) jaisa | Hydrogen asal mein |\n|---|---|---|\n| Baahari electron | 1 | 1 (isliye upar rakha gaya) |\n| Valency | 1 | 1 |\n| Roop | Chamakdar, thos dhatu | Rangheen, gandhheen gas |\n| Type | Metal | **Non-metal** |\n| Molecule | Dhatu ka jaal (metallic) | H₂, do atom ka (jaise Cl₂) |\n\nHydrogen ka 1 baahari electron use group 1 jaisa dikhata hai, par uske gun (gas, non-metal, covalent H₂ banana) halogens (F, Cl) jaise hain, jo ek electron **lekar** duplet ya octet banate hain. Isliye hydrogen \"do group ke beech\" ka hai. Use group 1 ke upar sirf **suvidha** ke liye rakha gaya hai, aur ye yaad rakhna zaroori hai ki wo **metal nahi** hai (periodic-table-part3.md ka note).\n\n> **Jaanch:** Hydrogen ko group 1 mein dekhkar use metal samajhna galat hai. ❌ Hydrogen ek non-metal gas hai; group 1 mein uski jagah sirf 1 baahari electron ki wajah se hai. ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: The Three Isotopes of Hydrogen",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Hydrogen ke teen isotopes",
            "body": "In Chapter 5, Topic 6, we learned that atoms of the same element can have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons—these are called **isotopes**. We saw a brief table of hydrogen's three isotopes there. Now let's examine them from the perspective of this chapter:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Protons | Neutrons | Electrons | Mass number A |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Protium | ¹₁H | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n| Deuterium | ²₁H | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 |\n| Tritium | ³₁H | 1 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n\nNeutrons = A − Z: 1 − 1 = 0, 2 − 1 = 1, 3 − 1 = 2 ✅\n\n```\n    Protium          Deuterium        Tritium\n    (A = 1)          (A = 2)          (A = 3)\n\n      (+)            (+)(n)           (+)(n)(n)\n    nucleus          nucleus          nucleus\n       o                o                o\n    1 electron       1 electron       1 electron\n\n    (+) = proton   (n) = neutron   o = electron\n\n    Ordinary         Heavy            Radioactive,\n    hydrogen         hydrogen         extremely rare\n    (about\n    99.98 percent)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – The three isotopes of hydrogen: all three have 1 proton and 1 electron, with 0, 1, and 2 neutrons respectively. Their mass numbers are therefore 1, 2, and 3. (Sketch is only for counting particles.)\n\n- **Protium** is our \"ordinary\" hydrogen: about **99.98 percent** of all hydrogen atoms are protium. Out of 10,000 hydrogen atoms, roughly 9,998 are protium. The tiny remainder (about 0.02 percent) is mostly deuterium.\n- **Deuterium** is often called \"heavy hydrogen\" (also written with the symbol D).\n- **Tritium** is **radioactive** (it emits radiation) and occurs in nature only in trace amounts. Its uses were mentioned in Chapter 5; here we simply recognize it.\n\nAll three have **almost identical chemical behavior**, because chemical reactions depend on electrons, and all three have just 1 electron (Chapter 5, Part C). Extra neutrons only change the **mass**.\n\n### Problem 2. Inside a deuterium atom\n\nHow many protons, neutrons, and electrons are in one neutral atom of deuterium (²₁H)?\n\n- Z = 1, so protons = **1**.\n- In a neutral atom, electrons = protons = **1**.\n- Neutrons = A − Z = 2 − 1 = **1**.\n\nCheck: protons + neutrons = 1 + 1 = 2 = A ✅\n\n### Problem 3. Three forms of hydrogen gas (molar mass)\n\nOrdinary hydrogen gas (H₂) has a molar mass of 2 g per mol. If both atoms are deuterium (D, mass 2), what is the molar mass of D₂? And what about a mixed molecule HD (one protium + one deuterium)?\n\n- D₂: 2 × 2 = **4 g per mol**. (Equal to helium's molar mass! Yet D₂ is still hydrogen, not helium; having the same mass is just a coincidence.)\n- HD: 1 + 2 = **3 g per mol**.\n\nCheck: H₂ 2 < HD 3 < D₂ 4 ✅ (each deuterium atom adds one extra neutron, or 1 u of mass.)",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter 5 ke Topic 6 mein dekha tha ki ek hi tatva ke atoms mein protons same aur neutrons alag ho sakte hain, ye **isotopes** hain. Wahan hydrogen ke teen isotopes ka chhota table tha. Ab unhe is chapter ke andaaz se dubara dekhte hain:\n\n| Naam | Symbol | Protons | Neutrons | Electrons | Mass number A |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Protium | ¹₁H | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n| Deuterium | ²₁H | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 |\n| Tritium | ³₁H | 1 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n\nNeutrons = A − Z: 1 − 1 = 0, 2 − 1 = 1, 3 − 1 = 2 ✅\n\n```\n   Protium          Deuterium        Tritium\n   (A = 1)          (A = 2)          (A = 3)\n\n     (+)            (+)(n)           (+)(n)(n)\n   nucleus          nucleus          nucleus\n      o                o                o\n   1 electron       1 electron       1 electron\n\n   (+) = proton   (n) = neutron   o = electron\n\n   Aam hydrogen     Bhaari           Radioactive,\n   (lagbhag         hydrogen         bahut rare\n   99.98 percent)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Hydrogen ke teen isotopes: teeno mein 1 proton aur 1 electron, neutron 0, 1 aur 2. Isliye mass number 1, 2, 3. (Sketch sirf ginti dikhane ke liye hai.)\n\n- **Protium** hi hamari \"aam\" hydrogen hai: lagbhag **99.98 percent** hydrogen atoms protium hain. 10000 hydrogen atoms mein se lagbhag 9998 protium. Baaki bahut kam (lagbhag 0.02 percent) mein zyadatar deuterium hai.\n- **Deuterium** ko kai baar \"heavy hydrogen\" kehte hain (symbol D bhi).\n- **Tritium** **radioactive** hai (kuch tarah ki kirnein chhodta hai) aur prakriti mein bahut hi kam hota hai. Uske upyog Chapter 5 mein aaye the; yahan bas pehchaan.\n\nTeeno ka **chemical behaviour lagbhag same** hai, kyunki chemistry electrons par chalti hai aur teeno mein electron 1 hi hai (Chapter 5 Part C). Neutron sirf **mass** badalta hai.\n\n### Problem 2. Deuterium atom mein kya kya hai\n\nDeuterium (²₁H) ke ek neutral atom mein kitne proton, neutron aur electron hote hain?\n\n- Z = 1, to protons = **1**.\n- Neutral atom mein electrons = protons = **1**.\n- Neutrons = A − Z = 2 − 1 = **1**.\n\nJaanch: protons + neutrons = 1 + 1 = 2 = A ✅\n\n### Problem 3. Hydrogen gas ke teen roop (molar mass)\n\nNormal hydrogen H₂ ka molar mass 2 g per mol hai. Agar dono atom deuterium (D, mass 2) hon, to D₂ ka molar mass? Aur ek protium + ek deuterium wale HD ka?\n\n- D₂: 2 × 2 = **4 g per mol**. (Helium ke barabar! Par D₂ hydrogen hi hai, helium nahi; mass ka barabar hona sirf ek sanyog hai.)\n- HD: 1 + 2 = **3 g per mol**.\n\nJaanch: H₂ 2 < HD 3 < D₂ 4 ✅ (har deuterium ek extra neutron jodta hai, yaani 1 u zyada.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Heavy Water",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Heavy water",
            "body": "If the ordinary hydrogen (protium) in water (H₂O) is replaced by **deuterium**, the resulting compound is called **heavy water**, written **D₂O**. It looks just like ordinary water, but it is slightly heavier because each D atom brings an extra neutron. (Same chemistry, different mass—just like isotopes.)\n\n### Problem 4. Molar mass of H₂O and D₂O\n\nCalculate and compare the molar masses of both. (H = 1, D = 2, O = 16.)\n\n- H₂O: 2 × 1 + 16 = **18 g per mol**.\n- D₂O: 2 × 2 + 16 = **20 g per mol**.\n- Difference = 20 − 18 = **2 g per mol**.\n- D₂O is heavier: 20 ÷ 18 = 1.111, or about **11 percent** heavier.\n\nCheck: 18 × 1.111 = 20.0 ✅\n\nThis slight extra mass is useful in nuclear engineering: **heavy water** is used in certain types of nuclear reactors (such as pressurized heavy-water reactors in Canada and India) as a **moderator**, meaning it helps slow down fast-moving neutrons inside the reactor core. For Class 9, this is just a brief glimpse; you will study nuclear reactors in higher classes.\n\n> **Safety:** Heavy water is part of specialized nuclear technology and is handled only by trained professionals. It is never used for home or school experiments. The water we drink every day is ordinary water (H₂O).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Paani H₂O mein agar normal hydrogen (protium) ki jagah **deuterium** ho, to jo paani banta hai use **heavy water**, **D₂O** kehte hain. Dekhne mein wo bilkul normal paani jaisa lagta hai, par thoda bhaari hota hai, kyunki har D ek extra neutron lekar aata hai. (Chemistry same, mass alag, bilkul isotopes ki tarah.)\n\n### Problem 4. H₂O aur D₂O ka molar mass\n\nDono ka molar mass nikaalo aur tulna karo. (H = 1, D = 2, O = 16.)\n\n- H₂O: 2 × 1 + 16 = **18 g per mol**.\n- D₂O: 2 × 2 + 16 = **20 g per mol**.\n- Fark = 20 − 18 = **2 g per mol**.\n- D₂O zyada bhaari hai: 20 ÷ 18 = 1.111, yaani lagbhag **11 percent** zyada.\n\nJaanch: 18 × 1.111 = 20.0 ✅\n\nYe \"bahut thoda sa\" bhaari fark nuclear reactors mein kaam aata hai: **heavy water** kuch tarah ke nuclear reactors (jaise Bharat ke heavy-water reactors) mein **moderator** ki tarah use hota hai, yaani wo reactor ke andar tezi se chalte kanon (neutrons) ki raftaar ko dheema karne mein madad karta hai. Ye bhi Class 9 ke liye bas ek jhalak hai; reactor ki poori kahani aage ki class mein.\n\n> **Safety:** Heavy water ek nuclear technology ka hissa hai; ise sirf prashikshit log sambhalte hain. Ghar ya school mein isse koi prayog nahi karna. Roz ka paani peene ke liye normal paani (H₂O) hi hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Quick Calculations: Counting Hydrogen Atoms",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Chhote hisaab: hydrogen ke atoms ginte hain",
            "body": "### Problem 5. Atoms in 1 g of hydrogen\n\nHow many atoms are in 1 g of hydrogen gas (H₂)? (Avogadro number = 6.022 × 10²³.)\n\n- M(H₂) = 2 g per mol, so 1 g = 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 mol of **molecules**.\n- Each molecule has 2 atoms: moles of atoms = 0.5 × 2 = **1 mol**.\n- Atoms = 1 × 6.022 × 10²³ = **6.022 × 10²³ atoms**.\n\nCheck: Directly, 1 mol of H atoms weighs 1 g (atomic mass 1), so 1 g contains 1 mol of atoms ✅\n\n### Problem 6. In 1,000 kg of solar matter\n\nIf 1,000 kg of the Sun's matter is divided in the mass ratio 73 : 25 : 2, how many kilograms of hydrogen, helium, and other elements does it contain?\n\n- Hydrogen = 1000 × 0.73 = **730 kg**.\n- Helium = 1000 × 0.25 = **250 kg**.\n- Others = 1000 × 0.02 = **20 kg**.\n\nCheck: 730 + 250 + 20 = 1000 kg ✅\n\n### Problem 7. Isotopes in 10,000 hydrogen atoms\n\nOut of 10,000 hydrogen atoms, roughly how many are protium and how many are \"others\" (deuterium + tritium)?\n\n- Protium = 10000 × 0.9998 = **9998**.\n- Others = 10000 − 9998 = **2**.\n\nCheck: 2 ÷ 10000 = 0.0002 = 0.02 percent ✅",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Problem 5. 1 g hydrogen mein atoms\n\n1 g hydrogen (H₂) mein kitne atoms hain? (Avogadro ki sankhya 6.022 × 10²³.)\n\n- M(H₂) = 2 g per mol, to 1 g = 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 mol **molecules**.\n- Har molecule mein 2 atoms: moles of atoms = 0.5 × 2 = **1 mol**.\n- Atoms = 1 × 6.022 × 10²³ = **6.022 × 10²³ atoms**.\n\nJaanch: Seedha: 1 mol H atoms ka vazan 1 g (atomic mass 1), to 1 g mein 1 mol atoms ✅\n\n### Problem 6. 1000 kg Sooraj ke padarth mein\n\nAgar Sooraj ke 1000 kg padarth ko (vazan ke hisaab se 73 : 25 : 2) baanta jaaye, to kitna hydrogen, helium aur baaki?\n\n- Hydrogen = 1000 × 0.73 = **730 kg**.\n- Helium = 1000 × 0.25 = **250 kg**.\n- Baaki = 1000 × 0.02 = **20 kg**.\n\nJaanch: 730 + 250 + 20 = 1000 kg ✅\n\n### Problem 7. 10000 hydrogen atoms mein isotopes\n\n10000 hydrogen atoms mein lagbhag kitne protium aur kitne \"baaki\" (deuterium + tritium) hain?\n\n- Protium = 10000 × 0.9998 = **9998**.\n- Baaki = 10000 − 9998 = **2**.\n\nJaanch: 2 ÷ 10000 = 0.0002 = 0.02 percent ✅"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake 1: Writing hydrogen gas as \"H\" instead of H₂**\n\nHydrogen gas is **H₂** (a diatomic molecule). A single H represents an isolated atom. Always write H₂ in chemical equations for hydrogen gas.\n\n**Mistake 2: Assuming hydrogen is a metal because it sits above Group 1**\n\nHydrogen is a **non-metal gas**. It is placed above Group 1 only for convenience because it has 1 outer electron.\n\n**Mistake 3: Thinking free hydrogen is abundant on Earth**\n\nHydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe, but on Earth **free (uncombined)** H₂ is extremely rare; almost all terrestrial hydrogen is **combined** in water, living organisms, and fuels.\n\n**Mistake 4: Thinking isotopes are different elements**\n\nProtium, deuterium, and tritium are **all hydrogen**, because every one of them has 1 proton. They differ only in their number of neutrons.\n\n**Mistake 5: Thinking heavy water is a \"super-dense, deadly\" liquid**\n\nHeavy water (D₂O) is simply water made with deuterium atoms, and it is only about 11 percent heavier than ordinary water (molar mass 20 vs. 18). It is used in specialized nuclear technology and handled by trained professionals.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: Hydrogen gas ko H₂ ki jagah sirf \"H\" likhna**\n\nGas hydrogen **H₂** hai (do atoms ka molecule). Akela H ek atom hai. Reaction likhte waqt H₂ hi likho.\n\n**Galti 2: Group 1 mein dekhkar hydrogen ko metal maan lena**\n\nHydrogen ek **non-metal gas** hai. Wo group 1 mein sirf 1 baahari electron ki wajah se suvidha ke liye rakha gaya hai.\n\n**Galti 3: Sochna ki hydrogen dharti par bahut milti hai**\n\nBrahmand mein sabse zyada, par dharti par **akeli (free)** bahut kam hai; zyadatar paani, jeevit cheezon aur fuels mein **jud kar** milti hai.\n\n**Galti 4: Isotopes ko alag tatva samajhna**\n\nProtium, deuterium aur tritium **teeno hydrogen** hi hain, kyunki protons teeno mein 1 hain. Fark sirf neutrons ka hai.\n\n**Galti 5: Heavy water ko \"bhaari bojh wala\" khatarnak paani samajhna**\n\nHeavy water D₂O normal paani ka hi roop hai jisme deuterium hai, aur wo sirf lagbhag 11 percent bhaari (molar mass 20 against 18). Wo ek khaas technology mein kaam aata hai; use sambhalna prashikshit logon ka kaam hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Hydrogen:** symbol H, Z = 1, 1 proton + 1 electron, 0 neutrons in ordinary hydrogen; valency 1; the lightest element.\n- Gaseous form **H₂**: **one single covalent bond** (H–H) between two atoms; molar mass 2 g per mol, the lightest of all gases.\n- **Most abundant element in the universe:** by mass, the Sun is about 73 percent H, 25 percent He, and 2 percent other elements.\n- On Earth, hydrogen occurs mostly in **compounds**: water (about 11.1 percent H by mass in H₂O), living matter, hydrocarbon fuels, and acids. Free H₂ in air is tiny (about 0.00005 percent).\n- In the periodic table, hydrogen is placed above Group 1 for convenience; it is a **non-metal gas**, not a metal.\n- **Isotopes:** protium (1p, 0n, about 99.98 percent), deuterium (1p, 1n), tritium (1p, 2n, radioactive, trace); all three share nearly identical chemistry.\n- **Heavy water D₂O:** molar mass 20 compared to 18 for H₂O; used as a moderator in certain nuclear reactors.\n- Safety: hydrogen is a highly flammable gas; all demonstrations involving it are performed only by a teacher, never by students on their own.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Hydrogen:** symbol H, Z = 1, 1 proton + 1 electron, aam hydrogen mein 0 neutron; valency 1; sabse halka tatva.\n- Gas ka roop **H₂**: do atoms ke beech **ek single covalent bond** (H–H); molar mass 2 g per mol, sabse halki gas.\n- **Brahmand ka sabse zyada milne wala tatva:** Sooraj mein vazan ke hisaab se lagbhag 73 percent H, 25 percent He, 2 percent baaki.\n- Dharti par hydrogen zyadatar **compounds** mein: paani (H₂O mein lagbhag 11.1 percent vazan), jeevit cheezein, hydrocarbon fuels, acid. Akeli H₂ bahut kam (lagbhag 0.00005 percent hawa mein).\n- Periodic table mein hydrogen group 1 ke upar sirf suvidha ke liye hai; wo **non-metal gas** hai, metal nahi.\n- **Isotopes:** protium (1p, 0n, lagbhag 99.98 percent), deuterium (1p, 1n), tritium (1p, 2n, radioactive, rare); teeno ki chemistry lagbhag same.\n- **Heavy water D₂O:** molar mass 20 against H₂O ka 18; kuch nuclear reactors mein moderator.\n- Safety: hydrogen ek aag pakadne wali gas hai; iske sabhi prayog sirf teacher ke saath hote hain, bachche akele kabhi nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. How many protons, neutrons and electrons are there in a neutral tritium atom (³₁H)? What is its mass number?\n\n2. Compare the percentage by mass of hydrogen in ammonia (NH₃) and in methane (CH₄). Which has the larger share? (N = 14, C = 12, H = 1.)\n\n3. Find the molar mass of tritium gas T₂ (each T has mass 3) and of the mixed molecule HT.\n\n4. How many moles of molecules and how many hydrogen atoms are there in 4 g of hydrogen gas (H₂)? (Avogadro number = 6.022 × 10²³.)\n\n5. In 5,000,000 hydrogen atoms, about how many are protium and how many are not (use 99.98 percent for protium)?\n\n6. A sample of 400 kg of cosmic gas is about 75 percent hydrogen, 24 percent helium and 1 percent other elements by mass. Find the mass of each.\n\n7. Compare the mass of 5 mol of D₂O with the mass of 5 mol of H₂O. Which is heavier, and by how much?\n\n8. Hydrogen is placed above group 1 in the periodic table. Give one reason it fits there and two reasons it is not a group 1 metal.\n\n9. Why is hydrogen mostly found in compounds on Earth, although it is the most abundant element in the universe? Name three places where you will find combined hydrogen.\n\n10. Protium, deuterium and tritium behave almost the same in chemical reactions but have different masses. Explain why.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Tritium: Z = 1, so **1 proton**; a neutral atom has **1 electron**; neutrons = A − Z = 3 − 1 = **2 neutrons**; mass number **A = 3**. Check: 1 + 2 = 3 ✅\n\n2. NH₃: M = 14 + 3 = 17; hydrogen = 3 ÷ 17 = 0.1765, or **about 17.6 percent**. CH₄: M = 12 + 4 = 16; hydrogen = 4 ÷ 16 = 0.25, or **25 percent**. **Methane** has the larger share of hydrogen by mass. Check: in NH₃, nitrogen is 14 ÷ 17 = 82.4 percent, 17.6 + 82.4 = 100 ✅; in CH₄, carbon is 12 ÷ 16 = 75 percent, 25 + 75 = 100 ✅\n\n3. T₂ = 2 × 3 = **6 g per mol**; HT = 1 + 3 = **4 g per mol**. Check: each tritium atom is 2 u heavier than ordinary hydrogen, so from H₂ (2) to T₂ is 2 + 2 × 2 = 6 ✅\n\n4. M(H₂) = 2, so moles of molecules = 4 ÷ 2 = **2 mol**. Moles of atoms = 2 × 2 = 4 mol. Number of atoms = 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ = **2.409 × 10²⁴ atoms** (approx.). Check: 4 g of hydrogen atoms = 4 mol (atomic mass 1), 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.409 × 10²⁴ ✅\n\n5. Protium = 5,000,000 × 0.9998 = **4,999,000**. Others = 5,000,000 − 4,999,000 = **1,000**. Check: 1000 ÷ 5,000,000 = 0.0002 = 0.02 percent ✅\n\n6. Hydrogen = 400 × 0.75 = **300 kg**; helium = 400 × 0.24 = **96 kg**; other elements = 400 × 0.01 = **4 kg**. Check: 300 + 96 + 4 = 400 kg ✅\n\n7. M(H₂O) = 18, M(D₂O) = 20. 5 mol H₂O = 5 × 18 = **90 g**; 5 mol D₂O = 5 × 20 = **100 g**. **D₂O is heavier by 10 g** (about 11 percent heavier). Check: 100 ÷ 90 = 1.111 ✅\n\n8. **Reason it fits:** it has **1 outer electron** and a **valency of 1**, just like the Group 1 metals. **Two reasons it is not a metal:** (i) it is a **colorless gas**, not a shiny solid metal; (ii) it is a **non-metal** that forms a diatomic **covalent H₂ molecule** like Cl₂ (similar to halogens). Its placement above Group 1 is purely for **convenience**.\n\n9. In the universe, stars are made mostly of hydrogen, but on Earth free H₂ is so **light** that it rises through the atmosphere and escapes into space, leaving almost none in the air. On Earth it is found mostly **combined**: in **water** (H₂O), **fuels** (gasoline, LPG: hydrocarbons), and **living things** (bodies, plants), as well as in acids.\n\n10. Chemical reactions depend on **electrons**, and all three isotopes have **1 proton and 1 electron**, so their chemical behavior is nearly identical. They differ only in the **number of neutrons** (0, 1, 2), which changes their **mass** (mass numbers 1, 2, 3).\n\n---\n\nWell done! You now know the answers to Leo's planetarium questions: hydrogen is the smallest, lightest atom (1 proton, 1 electron), two atoms join to form H₂ (a single covalent bond), it is the most abundant element in the universe yet found on Earth mostly **combined** in water, living things, and fuels, its position in the periodic table is a matter of convenience, and it has three isotopes (protium, deuterium, tritium) from which heavy water (D₂O) is formed.\n\n**What's next?** If free hydrogen hardly exists on Earth, how do we **extract** it? In the next topic, **\"Laboratory Preparation of Hydrogen,\"** we will start with a memorable classroom story and see which metals and acids react to release hydrogen, which metals do not, and why students never perform this experiment on their own—observing it only as a teacher demonstration.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. How many protons, neutrons and electrons are there in a neutral tritium atom (³₁H)? What is its mass number?\n\n2. Compare the percentage by mass of hydrogen in ammonia (NH₃) and in methane (CH₄). Which has the larger share? (N = 14, C = 12, H = 1.)\n\n3. Find the molar mass of tritium gas T₂ (each T has mass 3) and of the mixed molecule HT.\n\n4. How many moles of molecules and how many hydrogen atoms are there in 4 g of hydrogen gas (H₂)? (Avogadro number = 6.022 × 10²³.)\n\n5. In 5,000,000 hydrogen atoms, about how many are protium and how many are not (use 99.98 percent for protium)?\n\n6. A sample of 400 kg of cosmic gas is about 75 percent hydrogen, 24 percent helium and 1 percent other elements by mass. Find the mass of each.\n\n7. Compare the mass of 5 mol of D₂O with the mass of 5 mol of H₂O. Which is heavier, and by how much?\n\n8. Hydrogen is placed above group 1 in the periodic table. Give one reason it fits there and two reasons it is not a group 1 metal.\n\n9. Why is hydrogen mostly found in compounds on Earth, although it is the most abundant element in the universe? Name three places where you will find combined hydrogen.\n\n10. Protium, deuterium and tritium behave almost the same in chemical reactions but have different masses. Explain why.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Tritium: Z = 1, so **1 proton**; a neutral atom has **1 electron**; neutrons = A − Z = 3 − 1 = **2 neutrons**; mass number **A = 3**. Jaanch: 1 + 2 = 3 ✅\n\n2. NH₃: M = 14 + 3 = 17; hydrogen = 3 ÷ 17 = 0.1765, yaani **lagbhag 17.6 percent**. CH₄: M = 12 + 4 = 16; hydrogen = 4 ÷ 16 = 0.25, yaani **25 percent**. **Methane** mein hydrogen ka hissa zyada hai. Jaanch: NH₃ mein nitrogen 14 ÷ 17 = 82.4 percent, 17.6 + 82.4 = 100 ✅; CH₄ mein carbon 12 ÷ 16 = 75 percent, 25 + 75 = 100 ✅\n\n3. T₂ = 2 × 3 = **6 g per mol**; HT = 1 + 3 = **4 g per mol**. Jaanch: har tritium aam hydrogen se 2 u bhaari hai, to H₂ (2) se T₂ 2 + 2 × 2 = 6 ✅\n\n4. M(H₂) = 2, to moles of molecules = 4 ÷ 2 = **2 mol**. Atoms ke moles = 2 × 2 = 4 mol. Atoms = 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ = **2.409 × 10²⁴ atoms** (lagbhag). Jaanch: 4 g hydrogen atoms = 4 mol (atomic mass 1), 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.409 × 10²⁴ ✅\n\n5. Protium = 5,000,000 × 0.9998 = **4,999,000**. Baaki = 5,000,000 − 4,999,000 = **1000**. Jaanch: 1000 ÷ 5,000,000 = 0.0002 = 0.02 percent ✅\n\n6. Hydrogen = 400 × 0.75 = **300 kg**; helium = 400 × 0.24 = **96 kg**; baaki = 400 × 0.01 = **4 kg**. Jaanch: 300 + 96 + 4 = 400 kg ✅\n\n7. M(H₂O) = 18, M(D₂O) = 20. 5 mol H₂O = 5 × 18 = **90 g**; 5 mol D₂O = 5 × 20 = **100 g**. **D₂O zyada bhaari hai, 10 g zyada** (lagbhag 11 percent zyada). Jaanch: 100 ÷ 90 = 1.111 ✅\n\n8. **Reason it fits:** uske **1 baahari electron** aur **valency 1** group 1 ke metals jaise hain. **Do reasons it is not a metal:** (i) wo **rangheen gas** hai, chamakdar thos dhatu nahi; (ii) wo **non-metal** hai aur Cl₂ ki tarah do atoms ka **covalent H₂ molecule** banata hai (halogens jaisa). Isliye uski jagah sirf **suvidha** ke liye hai.\n\n9. Brahmand mein sitaron ka bada hissa hydrogen hai, par dharti par akeli H₂ itni **halki** hai ki wo hawa mein upar uthkar antariksh mein nikal jaati hai, isliye hawa mein bahut kam bachti hai. Dharti par wo zyadatar **jud kar** milti hai: **paani** (H₂O), **fuels** (petrol, LPG: hydrocarbon) aur **jeevit cheezein** (sharir, ped). Acid mein bhi.\n\n10. Chemical reactions **electrons** par chalti hain, aur teeno isotopes mein **1 proton aur 1 electron** hai, isliye unka chemical behaviour lagbhag same. Alag hai sirf **neutrons ki ginti** (0, 1, 2), jo **mass** badalti hai (mass number 1, 2, 3).\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Ab tum Sarthak ke sawaalon ke jawab jaante ho: hydrogen sabse chhota, sabse halka atom hai (1 proton, 1 electron), do atom jud kar H₂ banate hain (ek single covalent bond), wo brahmand ka sabse zyada milne wala tatva hai par dharti par zyadatar paani, jeevit cheezon aur fuels mein **jud kar** milta hai, periodic table mein uski jagah ek suvidha hai, aur uske teen isotopes hain (protium, deuterium, tritium) jinse heavy water D₂O banta hai.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab sawaal ye hai: agar dharti par hydrogen akeli nahi milti, to use **nikaala** kaise jaata hai? Agle topic **\"Hydrogen Ki Lab Mein Taiyari\"** mein hum ek teacher ke yaadgaar kisse se shuru karenge aur dekhenge ki kaun si dhatu aur kaun sa acid milakar hydrogen deta hai, kaun si dhatu nahi deti, aur ye kaam bachche akele kyun nahi karte, sirf teacher ke saath dekhte hain."
          }
