          {
            "heading": "Part A: Which Method to Choose? (Decision Flow)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Kaun sa tareeka? (decision flow)",
            "body": "We have learned several ways to make hydrogen (Topics 2 and 3). The practical question is: **which method fits which situation?** To decide, we look at three things: **how much** hydrogen is needed, **what raw materials** are available, and **whether zero-carbon production** is required.\n\n```\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n    QUESTION A: Do you need only a tiny amount for a\n                classroom observation (teacher demo)?\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n         yes --> metal + dilute acid (Zn, Mg, or Fe)\n                 [Topic 2, tiny scale, teacher demo only]\n         no\n          v\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n    QUESTION B: Do you need huge industrial quantities at\n                low cost, with natural gas or coke on hand?\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n         yes --> steam reforming or water gas, + Bosch step\n                 [Topic 3, \"grey\" (releases CO₂)]\n         no\n          v\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n    QUESTION C: Do you need zero-carbon hydrogen where\n                abundant solar or wind electricity is available?\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n         yes --> electrolysis of water\n                 [Topic 3, \"green\" hydrogen]\n         no\n          v\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n    QUESTION D: Is a chlor-alkali plant already electrolyzing\n                brine (NaCl solution) to make Cl₂ and NaOH?\n    +---------------------------------------------------+\n         yes --> collect the hydrogen produced as a by-product\n         no  --> re-check from Question A: which requirement changed?\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – Decision flow for choosing a hydrogen production method: first check scale and available feedstocks. Tiny classroom demo (by a teacher only): metal + dilute acid. Massive industrial scale at lowest cost: natural gas or coke with steam. Clean and zero-carbon with renewable electricity: water electrolysis (green hydrogen). Chlor-alkali brine plant: hydrogen is collected automatically as a by-product.\n\n**Example 1: Choosing the right method.**\nThree scenarios: (a) A chemistry teacher wants to show her class a small sample of hydrogen in a test tube. (b) A large fertilizer plant needs thousands of kilograms of hydrogen every day and sits next to a natural gas pipeline. (c) A sunny desert region with abundant solar power wants to produce carbon-free hydrogen under a national clean-energy initiative.\n- (a) Question A: yes → **zinc granules and dilute acid** (Topic 2: zinc reacts at a steady, controllable rate), performed strictly as a teacher demonstration in tiny amounts, away from flames, in a well-ventilated room behind a safety screen.\n- (b) Question A: no (massive scale). Question B: yes → **steam reforming**: CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂, followed by the Bosch step: CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂. This produces \"grey\" hydrogen because CO₂ is released.\n- (c) Question C: yes → **water electrolysis powered by solar electricity**: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂. This produces **green hydrogen**, the focus of clean-energy initiatives worldwide (including India's 2023 National Green Hydrogen Mission).\n\n> **Check:** Each scenario stops at a different branch of the decision tree. Note that if the electricity in (c) came from burning coal instead of solar panels, the hydrogen would not be \"green,\" because true cleanliness depends on the electricity source too. ✅\n\n**Example 2: How much hydrogen comes from natural gas?**\nAdd the equations for steam reforming and the Bosch step:\nCH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂\nCO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂\nCombined equation: **CH₄ + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 4H₂** (the intermediate CO cancels on both sides).\nThus, 1 CH₄ molecule yields **4 H₂** molecules, so 10 CH₄ molecules yield **10 × 4 = 40 H₂** molecules. Those 4 H₂ molecules contain 8 H atoms in total: 4 come from the CH₄ molecule, and 4 come from the two H₂O molecules (2 × 2).\n\n> **Check:** Atom count. Left: C = 1, H = 4 + 4 = 8, O = 2. Right: C = 1, O = 2, H = 8. ✅\n\nRemember: releasing that CO₂ into the sky makes the hydrogen \"grey.\" If the CO₂ is captured and stored deep underground, it is called \"blue\" hydrogen (Topic 3).\n\n**Example 3: Water electrolysis—mass and volume.**\nSuppose 90 g of water undergoes electrolysis: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂. The molar mass of water is 2 × 1 + 16 = 18 g/mol, so 90 g = 90 ÷ 18 = 5 mol of water. Since 2 mol of water gives 2 mol of H₂, 5 mol of water gives **5 mol of H₂**, which is 5 × 2 = **10 g of H₂**. For oxygen: 2 mol of water gives 1 mol of O₂, so 5 mol gives 2.5 mol of O₂ = 2.5 × 32 = **80 g of O₂**. Total mass of products = 10 + 80 = 90 g, matching the starting mass of water (conservation of mass).\nFor gas volumes (Chapter 10): 2 volumes of hydrogen form for every 1 volume of oxygen. If 40 mL of hydrogen collects at the negative electrode, then **20 mL of O₂** collects at the positive electrode.\n\n> **Check:** 10 + 80 = 90 ✅. The mass ratio of hydrogen to oxygen is 10 : 80 = 1 : 8, the fixed composition of water (Chapter 9). Volume ratio 40 : 20 = 2 : 1 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Hydrogen banane ke kai tareeke hain (Topic 2 aur Topic 3). Sawaal hai: **kab kaun sa?** Isme teen cheezein dekhi jaati hain: **kitni** chahiye, **kaccha maal** kya hai, aur **saaf** hona zaroori hai ya nahi.\n\n```\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n   SAWAAL A: Bahut thodi hydrogen, sirf dekhne ke liye\n             (school lab, teacher demo)?\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n        haan --> metal + dilute acid (Zn, Mg ya Fe)\n                 [Topic 2, thodi matra, teacher ke saath]\n        nahi\n         v\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n   SAWAAL B: Factory ke pamaane par, saste mein, aur\n             natural gas ya coke maujood hai?\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n        haan --> steam reforming ya water gas, phir Bosch step\n                 [Topic 3, \"grey\" (CO₂ nikalti hai)]\n        nahi\n         v\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n   SAWAAL C: Saaf hydrogen chahiye aur solar ya wind ki\n             sasti bijli mil rahi hai?\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n        haan --> paani ka electrolysis\n                 [Topic 3, \"green\"]\n        nahi\n         v\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n   SAWAAL D: Namak ke ghol (brine) ka electrolysis pehle se\n             kisi kaam ke liye chal raha hai?\n   +---------------------------------------------------+\n        haan --> hydrogen by-product ke roop mein mil jaati hai\n        nahi --> sawaal A se phir dekho: kaun si zaroorat badli?\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – Decision flow: hydrogen banane ka tareeka chunne ke liye pehle matra aur kaccha maal dekho. Thodi matra (sirf teacher demo): metal aur dilute acid. Bahut zyada aur saste mein: natural gas ya coke se. Saaf aur sasti bijli ke saath: paani ka electrolysis (green). Brine ke electrolysis mein hydrogen apne aap by-product mil jaati hai.\n\n**Example 1: Kaun sa tareeka?**\nTeen situations: (a) Class ke saamne teacher ek chhota demo dikhana chahte hain. (b) Ek khaad ka bada plant roz hazaron kilo hydrogen chahta hai aur paas mein natural gas ki pipeline hai. (c) Ek registani ilaka jahan dhoop bahut hai aur solar bijli bahut sasti hai, aur sarkar saaf hydrogen chahti hai.\n(a) Sawaal A: haan, to **zinc granules aur dilute acid** (Topic 2: zinc ka reaction kaabu mein rehta hai), sirf teacher ke saath, thodi matra mein, aag door, hawa ki aavjaahi, safety screen ke saath (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo).\n(b) Sawaal A nahi (matra bahut badi). Sawaal B: haan, to **steam reforming**: CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂, phir Bosch step: CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂. Ye \"grey\" hydrogen hai kyunki CO₂ nikalti hai.\n(c) Sawaal C: haan, to **solar bijli se paani ka electrolysis**: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂. Ye **green hydrogen** hai, aur isi ke liye India ne 2023 mein **National Green Hydrogen Mission** shuru kiya.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Teeno jawab alag-alag sawaal par ruke. Agar (c) mein bijli coal se aati to hydrogen \"green\" nahi kehlati, kyunki asli saaf-pan bijli ke source par bhi tika hai. ✅\n\n**Example 2: Natural gas se kitni hydrogen?**\nSteam reforming aur Bosch step ke do equations ko jodo:\nCH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂\nCO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂\nDono ka jod: **CH₄ + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 4H₂** (beech ka CO dono taraf kat gaya).\nYaani 1 CH₄ se **4 H₂** milte hain. 10 CH₄ kan se **10 × 4 = 40 H₂** kan. In 4 hydrogen molecules mein 8 hydrogen atoms hain: 4 CH₄ ke kan se aate hain, aur 4 do paani ke kan se (2 × 2).\n\n> **Jaanch:** Atom count. Baayein: C 1, H 4 + 4 = 8, O 2. Daayein: C 1, O 2, H 8. ✅\n\nDhyan do: iska CO₂ hawa mein chhodna hi \"grey\" hone ki wajah hai. Agar CO₂ ko pakad kar zameen mein rok diya jaye, to wo \"blue\" kehlati hai (Topic 3).\n\n**Example 3: Paani ka electrolysis, mass aur volume.**\nMaan lo 90 g paani ka electrolysis hota hai: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂. Paani ka molar mass 2 × 1 + 16 = 18 g, to 90 g = 90 ÷ 18 = 5 mol paani. 2 mol paani se 2 mol H₂ milta hai, to 5 mol paani se **5 mol H₂**, yaani 5 × 2 = **10 g H₂**. Oxygen: 2 mol paani se 1 mol O₂, to 5 mol se 2.5 mol O₂ = 2.5 × 32 = **80 g O₂**. Kul 10 + 80 = 90 g: bilkul paani ka mass (mass conserved).\nVolume ke liye Chapter 10: 2 volume hydrogen aur 1 volume oxygen. Agar negative electrode par 40 mL hydrogen ikattha hui, to positive electrode par **20 mL O₂** hogi.\n\n> **Jaanch:** 10 + 80 = 90 ✅. Hydrogen aur oxygen ka mass ratio 10 : 80 = 1 : 8, jo paani ka fixed composition hai (Chapter 9). Volume 40 : 20 = 2 : 1 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Which Metal Will Work?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Kaun sa metal kaam karega?",
            "body": "In Topic 2, we learned that only metals sitting **above hydrogen** in the reactivity series displace hydrogen from dilute acids. Reactivity series (from top to bottom): K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, **(H)**, Cu, Hg, Ag, Au.\n\n**Example 4: Which metals release hydrogen from dilute acid?**\nConsider adding dilute hydrochloric acid to each of these metals (for observation only, teacher demonstration): Mg, Zn, Fe, Cu, Ag, Na.\n- **Mg, Zn, Fe**: sit above hydrogen, so they fizz and release H₂ gas. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (fast), Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ (moderate and steady), Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂ (slow).\n- **Cu, Ag**: sit below hydrogen, so **no reaction occurs**.\n- **Na**: sits near the very top of the series and reacts so violently and explosively with acid or water that it is never used in the lab to prepare hydrogen.\n\n> **Check:** Atom counts: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂: Mg 1, H 2, Cl 2 on both sides ✅. Zn + 2HCl: Zn 1, H 2, Cl 2 ✅. Fe + 2HCl: Fe 1, H 2, Cl 2 ✅.\n\n**Example 5: Identifying an unknown metal X.**\nAn unknown metal X produces bubbles of gas when placed in dilute HCl. Metal X **cannot displace** zinc from zinc sulfate solution, but it **does displace** copper from copper sulfate solution. Options: Mg, Fe, Cu, Ag. What is metal X?\n- Gives bubbles with dilute HCl → X sits above hydrogen, ruling out **Cu and Ag**.\n- Cannot displace zinc → X is **less reactive than zinc**, ruling out **Mg** (since Mg is more reactive than zinc and would displace it).\n- Displaces copper → X is more reactive than copper.\nThe only metal on the list that sits below Zn and above both H and Cu is **Fe** (iron). Answer: **Fe**, uniquely determined by the three clues.\n\n> **Check:** Fe is below Zn ✅ (does not displace Zn), Fe is above H ✅ (fizzes in dilute acid), and Fe is above Cu ✅ (displaces Cu). Three clues, one unique answer.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Topic 2 mein humne dekha tha ki sirf wahi metals hydrogen dete hain jo reactivity series mein **hydrogen se upar** hain. Series (upar se neeche): K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, **(H)**, Cu, Hg, Ag, Au.\n\n**Example 4: Kaun hydrogen dega?**\nDilute hydrochloric acid ke saath in metals ko dekhte hain (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo): Mg, Zn, Fe, Cu, Ag, Na.\n- **Mg, Zn, Fe**: hydrogen se upar, to bubbles dete hain. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (tez), Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ (darmiyani), Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂ (dheere).\n- **Cu, Ag**: hydrogen se neeche, to **kuch nahi hota**.\n- **Na**: bahut upar hai, acid ya paani ke saath itna tez aur khatarnak react karta hai ki lab mein use is kaam ke liye nahi lete.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Atom count: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂: Mg 1, H 2, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅. Zn + 2HCl: Zn 1, H 2, Cl 2 ✅. Fe + 2HCl: Fe 1, H 2, Cl 2 ✅.\n\n**Example 5: Anjaan metal X.**\nEk anjaan metal X dilute HCl ke saath bubbles deta hai. X zinc sulphate ke ghol se zinc ko **nahi hataata**, par copper sulphate ke ghol se copper ko hata deta hai. Options: Mg, Fe, Cu, Ag. Kaun hai X?\nBubbles diye: to X hydrogen se upar: Cu aur Ag **kat gaye**. X, zinc ko nahi hata paaya: to X zinc se **kam reactive** hai. Mg zinc se zyada reactive hai aur zinc ko hata deta, to Mg **kat gaya**. X ne copper ko hataya: to X copper se zyada reactive. Bacha **Fe**: Zn ke neeche aur H aur Cu ke upar. Jawab: **Fe** (iron), aur sirf ek hi option sab clues par khara utarta hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Fe, Zn se neeche ✅ (Zn ko nahi hataata), Fe, H se upar ✅ (bubbles), Fe, Cu se upar ✅ (Cu ko hataata). Teen clue, ek jawab."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: The Riddle Card: \"Who Am I?\"",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Riddle card: \"Main kaun hoon?\"",
            "body": "Now let's test Chloe's riddle card clue by clue. Each clue eliminates one or more gases until **only one** remains.\n\n```\n    *====================================================*\n    *            RIDDLE CARD No. 1  (Who Am I?)          *\n    *----------------------------------------------------*\n    *  1. I am the lightest gas of all.                  *\n    *  2. I have no color, odor, or taste.               *\n    *  3. I am almost insoluble in water.                *\n    *  4. When I burn in oxygen, pure water is the       *\n    *     only product formed.                           *\n    *  5. A small sample of me gives a squeaky \"pop\"     *\n    *     with a lighted splint (teacher demo only).     *\n    *                                                    *\n    *  WHO AM I?                                         *\n    *====================================================*\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Riddle Card No. 1: five clues about an unknown gas. Each clue narrows down the possibilities until only one gas fits every clue. (This is a paper-and-pencil reasoning game; no gas is prepared or burned.)\n\n**Example 6: Solving Riddle Card No. 1.**\n- Clue 1 (\"lightest gas of all\"): helium is the second lightest (M = 4), while hydrogen is the lightest (M = 2). Only **hydrogen** fits!\n- Clue 2: hydrogen is colorless, odorless, and tasteless ✅ (Topic 4).\n- Clue 3: hydrogen barely dissolves in water ✅ (which is why it can be collected over water, Topic 2).\n- Clue 4: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, forming only water ✅ (unlike methane, which also forms CO₂, or CO₂/N₂, which do not burn).\n- Clue 5: burns with a squeaky **pop** when tested with a lighted splint (teacher demo only, Topics 2 and 4) ✅.\nEvery single clue matches hydrogen, and Clue 1 alone rules out all other gases (He, N₂, O₂, CO₂). Answer: **hydrogen**.\n\n**Example 7: Riddle Card No. 2—a different gas.**\n\"I am a gas formed alongside hydrogen in the **Bosch step** (CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂). I dissolve readily in water under high pressure, allowing pure hydrogen to be separated from me. I turn clear limewater milky. I am heavier than air.\" Who am I?\n- The Bosch step produces two gases: H₂ and **CO₂**.\n- The one that dissolves in pressurized water is CO₂ (hydrogen barely dissolves).\n- Turning limewater milky is the classic test for CO₂.\n- \"Heavier than air\": the molar mass of CO₂ is 12 + 32 = 44 g/mol, compared with about 29 g/mol for air ✅ (Chapter 10). Answer: **carbon dioxide (CO₂)**.\n\n> **Check:** Washing the Bosch-step mixture with pressurized water dissolves CO₂ while leaving insoluble H₂ behind ✅. 44 > 29 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab Riddhi ke card ko ek ek clue se jaanchte hain. Har clue ek gas hata sakta hai, aur aakhir mein **sirf ek** bachni chahiye.\n\n```\n   *====================================================*\n   *            RIDDLE CARD No. 1  (Main kaun hoon?)    *\n   *----------------------------------------------------*\n   *  1. Main sabse halki gas hoon.                     *\n   *  2. Mera koi rang, gandh ya swad nahi.             *\n   *  3. Paani mein main lagbhag nahi ghulti.           *\n   *  4. Oxygen ke saath jalne par mujhse sirf paani    *\n   *     banta hai (aur kuch nahi).                     *\n   *  5. Meri thodi matra jalte splinter ke paas aaye   *\n   *     to \"pop\" (sirf teacher demo).                  *\n   *                                                    *\n   *  MAIN KAUN HOON?                                   *\n   *====================================================*\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Riddle card: ek gas ke paanch clue. Har clue ek ya zyada gaason ko hataata hai; jo gas saare clue par khari utarti hai wahi jawab hai. (Ye sirf kagaz ka khel hai, koi gas banayi ya jalayi nahi jaati.)\n\n**Example 6: Card ka hal.**\n- Clue 1 (\"sabse halki gas\"): helium dusri sabse halki hai, hydrogen pehli. Sirf **hydrogen** bachti hai.\n- Clue 2: hydrogen colourless, odourless, tasteless ✅ (Topic 4).\n- Clue 3: hydrogen paani mein lagbhag nahi ghulti ✅ (isi liye paani ke upar ikattha ki jaati hai, Topic 2).\n- Clue 4: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, sirf paani ✅ (CO₂ ya N₂ jaisi gas ye nahi karti).\n- Clue 5: jalte hue splinter se **pop** ki aavaaz (sirf teacher demo, Topic 2 aur Topic 4) ✅.\nSaare clue hydrogen par khare utarte hain, aur clue 1 akela hi doosri gaason (He, N₂, O₂, CO₂) ko hata deta hai. Jawab: **hydrogen**, sirf ek.\n\n**Example 7: Card No. 2, naya riddle.**\n\"Main ek gas hoon jo **Bosch step** mein banti hoon (CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂). Mujhe dabav (pressure) mein paani mein ghola ja sakta hai taaki hydrogen alag ho jaye. Main chuna-paani ko doodhiya kar deti hoon. Main hawa se bhaari hoon.\" Kaun?\nBosch step mein do gas milti hain: H₂ aur **CO₂**. \"Paani mein ghola jaata hai\" wali gas CO₂ hai (hydrogen to lagbhag nahi ghulti). \"Lime water doodhiya\" CO₂ ka test hai. \"Hawa se bhaari\": CO₂ ka molar mass 12 + 32 = 44 g, hawa ka lagbhag 29 g ✅ (Chapter 10). Jawab: **CO₂**.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Bosch step ke baad hydrogen aur CO₂ ke mishran ko paani se guzarne par CO₂ ghul jaati hai aur hydrogen bach jaati hai: kyunki hydrogen lagbhag nahi ghulti ✅. 44 > 29 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Equations and Reducing Agents",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Equation aur reducing agent",
            "body": "**Example 8: Hydrogen as a reducing agent.**\nFe₂O₃ + 3H₂ → 2Fe + 3H₂O\nAtom count: left side has Fe = 2, O = 3, H = 6; right side has Fe = 2, H = 6, O = 3 ✅.\nUsing Chapter 7 definitions: **Fe₂O₃ loses oxygen**, so it is **reduced** (forming metallic iron, Fe). **H₂ gains oxygen** (forming H₂O), so it is **oxidized**. Therefore, hydrogen acts as the **reducing agent**: it removes oxygen from the metal oxide.\nRemember that this works for oxides of moderately or less reactive metals (such as Cu, Pb, W, and Fe), whereas oxides of very reactive metals (like Mg or Al) cannot be reduced by hydrogen.\n\n> **Check:** In CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O: Cu = 1, O = 1, H = 2 on both sides ✅. Water is formed because hydrogen took the oxygen from CuO ✅.\n\n**Example 9: Counting molecules in ammonia synthesis.**\nN₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ (Haber process: iron catalyst, high pressure, reversible reaction—studied further in Class 10).\nIf 5 molecules of N₂ react completely, they require **5 × 3 = 15 H₂** molecules and produce **5 × 2 = 10 NH₃** molecules (in the ideal case). Atom count: left side has N = 5 × 2 = 10, H = 15 × 2 = 30; right side has N = 10, H = 10 × 3 = 30 ✅.\nThe molar mass of NH₃ is 14 + 3 × 1 = **17 g/mol**, and its chief use is manufacturing agricultural fertilizers (Topic 5).\n\n> **Check:** Each NH₃ molecule has 1 N atom bonded to 3 H atoms, matching nitrogen's valency of 3 and hydrogen's valency of 1 ✅.\n\n**Example 10: Comparing percentage by mass of hydrogen across compounds.**\nCalculate the mass percentage of hydrogen in CH₄, NH₃, H₂O, and HCl (using H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Cl = 35.5):\n- CH₄: 4 ÷ 16 × 100 = **25.0%**\n- NH₃: 3 ÷ 17 × 100 = **17.6%**\n- H₂O: 2 ÷ 18 × 100 = **11.1%**\n- HCl: 1 ÷ 36.5 × 100 = **2.7%**\nOf the four, **methane (CH₄)** has the highest mass percentage of hydrogen—one reason natural gas is such a rich feedstock for industrial hydrogen production.\n\n> **Check:** Mass percentage = (mass of H in 1 mol ÷ molar mass of compound) × 100; in CH₄, 4 g of H out of 16 g total gives 4 ÷ 16 = 0.25 (25%) ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 8: Hydrogen ek reducing agent.**\nFe₂O₃ + 3H₂ → 2Fe + 3H₂O\nAtom count: baayein: Fe 2, O 3, H 6. Daayein: Fe 2, H 6, O 3 ✅.\nChapter 7 ke hisaab se: **Fe₂O₃ ne oxygen khoya** to wo **reduce** hua (aur Fe milta hai). **H₂ ne oxygen paya** (H₂O bana) to wo **oxidise** hua. Isliye hydrogen yahan **reducing agent** hai: wo doosre se oxygen le leta hai.\nYe tabhi hota hai jab metal hydrogen se neeche ya thoda upar ho (jaise Cu aur Fe). Bahut reactive metals (jaise Mg) ke oxide ko hydrogen reduce **nahi** kar paati.\n\n> **Jaanch:** CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O: Cu 1, O 1, H 2 dono taraf ✅. Paani bana, to hydrogen ne CuO ka oxygen liya ✅.\n\n**Example 9: Ammonia banane mein hydrogen ke kan.**\nN₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ (Haber process, iron catalyst, bahut pressure, ulti ho sakne wali reaction: aage ki class mein).\nAgar 5 N₂ kan hain to **5 × 3 = 15 H₂** kan lagenge, aur **5 × 2 = 10 NH₃** kan bante hain (ideal roop mein). Atom count: baayein: N 5 × 2 = 10, H 15 × 2 = 30. Daayein: N 10, H 10 × 3 = 30 ✅.\nNH₃ ka molar mass 14 + 3 × 1 = **17 g**. Ye ammonia fertiliser banane mein jaati hai (Topic 5).\n\n> **Jaanch:** Ek NH₃ mein 3 H hain aur ek N: valency 3 aur 1 ka sauda, N ki valency 3 ✅.\n\n**Example 10: Hydrogen kitni \"bhaari\" hai ammonia, paani aur methane mein?**\nMass percent of hydrogen (H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Cl = 35.5):\n- CH₄: 4 ÷ 16 × 100 = **25%**\n- NH₃: 3 ÷ 17 × 100 = **17.6%**\n- H₂O: 2 ÷ 18 × 100 = **11.1%**\n- HCl: 1 ÷ 36.5 × 100 = **2.7%**\nSabse zyada hydrogen mass ke hisaab se **methane (CH₄)** mein hai, isi liye natural gas hydrogen banane ka achha source hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** Percentages ke formula mein H ka mass ÷ kul mass lena tha; CH₄ mein 4 hydrogen = 4 g, kul 16 g: 4 ÷ 16 = 0.25 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Balloons, Energy, and Volume Calculations",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Balloon, energy aur bulk ke hisaab",
            "body": "**Example 11: Balloon lift comparison.**\nFrom Chapter 10: density at STP = molar mass ÷ 22.4 L. For hydrogen: 2 ÷ 22.4 = 0.0893 g/L ≈ **0.09 kg per m³**. For air: 1.29 kg per m³, making air 1.29 ÷ 0.09 ≈ **14.3 times** heavier than hydrogen. For helium: 4 ÷ 22.4 = 0.1786 ≈ **0.18 kg per m³**.\nLift per m³: hydrogen = 1.29 − 0.09 = **1.20 kg**, helium = 1.29 − 0.18 = **1.11 kg**.\nFor a 10 m³ balloon: hydrogen gives 10 × 1.20 = 12.0 kg of lift, while helium gives 10 × 1.11 = **11.1 kg** of lift. Helium provides 11.1 ÷ 12.0 = **92.5%** of hydrogen's lift—only 7.5% less—while being completely non-flammable. That is why balloons use **helium** (never fill a balloon with hydrogen; buy only helium balloons from a shop, Topic 5).\n\n> **Check:** 1.20 − 1.11 = 0.09 ✅. The 0.9 kg difference over 12.0 kg is 0.9 ÷ 12.0 = 7.5% ✅.\n\n**Example 12: Fuel energy vs. storage volume.**\nHydrogen releases about **120 MJ per kg**, whereas gasoline releases about **45 MJ per kg** (approximate). Per kilogram, hydrogen delivers 120 ÷ 45 = **2.67 times** (about 2.7 times) as much energy.\nThus, 2.5 kg of hydrogen releases 2.5 × 120 = **300 MJ** of energy. To get that same 300 MJ from gasoline requires 300 ÷ 45 = **6.67 kg** of gasoline—so by mass, hydrogen is far lighter.\nWhat about volume? 2.5 kg of H₂ = 2,500 g ÷ 2 g/mol = 1,250 mol. At STP, 1,250 × 22.4 L = 28,000 L = **28 m³** (the size of a small bedroom!). That is why hydrogen must be stored under high compression or chilled to a cryogenic liquid—the **storage challenge** we studied in Topic 5.\n\n> **Check:** 1,250 × 22.4 = 28,000 L = 28 m³ ✅. 6.67 × 45 = 300 MJ ✅.\n\n**Example 13: Checking the explosive range.**\nHydrogen in air can ignite or explode between roughly **4% and 75%** by volume (approximate). Classify three mixtures: (a) 3% hydrogen in air, (b) 30% hydrogen in air, (c) 80% hydrogen in air.\n- (a) 3% < 4%: below the lower limit (too lean to sustain a flame).\n- (b) 30%: **inside the explosive range—extremely hazardous**.\n- (c) 80% > 75%: above the upper limit (too rich in place), **but still dangerous** because as soon as it mixes with surrounding room air, it dilutes right through the 75%–4% explosive window!\nTherefore, the rule for any hydrogen leak is strict: **keep all sparks and flames away, ventilate from the ceiling, and never let hydrogen accumulate in an enclosed space.**\n\n> **Check:** 3 < 4 ✅, 4 ≤ 30 ≤ 75 ✅, 80 > 75 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 11: Balloon ka lift.**\nChapter 10 se: gas ki density = molar mass ÷ 22.4 L (STP). Hydrogen: 2 ÷ 22.4 = 0.0893 g per L ≈ **0.09 kg per m³**. Hawa: 1.29 kg per m³, to hawa hydrogen se 1.29 ÷ 0.09 ≈ **14 guna** bhaari. Helium: 4 ÷ 22.4 = 0.1786 ≈ **0.18 kg per m³**.\nLift per m³: hydrogen = 1.29 − 0.09 = **1.20 kg**, helium = 1.29 − 0.18 = **1.11 kg**.\nAgar 10 m³ ka ek balloon ho: hydrogen ka lift 10 × 1.20 = 12.0 kg, helium ka 10 × 1.11 = **11.1 kg**. Helium ka lift hydrogen ka 11.1 ÷ 12.0 = **92.5%** hai: sirf 7.5% kam. Isliye weather balloon mein **helium** dalte hain: lift lagbhag utna hi, aur wo jalti nahi. (Balloon mein kabhi hydrogen mat bharo: shop se sirf helium lo, Topic 5.)\n\n> **Jaanch:** 1.20 − 1.11 = 0.09 ✅. 12.0 − 11.1 = 0.9 kg fark, 0.9 ÷ 12.0 = 7.5% ✅.\n\n**Example 12: Energy aur jagah.**\nHydrogen: lagbhag **120 MJ per kg**; petrol: lagbhag **45 MJ per kg** (approximate). Hydrogen kareeb 120 ÷ 45 = **2.7 guna** zyada energy per kg deti hai.\n2.5 kg hydrogen ki energy = 2.5 × 120 = **300 MJ**. Itni energy ke liye petrol: 300 ÷ 45 = **6.67 kg**. Yaani mass mein hydrogen bahut halki padti hai.\nPar jagah ka kya? 2.5 kg hydrogen = 2.5 × 1000 ÷ 2 = 1250 mol. STP par 1250 × 22.4 = 28000 L = **28 m³** (ek chhote kamre ki jagah!). Isi wajah se hydrogen ko bahut dabaa kar (compress) ya thanda karke (liquid) rakhna padta hai, aur yahin se **storage ki samasya** aati hai (Topic 5).\n\n> **Jaanch:** 28 m³ ya 28000 L: 1250 × 22.4 = 28000 ✅. 6.67 × 45 = 300 ✅.\n\n**Example 13: Kya jal sakti hai?**\nHydrogen hawa mein lagbhag **4% se 75%** (approximate) ke beech jal ya phat sakti hai. Teen mishran: (a) 3% hydrogen, (b) 30%, (c) 80%.\n(a) 3% < 4%: jalne ke liye bahut kam (lean). (b) 30%: **range ke andar, khatarnak**. (c) 80% > 75%: bahut zyada (rich), par **surakshit nahi**: jaise hi ye hawa mein milti hai, mishran 75% se 4% ki range se hokar guzarta hai.\nIsliye leak hone par bhi sabse zaroori baat: **aag door, hawa ki aavjaahi, band jagah mein kabhi jama na hone do.**\n\n> **Jaanch:** 3 < 4 ✅, 4 ≤ 30 ≤ 75 ✅, 80 > 75 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Connecting Across Chapters",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Chapters ka jod",
            "body": "**Example 14: Isotopes and heavy water.**\nFrom Chapter 5 and Topic 1: protium (1 proton, 0 neutrons), deuterium (1 proton, 1 neutron), tritium (1 proton, 2 neutrons, radioactive, trace). Protium makes up about 99.98% of natural hydrogen: out of 50,000 hydrogen atoms, **50,000 × 0.9998 = 49,990** are protium, and the remaining 10 are mostly deuterium.\nHeavy water (D₂O) has a molar mass of 2 × 2 + 16 = **20 g/mol**, compared with 18 g/mol for ordinary water (H₂O). Ratio: 20 ÷ 18 = **1.11** (about 11% heavier). Counting neutrons in one molecule of D₂O: 2 deuterium atoms contribute 1 + 1 = 2 neutrons, and oxygen-16 contributes 8 neutrons, giving **10 neutrons in total** (compared with 8 neutrons in H₂O). Heavy water is used as a **moderator** in pressurized heavy-water nuclear reactors.\n\n> **Check:** In D₂O, total protons = 1 + 1 + 8 = 10 and total neutrons = 1 + 1 + 8 = 10, giving a mass number sum of 20 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Example 14: Isotopes aur heavy water.**\nChapter 5 se: protium (1 proton, 0 neutron), deuterium (1 proton, 1 neutron), tritium (1 proton, 2 neutrons, radioactive, rare). Protium lagbhag 99.98% hai: 50000 hydrogen atoms mein protium **50000 × 0.9998 = 49990**, baaki 10 (deuterium aur tritium).\nHeavy water D₂O: molar mass 2 × 2 + 16 = **20 g**, jabki H₂O ka 18 g. 20 ÷ 18 = **1.11 guna**: lagbhag 11% zyada bhaari. D₂O ke ek kan mein neutrons: 2 deuterium ke 1 + 1 = 2, aur oxygen-16 ke 8: kul **10 neutrons** (H₂O mein sirf 8). Heavy water India ke heavy-water reactors mein **moderator** ke roop mein kaam aati hai.\n\n> **Jaanch:** D₂O mein protons: 1 + 1 + 8 = 10 aur neutrons: 1 + 1 + 8 = 10, kul 20 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Safety Rules (Hydrogen)",
            "headingHinglish": "Surakshit rehne ke niyam (hydrogen)",
            "body": "- Hydrogen is **extremely flammable**, and mixtures of hydrogen with air or oxygen can **explode**. Preparing, collecting, burning, or testing hydrogen is performed **only by a teacher in the school lab (for observation only, teacher demonstration)**, in tiny amounts, far from flames, with good ventilation and a safety screen.\n- **Never attempt to make or burn hydrogen at home.** Student activities at home are strictly paper-based (riddles, sorting tables, diagrams, calculations).\n- **Never fill balloons with hydrogen:** always use store-bought helium balloons. Remember the lesson of the 1937 Hindenburg airship disaster (36 fatalities).\n- **Never heat a sealed container or gas cylinder.** Keep compressed gas cylinders cool, ventilated, and out of direct sunlight.\n- Never mix reactive metals like sodium, potassium, or calcium with water or acid: those reactions are studied on paper only.\n- Never **taste** or directly **inhale** any laboratory gas.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Hydrogen **bahut jwalansheel** hai aur hawa ya oxygen ke saath mishran **phat** sakta hai. Ise banana, bharna, jalana ya test karna sirf **teacher ke saath school lab mein (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo)** hota hai, thodi matra mein, aag door, hawa ki aavjaahi aur safety screen ke saath.\n- **Kabhi bhi ghar par hydrogen mat banao ya jalao.** Ghar par sirf kagaz ke kaam (riddle, sorting, diagram) karo.\n- **Balloon mein hydrogen kabhi nahi:** shop se sirf helium. 1937 ka Hindenburg airship hadsa (36 mauten) yaad rakho.\n- **Band dibba kabhi garam mat karo.** Cylinder dhoop ya aag se door.\n- Sodium, potassium, calcium jaise metals ko paani ya acid ke saath mat milao: ye sirf padhne ke liye hain.\n- Koi bhi gas kabhi **chakh kar** ya **sungh kar** mat dekho."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **\"Hydrogen can burn even without air or oxygen.\"** False. Hydrogen is a **fuel**, so it requires **oxygen** to burn. Without oxygen, pure hydrogen cannot burn.\n2. **\"Every metal reacts with dilute acid to give hydrogen.\"** False. Only metals **above** hydrogen in the reactivity series displace hydrogen from dilute acids (Cu, Ag, and Au do not). And alkali metals like Na and K are far too explosive to use.\n3. **\"Dilute nitric acid also gives hydrogen gas with zinc.\"** False. Nitric acid acts as an oxidizing agent and behaves differently; you will study its reactions in Class 10.\n4. **\"Students can try the squeaky pop test at home.\"** False. Preparing, igniting, or testing hydrogen is strictly a **teacher demonstration**.\n5. **\"Because hydrogen burns to water, all hydrogen fuel is automatically carbon-free.\"** Burning hydrogen produces only water at the tailpipe, but if the hydrogen was made from natural gas (\"grey\" hydrogen), **CO₂ was emitted at the factory.** Always check how the hydrogen was produced.\n6. **\"Hydrogen is a weak fuel because it is so light.\"** Per **kilogram**, hydrogen has nearly 2.7 times *more* energy than gasoline (120 MJ vs. 45 MJ); it is only per **cubic meter (volume)** that uncompressed hydrogen is low, creating a storage challenge.\n7. **\"Hydrogen is a metal because it sits at the top of Group 1.\"** False. Hydrogen is a **non-metal gas** placed above Group 1 purely for convenience because it has 1 outer electron (Chapter 8).\n8. **\"A 90% hydrogen mixture in a pipe is completely safe because it's above 75%.\"** False. The moment it leaks into the room, mixing with air brings it right into the 4%–75% explosive range (Example 13).",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **\"Hydrogen bina hawa ya oxygen ke bhi jal jaati hai.\"** Nahi. Hydrogen **jalti** hai, par jalne ke liye **oxygen** chahiye. Bina oxygen ke wo bas rakhi rehti hai.\n2. **\"Hydrogen sab metals se banti hai.\"** Nahi. Sirf wahi metals jo reactivity series mein hydrogen se **upar** hain (Cu, Ag, Au nahi). Aur Na, K jaise bahut tez metals lab mein nahi lete.\n3. **\"Nitric acid ke saath bhi hydrogen milti hai.\"** Nahi. Nitric acid alag tarah se kaam karta hai; iska hisaab Class 10 mein hai.\n4. **\"Hydrogen ka pop test ghar par bhi kar sakte hain.\"** Nahi. Hydrogen ki taiyari, test aur jalana sirf **teacher demo** hai.\n5. **\"Hydrogen fuel hai to dhuan nahi chhodti.\"** Hydrogen jalane par sirf paani deti hai, par agar wo natural gas se bani hai (\"grey\"), to **banate waqt CO₂ nikalti hai.** Saaf-pan poore safar ka hai.\n6. **\"Hydrogen ki energy kam hai kyunki wo halki hai.\"** Per **kg** energy zyada hai (120 vs 45 MJ), par per **m³** bahut kam, isliye storage mushkil hai.\n7. **\"Hydrogen ek metal hai kyunki group 1 ke upar hai.\"** Nahi. Wo ek **non-metal gas** hai; sirf suvidha ke liye group 1 ke upar rakhi jaati hai (Chapter 8).\n8. **\"Zyada hydrogen mishran ho to surakshit.\"** Nahi. Hawa mein milte hi wo jalne ki range se guzarti hai (Example 13)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Hydrogen: lightest element, most abundant in the universe, symbol H, valency 1, diatomic gas H₂ (one single covalent bond). Isotopes: protium, deuterium, tritium. Heavy water D₂O (20 g/mol).\n- Lab preparation (teacher demo only): Zn, Mg, or Fe + dilute acid (HCl or H₂SO₄); copper does not react. Collected over water or by downward displacement of air in an inverted jar; identified by the squeaky pop test.\n- Other methods: electrolysis of water (2 : 1 volume ratio of H₂ : O₂), active metals + water (description only), steam reforming of natural gas and water-gas reaction from coke + Bosch step in industry, by-product of brine electrolysis, and green hydrogen from solar/wind electrolysis.\n- Properties: colorless, odorless, density ~0.09 g/L at STP, boiling point −253 °C (20 K), neutral to litmus. Burns in oxygen (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), combines with Cl₂ and N₂, and reduces heated oxides of moderately/less reactive metals.\n- Uses: ammonia (fertilizers), hydrogenating vegetable oils, cryogenic rocket fuel, fuel cells, welding. Delivers ~120 MJ/kg by mass, but takes up a huge volume as a gas.\n- Safety: highly flammable, explosive in air from ~4% to 75%, use helium (never hydrogen) in balloons, never heat sealed cylinders.\n- Burning hydrogen produces only pure water; what burning fossil fuels releases into the air is the subject of our next chapter.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Hydrogen: sabse halki, brahmand mein sabse zyada, symbol H, valency 1, H₂ (ek covalent bond). Isotopes: protium, deuterium, tritium. Heavy water D₂O (20 g).\n- Lab (teacher demo): Zn, Mg ya Fe + dilute acid; copper nahi deta. Paani ke upar ya hawa ko neeche dhakel kar (ulti jar) ikattha; pop test.\n- Aur tareeke: paani ka electrolysis (2 : 1 volume), active metals aur paani (sirf padhna), steam, natural gas aur coke se factory mein, brine ka by-product, green hydrogen.\n- Gun: colourless, odourless, density lagbhag 0.09 g per L, boiling point lagbhag −253 °C (20 K), neutral. Jalti hai (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), Cl₂ aur N₂ ke saath milti hai, reducing agent hai.\n- Upyog: ammonia (fertiliser), vanaspati, rocket fuel, fuel cell, welding. Energy 120 MJ per kg, par bahut bulky.\n- Safety: jwalansheel, 4% se 75% hawa mein phat sakti hai, balloon mein helium, band dibba garam nahi.\n- Hydrogen jalane par sirf paani; baaki fuels kya chhodte hain, agla chapter batayega."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Choose the best method of making hydrogen for each situation and state one reason: (a) a steel plant that already has coke and steam; (b) a chlor-alkali plant that splits brine to make chlorine and sodium hydroxide; (c) a school lab that has only copper turnings and dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\n2. A teacher plans a demonstration with dilute sulphuric acid. Which of these metals will give hydrogen: magnesium, gold, potassium, iron? For each one that does not, give the reason.\n\n3. Balance and check atoms: Fe + H₂O (steam) → Fe₃O₄ + H₂. Is iron oxidised or reduced here? Why?\n\n4. Combine C + H₂O → CO + H₂ and CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂ into one equation. How many H₂ molecules come from 7 carbon atoms by this two-step route?\n\n5. Riddle: \"I am a colourless, odourless gas. I am made at the positive electrode when water is electrolysed. The volume I make is half the volume of the gas at the other electrode. I relight a glowing splinter.\" Who am I? Explain why only one gas fits.\n\n6. Riddle: \"I am a greenish-yellow gas. I am made together with hydrogen and sodium hydroxide when brine is electrolysed. Hydrogen joins with me in sunlight or on heating to give hydrogen chloride. I am heavier than air.\" Who am I? Write the two equations and give the molar mass check for \"heavier than air\" (Cl = 35.5).\n\n7. A rocket stage carries 4 kg of hydrogen. (a) How much energy is that (120 MJ per kg)? (b) What mass of petrol would give the same energy (45 MJ per kg)? (c) What volume would 4 kg of hydrogen occupy at STP, in m³?\n\n8. A weather balloon of 25 m³ is filled with helium (density 0.18 kg per m³, air 1.29 kg per m³). Find the lift. What lift would hydrogen give, and why is helium still chosen?\n\n9. In the Haber process N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, how many H₂ and how many NH₃ particles correspond to 12 N₂ particles (ideal case)? Name the catalyst and state whether the reaction can go both ways.\n\n10. \"Semi-heavy\" water is HDO (one protium, one deuterium, one oxygen-16). Find its molar mass and the number of neutrons in one molecule. In 20000 hydrogen atoms of ordinary hydrogen, how many are protium (99.98%)?\n\n11. Find the mass percent of hydrogen in methanol, CH₃OH (C = 12, O = 16, H = 1). Is it more or less than in water?\n\n12. Classify each mixture as below, inside or above the flammable range (about 4% to 75% hydrogen in air) and say which is safe to leave near a spark: (a) 6%; (b) 50%; (c) 90%; (d) 0.5%.\n\n13. Liquid oxygen boils at −183 °C and hydrogen at −253 °C. Give both in kelvin, the difference, and say which gas needs more cooling to liquefy.\n\n14. True or false, with a reason: (a) Copper gives hydrogen with dilute hydrochloric acid. (b) In Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂ → 3Fe + 4H₂O hydrogen is oxidised. (c) Ordinary hydrogen (protium) has no neutron. (d) Students may test hydrogen with a lighted splinter at home.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Water-gas reaction + Bosch step**: C + H₂O → CO + H₂ followed by CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂, since coke and steam are already on hand. (b) **By-product collection**: electrolysis of brine automatically produces hydrogen alongside chlorine and NaOH, so no separate process is needed. (c) Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series and **will not release hydrogen** from dilute HCl—the lab needs a metal above hydrogen such as Zn, Mg, or Fe. Check: each case answers a different branch (A, B, D) of the decision flow. ✅\n\n2. **Magnesium** (Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂) and **iron** (Fe + H₂SO₄ → FeSO₄ + H₂) will release hydrogen. **Gold** sits far below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so no reaction occurs. **Potassium** sits at the very top of the series and reacts violently and explosively with acid or water, so it is never used in a lab demonstration. Check: Mg 1, S 1, O 4, H 2 on both sides ✅.\n\n3. **3Fe + 4H₂O → Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂**. Atom count: left side has Fe = 3, H = 8, O = 4; right side has Fe = 3, O = 4, H = 8 ✅. Iron gained oxygen, so **iron is oxidized** (while the steam lost oxygen and was reduced to H₂). Check: gaining oxygen is oxidation (Chapter 7) ✅.\n\n4. Add C + H₂O → CO + H₂ and CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂: **C + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 2H₂** (CO cancels on both sides). Since 1 C yields 2 H₂ molecules, 7 C atoms yield 7 × 2 = **14 H₂** molecules. Check: left has C = 1, H = 4, O = 2; right has C = 1, O = 2, H = 4 ✅.\n\n5. **Oxygen (O₂)**. During water electrolysis, oxygen forms at the positive electrode and hydrogen at the negative electrode in a 1 : 2 volume ratio (so oxygen's volume is half that of hydrogen). Relighting a glowing splint is the signature test for oxygen. Only O₂ fits both the electrolysis ratio and the glowing-splint test. Check: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂ gives 2 volumes of H₂ per 1 volume of O₂ ✅.\n\n6. **Chlorine (Cl₂)**. Brine electrolysis: 2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂ (Topic 3). Reaction with hydrogen: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (Topic 4). The molar mass of Cl₂ is 2 × 35.5 = 71 g/mol, compared with ~29 g/mol for air; since 71 > 29, chlorine is heavier than air ✅. Its greenish-yellow color and formation from brine uniquely identify chlorine. Check: in 2NaCl + 2H₂O, Na = 2, Cl = 2, H = 4, O = 2 on both sides ✅; in H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl, H = 2, Cl = 2 on both sides ✅. (Note: chlorine is a toxic gas; this is strictly a paper riddle.)\n\n7. (a) 4 × 120 = **480 MJ**. (b) 480 ÷ 45 = **10.67 kg** of gasoline (about 10.7 kg). (c) 4 kg = 4,000 g ÷ 2 g/mol = 2,000 mol; at STP, 2,000 × 22.4 L = 44,800 L = **44.8 m³**. That huge volume is why rockets chill hydrogen into a dense cryogenic liquid. Check: 10.67 × 45 = 480 ✅.\n\n8. Lift with helium = 1.29 − 0.18 = 1.11 kg/m³, so 25 × 1.11 = **27.75 kg**. Lift with hydrogen = 1.29 − 0.09 = 1.20 kg/m³, so 25 × 1.20 = **30.0 kg**. Difference = 30.0 − 27.75 = 2.25 kg (about 7.5%). Helium is chosen because it **cannot burn or explode**, whereas hydrogen is highly flammable. Check: 27.75 ÷ 30.0 = 0.925 ✅.\n\n9. For 12 N₂ molecules, 12 × 3 = **36 H₂** molecules are needed and 12 × 2 = **24 NH₃** molecules form. Catalyst: **iron**. The reaction is **reversible** (can go both ways, studied in higher classes). Check: left has N = 24, H = 72; right has N = 24, H = 24 × 3 = 72 ✅.\n\n10. Molar mass of HDO = 1 + 2 + 16 = **19 g/mol**. Neutrons in one HDO molecule: protium (0) + deuterium (1) + oxygen-16 (8) = **9 neutrons**. Out of 20,000 hydrogen atoms: 20,000 × 0.9998 = **19,996** are protium. Check: 19,996 + 4 = 20,000 ✅.\n\n11. For CH₃OH: molar mass = 12 + (4 × 1) + 16 = **32 g/mol**, with 4 g of hydrogen. Mass percentage of H = 4 ÷ 32 × 100 = **12.5%**. In water it is 11.1%, so methanol has a **higher** mass percentage of hydrogen. Check: 12 + 4 + 16 = 32 ✅.\n\n12. (a) 6%: **inside** the explosive range (hazardous). (b) 50%: **inside** (hazardous). (c) 90%: **above** the upper limit in place, but dangerous because mixing with air dilutes it right through the explosive range. (d) 0.5%: **below** the lower limit (too lean to burn). None of them should ever be left near a spark—all hydrogen leaks require immediate ventilation and zero ignition sources. Check: 4 ≤ 6 ≤ 75, 4 ≤ 50 ≤ 75, 90 > 75, 0.5 < 4 ✅.\n\n13. Oxygen: −183 + 273 = **90 K**. Hydrogen: −253 + 273 = **20 K**. Difference: 90 − 20 = **70 K** (or 70 °C, since step sizes are equal on both scales). **Hydrogen** requires much deeper cooling (down to 20 K) to liquefy. Check: −183 − (−253) = 70 ✅.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series and does not displace hydrogen from dilute HCl. (b) **True** ✅. Hydrogen gains oxygen to form H₂O, so it is oxidized (while Fe₃O₄ is reduced). Atom count: Fe = 3, O = 4, H = 8 on both sides. (c) **True** ✅. Protium has 1 proton and 0 neutrons. (d) **False** ❌. Testing hydrogen is strictly a teacher demonstration in a school lab, never done by students at home.\n\n---\n\nChloe tucked the last riddle card back into the box. \"Ms. Ortiz, now I see how it works: solving a chemistry puzzle means checking every single property and equation until only one answer stands.\" Ms. Ortiz glanced out the window. \"And look—the rain has finally stopped! We didn't light a single match today, yet we tested every property of hydrogen.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Choose the best method of making hydrogen for each situation and state one reason: (a) a steel plant that already has coke and steam; (b) a chlor-alkali plant that splits brine to make chlorine and sodium hydroxide; (c) a school lab that has only copper turnings and dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\n2. A teacher plans a demonstration with dilute sulphuric acid. Which of these metals will give hydrogen: magnesium, gold, potassium, iron? For each one that does not, give the reason.\n\n3. Balance and check atoms: Fe + H₂O (steam) → Fe₃O₄ + H₂. Is iron oxidised or reduced here? Why?\n\n4. Combine C + H₂O → CO + H₂ and CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂ into one equation. How many H₂ molecules come from 7 carbon atoms by this two-step route?\n\n5. Riddle: \"I am a colourless, odourless gas. I am made at the positive electrode when water is electrolysed. The volume I make is half the volume of the gas at the other electrode. I relight a glowing splinter.\" Who am I? Explain why only one gas fits.\n\n6. Riddle: \"I am a greenish-yellow gas. I am made together with hydrogen and sodium hydroxide when brine is electrolysed. Hydrogen joins with me in sunlight or on heating to give hydrogen chloride. I am heavier than air.\" Who am I? Write the two equations and give the molar mass check for \"heavier than air\" (Cl = 35.5).\n\n7. A rocket stage carries 4 kg of hydrogen. (a) How much energy is that (120 MJ per kg)? (b) What mass of petrol would give the same energy (45 MJ per kg)? (c) What volume would 4 kg of hydrogen occupy at STP, in m³?\n\n8. A weather balloon of 25 m³ is filled with helium (density 0.18 kg per m³, air 1.29 kg per m³). Find the lift. What lift would hydrogen give, and why is helium still chosen?\n\n9. In the Haber process N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, how many H₂ and how many NH₃ particles correspond to 12 N₂ particles (ideal case)? Name the catalyst and state whether the reaction can go both ways.\n\n10. \"Semi-heavy\" water is HDO (one protium, one deuterium, one oxygen-16). Find its molar mass and the number of neutrons in one molecule. In 20000 hydrogen atoms of ordinary hydrogen, how many are protium (99.98%)?\n\n11. Find the mass percent of hydrogen in methanol, CH₃OH (C = 12, O = 16, H = 1). Is it more or less than in water?\n\n12. Classify each mixture as below, inside or above the flammable range (about 4% to 75% hydrogen in air) and say which is safe to leave near a spark: (a) 6%; (b) 50%; (c) 90%; (d) 0.5%.\n\n13. Liquid oxygen boils at −183 °C and hydrogen at −253 °C. Give both in kelvin, the difference, and say which gas needs more cooling to liquefy.\n\n14. True or false, with a reason: (a) Copper gives hydrogen with dilute hydrochloric acid. (b) In Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂ → 3Fe + 4H₂O hydrogen is oxidised. (c) Ordinary hydrogen (protium) has no neutron. (d) Students may test hydrogen with a lighted splinter at home.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Water gas**: C + H₂O → CO + H₂, phir Bosch step; coke aur steam pehle se maujood hain. (b) **By-product**: brine ke electrolysis mein hydrogen apne aap mil jaati hai, alag se banane ki zaroorat nahi. (c) Copper hydrogen se neeche hai, dilute HCl se **hydrogen nahi** dega: ye lab is kaam ke liye galat samagri rakhti hai (Zn, Mg ya Fe chahiye). Jaanch: teeno mein alag-alag sawaal (A, B, D) ka jawab alag. ✅\n\n2. **Magnesium** (Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂) aur **iron** (Fe + H₂SO₄ → FeSO₄ + H₂) dete hain. **Gold** hydrogen se bahut neeche hai: koi reaction nahi. **Potassium** bahut zyada reactive hai: acid ya paani ke saath bahut tez aur khatarnak, lab mein is kaam ke liye nahi liya jaata. Jaanch: Mg 1, S 1, O 4, H 2 dono taraf ✅.\n\n3. **3Fe + 4H₂O → Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂**. Atom count: baayein Fe 3, H 8, O 4. Daayein Fe 3, O 4, H 8 ✅. Iron ne oxygen paya, to **oxidise** hua (aur steam ke hydrogen ne oxygen khoya, wo H₂ bana). Jaanch: Chapter 7: oxygen milna oxidation ✅.\n\n4. C + H₂O → CO + H₂ aur CO + H₂O → CO₂ + H₂ ko jodo: **C + 2H₂O → CO₂ + 2H₂** (CO dono taraf kat gaya). 1 C se 2 H₂, to 7 C se 7 × 2 = **14 H₂**. Jaanch: baayein C 1, H 4, O 2; daayein C 1, O 2, H 4 ✅.\n\n5. **Oxygen (O₂)**. Electrolysis mein positive electrode par oxygen aur negative par hydrogen banti hai; volume ka ratio 2 : 1, to oxygen ka volume hydrogen ka aadha. Glowing splinter ko relight karna oxygen ka test hai. Sirf ek gas fit hoti hai kyunki \"positive electrode\" aur \"aadha volume\" milkar sirf O₂ ko chunte hain. Jaanch: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂: 2 volume H₂ ke saath 1 volume O₂ ✅.\n\n6. **Chlorine (Cl₂)**. Brine ka electrolysis: 2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂ (Topic 3). Hydrogen ke saath: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (Topic 4). Cl₂ ka molar mass 2 × 35.5 = 71 g, hawa ka lagbhag 29 g, to 71 > 29: hawa se bhaari ✅. Hara-peela rang aur brine wala clue milkar sirf chlorine ko chunte hain (hydrogen aur NaOH ke saath wahi banti hai). Jaanch: 2NaCl + 2H₂O mein Na 2, Cl 2, H 4, O 2 dono taraf ✅; H₂ + Cl₂ mein H 2, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅. (Chlorine zehreeli gas hai: ye sirf kaagaz ka riddle hai.)\n\n7. (a) 4 × 120 = **480 MJ**. (b) 480 ÷ 45 = **10.67 kg** petrol (lagbhag 10.7 kg). (c) 4 kg = 4000 ÷ 2 = 2000 mol, 2000 × 22.4 = 44800 L = **44.8 m³**. Isliye rocket mein hydrogen ko liquid bana kar rakha jaata hai. Jaanch: 10.67 × 45 = 480 ✅.\n\n8. Helium ka lift per m³ = 1.29 − 0.18 = 1.11 kg, to 25 × 1.11 = **27.75 kg**. Hydrogen ka lift per m³ = 1.29 − 0.09 = 1.20, to 25 × 1.20 = **30.0 kg**. Fark 30.0 − 27.75 = 2.25 kg (lagbhag 7.5%). Helium isliye chuni jaati hai kyunki wo **jalti nahi**, hydrogen jwalansheel hai. Jaanch: 27.75 ÷ 30.0 = 0.925 ✅.\n\n9. 12 N₂ ke liye 12 × 3 = **36 H₂** kan, aur 12 × 2 = **24 NH₃** kan. Catalyst: **iron**. Reaction **ulti ho sakti hai** (reversible), aage ki class mein. Jaanch: baayein N 24, H 72; daayein N 24, H 24 × 3 = 72 ✅.\n\n10. HDO ka molar mass = 1 + 2 + 16 = **19 g**. Neutrons: protium 0 + deuterium 1 + oxygen-16 8 = **9**. 20000 × 0.9998 = **19996** protium. Jaanch: 19996 + 4 = 20000 ✅.\n\n11. CH₃OH: C 12 + 4 × 1 + O 16 = **32 g**, hydrogen 4. 4 ÷ 32 × 100 = **12.5%**. Paani mein 11.1%, to methanol mein **zyada**. Jaanch: 12 + 4 + 16 = 32 ✅.\n\n12. (a) 6%: **range ke andar** (khatarnak). (b) 50%: **andar** (khatarnak). (c) 90%: **upar**, par leak hote hi hawa mein milkar range se guzarta hai, isliye surakshit nahi. (d) 0.5%: **neeche**, jalne ke liye kam. Spark ke paas rakhna kisi bhi mein surakshit nahi (hydrogen ka leak kabhi band jagah mein jama na ho); sabse kam khatarnak (d), par asli niyam hai aag door aur hawa ki aavjaahi. Jaanch: 4 ≤ 6 ≤ 75, 4 ≤ 50 ≤ 75, 90 > 75, 0.5 < 4 ✅.\n\n13. Oxygen: −183 + 273 = **90 K**. Hydrogen: −253 + 273 = **20 K**. Fark: 90 − 20 = **70 K** (ya 70 °C, kyunki fark dono scale par barabar). Hydrogen ko zyada thanda karna padta hai (20 K tak), isliye uska liquefaction mushkil hai. Jaanch: −183 − (−253) = 70 ✅.\n\n14. (a) **False** ❌. Copper hydrogen se neeche hai, dilute HCl se hydrogen nahi deta. (b) **True** ✅. Hydrogen ne oxygen liya aur H₂O bani, to oxidise hui (Fe₃O₄ reduce hua). Atom count: Fe 3, O 4, H 8 dono taraf. (c) **True** ✅. Protium: 1 proton, 0 neutron. (d) **False** ❌. Hydrogen ka test sirf teacher ke saath, school lab mein (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo).\n\n---\n\nRiddhi ne aakhri card mod kar copy mein rakha. \"Sir, aaj samajh aaya: riddle hal karna matlab har clue ko jaanchna.\" Tilak sir ne barish ki taraf dekha. \"Aur dekho, barish bhi ruk rahi hai. Aaj ke khel mein ek bhi gas nahi jali, aur seekha sab kuch.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary: Hydrogen",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter Ka Saar: Hydrogen",
            "body": "Here is the entire journey of our Hydrogen chapter at a glance—what hydrogen is, how it is prepared, how it behaves, where it is used, and how to stay safe around it:\n\n| Topic | What we learned | One-line takeaway |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topic 1 | Position, introduction, and isotopes of hydrogen | Hydrogen is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe; H₂ is a diatomic non-metal gas with a single covalent bond; its isotopes are protium, deuterium, and tritium, with deuterium forming heavy water D₂O (20 g/mol). |\n| Topic 2 | Laboratory preparation | Zinc, magnesium, or iron reacts with dilute HCl or H₂SO₄ (teacher demo only) to release hydrogen, while copper does not; H₂ is collected over water or by downward displacement of air in an inverted jar and identified by the squeaky pop test. |\n| Topic 3 | Other ways of making hydrogen | Hydrogen is obtained by electrolyzing water, from steam and active metals (description only), industrially from natural gas or coke with steam plus the Bosch step, as a by-product of brine electrolysis, and as green hydrogen using solar or wind electricity. |\n| Topic 4 | Physical and chemical properties | Colorless, odorless, lightest gas (0.09 g/L), boils at −253 °C (20 K), burns in oxygen (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), combines with Cl₂ and N₂, reduces heated metal oxides, and has a wide 4%–75% explosive range in air. |\n| Topic 5 | Uses, safety, and the future | Used in making ammonia (fertilizers), hydrogenating vegetable oils, cryogenic rockets, fuel cells, and welding; packs huge energy per kg (120 MJ) but takes up large volume; the Hindenburg lesson mandates helium for balloons. |\n| Topic 6 | Practice and chapter summary | Decision trees, reactivity comparisons, gas riddles, redox equations, balloon lift, and fuel-energy calculations tie the entire chapter together. |\n\n**What's next?** In our final Class 9 Chemistry chapter, **\"Atmospheric Pollution,\"** we will answer the question left hanging at the tailpipe: when hydrogen burns, it produces only pure water—so what pollutants do ordinary fossil fuels release into our atmosphere, and how can chemistry help protect our sky?",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ek nazar mein poore chapter ka safar: hydrogen kya hai, kaise banti hai, kaise vyavhaar karti hai, kya kaam aati hai aur kahan savdhani chahiye.\n\n| Topic | Kya seekha | Ek line |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topic 1 | Hydrogen ki position, parichay aur isotopes | Hydrogen sabse halki aur brahmand mein sabse zyada hai, H₂ ek covalent bond wala non-metal gas; protium, deuterium aur tritium uske isotopes hain, aur deuterium se heavy water D₂O (20 g per mol) banta hai. |\n| Topic 2 | Lab mein taiyari | Zinc, magnesium ya iron aur dilute acid se (sirf teacher demo) hydrogen banti hai; copper nahi deta; use paani ke upar ya hawa ko neeche dhakel kar ikattha karte hain aur jalte splinter se pop test hota hai. |\n| Topic 3 | Banane ke aur tareeke | Paani ka electrolysis, active metals aur steam, natural gas aur coke se factory mein, brine ka by-product, aur green hydrogen jo solar ya wind bijli se banti hai. |\n| Topic 4 | Bhautik aur rasayanik gun | Colourless, odourless, sabse halki, −253 °C par ubalti, jalti hai (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), Cl₂ aur N₂ ke saath milti hai aur metal oxides ki reducing agent hai. |\n| Topic 5 | Upyog, safety aur bhavishya | Ammonia, vanaspati, rocket fuel, fuel cell aur welding mein kaam aati hai; per kg energy bahut zyada par jagah bahut chahiye; Hindenburg ki seekh aur hydrogen economy. |\n| Topic 6 | Practice aur chapter ka saar | Method, metal, gas ki pehchaan, equation, balloon aur energy ke hisaab ek saath jode gaye aur riddle card se jaanch ki gayi. |\n\n**Aage kya?** Agle chapter **\"Atmospheric Pollution\"** mein dekhenge: hydrogen jalne par sirf paani deti hai, to baaki fuels hawa mein kya chhod rahe hain?"
          }
