          {
            "heading": "Part A: What is in Clean Air?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Saaf hawa mein kya hota hai?",
            "body": "In our study of matter, we recognized that dry air is not a pure chemical compound, but a homogeneous **mixture** of distinct gaseous elements and compounds. Let us examine the composition of clean, dry air at sea level **by volume**. The values below represent close approximations, as natural air varies slightly depending on geographic location and atmospheric conditions.\n\n| Gas | Approximate Proportion (by volume) |\n|---|---|\n| Nitrogen ($\\text{N}_2$) | 78% |\n| Oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$) | 21% |\n| Argon ($\\text{Ar}$) | 0.93% |\n| Carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$) | ~0.04% (~420 ppm) |\n| Neon, helium, methane, and other noble/trace gases | Trace amounts |\n| Water vapour ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) | Variable: near zero in arid deserts, up to 4% in humid tropical conditions |\n\nTo visualize this composition, imagine 100 identical small blocks representing the total volume of dry air, where each block accounts for exactly 1%.\n\n```\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N O O\n   O O O O O O O O O O\n   O O O O O O O O O A\n\n   N = Nitrogen (78 blocks)     O = Oxygen (21 blocks)\n   A = Argon, carbon dioxide, and all trace gases combined (~1 block)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – A 100-block volume model of clean, dry air: 78 blocks of nitrogen, 21 blocks of oxygen, and approximately 1 single block comprising argon, carbon dioxide, and all trace gases. Water vapour is omitted here because its concentration fluctuates continually with weather.\n\nNotice that carbon dioxide represents only **0.04%** by volume — a tiny fraction of a single block out of a hundred. Yet, as we will explore in Topic 3, even this tiny fraction exerts a tremendous influence on global surface temperatures. This highlights a crucial principle of atmospheric science: **substances present in extremely small concentrations can have massive planetary consequences**. Because percentages become awkward when dealing with such minuscule quantities, scientists use an alternative concentration unit: **parts per million (ppm)**.\n\n---\n\n### What is ppm (Parts Per Million)?\n\n**1 ppm = 1 part in 1,000,000 (one million) parts.** Just as **percentage (%)** denotes parts per hundred ($1 \\div 100$), **ppm** denotes parts per million ($1 \\div 1{,}000{,}000$). The mathematical relationship between them is straightforward:\n\n$$\\text{1\\%} = \\frac{1}{100} = \\frac{10{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = \\text{10,000 ppm}$$\n\nTherefore:\n- To convert **ppm to percentage**, **divide by 10,000**.\n- To convert **percentage to ppm**, **multiply by 10,000**.\n\n**Example 1: Convert an atmospheric $\\text{CO}_2$ concentration of 420 ppm into a percentage.**\n$$\\text{Percentage} = \\frac{420}{10{,}000} = 0.042\\%$$\nThis matches the approximate value of 0.04% noted in our composition table ✅.\n\n**Example 2: Convert the concentration of argon (0.93% by volume) into ppm.**\n$$\\text{Concentration in ppm} = 0.93 \\times 10{,}000 = 9{,}300\\text{ ppm}$$\n\n**Example 3: Calculate the combined concentration of all trace gases in ppm.**\nAfter accounting for nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%), the remaining fraction of air is:\n$$100\\% - 78\\% - 21\\% = 1\\% = 10{,}000\\text{ ppm}$$\nWithin this 10,000 ppm slice, argon contributes 9,300 ppm and carbon dioxide contributes approximately 420 ppm. The remaining noble gases (neon, helium, krypton), methane, and other traces account for:\n$$10{,}000 - 9{,}300 - 420 \\approx 280\\text{ ppm}$$\n\n> **Verification:**\n> $78 + 21 + 0.93 + 0.04 = 99.97\\%$, leaving an unallocated residual of $0.03\\%$ ($300\\text{ ppm}$) ✅. The calculated value of $280\\text{ ppm}$ aligns comfortably within rounding margins.\n\n**Example 4: How much oxygen is present in an average room?**\nConsider a bedroom measuring $3\\text{ m} \\times 4\\text{ m} \\times 2.5\\text{ m}$.\n$$\\text{Volume} = 3 \\times 4 \\times 2.5 = 30\\text{ m}^3$$\nSince $1\\text{ m}^3 = 1{,}000\\text{ L}$, the room contains $30 \\times 1{,}000 = 30{,}000\\text{ L}$ of air.\n- Oxygen volume (21%): $30{,}000 \\times \\frac{21}{100} = 6{,}300\\text{ L}$\n- Nitrogen volume (78%): $30{,}000 \\times 0.78 = 23{,}400\\text{ L}$\n\n> **Verification:**\n> $6{,}300 + 23{,}400 = 29{,}700\\text{ L}$, leaving $300\\text{ L}$ ($1\\%$) for argon and trace gases ($6{,}300 + 23{,}400 + 300 = 30{,}000\\text{ L}$) ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter 2 mein humne dekha tha ki hawa ek **mixture** hai. Ab hum use thoda aur dhyan se, **volume ke hisaab se** dekhte hain. Neeche ke aankde **lagbhag (approximate)** hain, kyunki asli hawa mein jagah aur samay ke saath thoda fark hota hai.\n\n| Gas | Lagbhag matra (volume ke hisaab se) |\n|---|---|\n| Nitrogen (N₂) | 78% |\n| Oxygen (O₂) | 21% |\n| Argon (Ar) | 0.93% |\n| Carbon dioxide (CO₂) | lagbhag 0.04% (lagbhag 420 ppm) |\n| Neon, helium, methane aur doosri gasein | bahut kam (traces) |\n| Paani ki bhaap | badalti rehti hai: registan mein bahut kam, barsaat ke mausam mein zyada |\n\nYe table 100 hisson ki banti hai. Dekho, 100 chhote dibbe maan lo, har dibba hawa ka 1%.\n\n```\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N N N\n   N N N N N N N N O O\n   O O O O O O O O O O\n   O O O O O O O O O A\n\n   N = nitrogen (78 dibbe)    O = oxygen (21 dibbe)\n   A = argon, CO₂ aur baaki sab (lagbhag 1 dibba)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Hawa ka 100 dibbon ka model (volume ke hisaab se, lagbhag): 78 dibbe nitrogen, 21 dibbe oxygen aur lagbhag 1 dibba argon, carbon dioxide aur baaki gason ka. Paani ki bhaap yahan alag se nahi dikhayi gayi, kyunki wo badalti rehti hai.\n\nDhyan do: carbon dioxide sirf **0.04%** hai, yaani 100 dibbon mein ek dibbe ka bhi bahut chhota sa hissa. Phir bhi iska asar bahut bada hota hai (Topic 3 mein dekhenge). Isse ek zaroori baat samajh aati hai: **bahut thodi matra bhi hawa ke liye bahut maayne rakh sakti hai**. Isliye bahut chhoti matra ko naapne ke liye ek naya unit chahiye: **ppm**.\n\n### ppm kya hai?\n\n**1 ppm = 10 lakh (10,00,000) hisson mein 1 hissa.** Ye wahi hai jaise **percent** ka matlab 100 hisson mein kitne. Dono ka rishta:\n\n- 1% = 1 ÷ 100 = 10,000 ÷ 10,00,000, yaani **1% = 10,000 ppm**.\n- Isliye ppm ko percent mein badalne ke liye **10,000 se bhaag** do, aur percent ko ppm mein badalne ke liye **10,000 se gunna** karo.\n\n**Example 1: CO₂ ke 420 ppm ko percent mein badlo.**\n420 ÷ 10,000 = **0.042%**. Ye lagbhag 0.04% hi hai, jaisa table mein tha ✅.\n\n**Example 2: Argon ke 0.93% ko ppm mein badlo.**\n0.93 × 10,000 = **9300 ppm**.\n\n**Example 3: \"Baaki sab\" ko ppm mein samjho.**\nNitrogen aur oxygen ke baad bacha: 100 − 78 − 21 = **1%**. Ye 1 × 10,000 = **10,000 ppm** hua. Isme argon 9300 ppm, aur CO₂ lagbhag 420 ppm. To neon, helium, methane aur baaki sab milkar: 10,000 − 9300 − 420 = **lagbhag 280 ppm**.\n\n> **Jaanch:** 78 + 21 + 0.93 + 0.04 = 99.97, yaani lagbhag 100 aur bacha 0.03% (lagbhag 300 ppm) ✅. 280 ppm ki baat usi ke kareeb hai (aankde lagbhag).\n\n**Example 4: Kamre mein kitni oxygen?**\nEk kamra 3 m × 4 m × 2.5 m ka hai. Aayatan = 3 × 4 × 2.5 = **30 m³**. 1 m³ = 1000 L (Water chapter ki tarah), to 30 m³ = 30,000 L. Oxygen 21% hai, to 30,000 × 21 ÷ 100 = **6300 L**. Aur nitrogen 78% = 30,000 × 0.78 = **23,400 L**.\n\n> **Jaanch:** 6300 + 23,400 = 29,700 L, aur bacha 300 L (1%) ✅ (6300 + 23,400 + 300 = 30,000)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Layers of the Atmosphere",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Atmosphere ki parte",
            "body": "Earth's atmosphere is not a uniform ocean of gas; its density, pressure, temperature, and chemical composition change dramatically with altitude. Atmospheric scientists divide the atmosphere into **five concentric vertical layers**, summarized below with approximate altitude boundaries.\n\n```\n   Altitude\n   (approximate)\n\n   Above 600 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   |  EXOSPHERE: Extremely tenuous    |\n   |  fringe merging into outer space |\n   +----------------------------------+\n   85 km to ~600 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   |  THERMOSPHERE: Very thin air;    |\n   |  satellites and auroras occur    |\n   +----------------------------------+\n   50 km to 85 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   |  MESOSPHERE: Very cold; meteors  |\n   |  burn up here as shooting stars  |\n   +----------------------------------+\n   12 km to 50 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   |  STRATOSPHERE: OZONE LAYER       |\n   |  (concentrated mostly 15–35 km)  |\n   +----------------------------------+\n   0 to 12 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   |  TROPOSPHERE: Where we live;     |\n   |  weather, clouds, convection     |\n   +----------------------------------+\n   =========== Earth's Surface ========\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – The five major layers of Earth's atmosphere (approximate boundaries, not drawn to linear scale): troposphere (0 to 12 km), stratosphere (12 to 50 km, enclosing the ozone layer), mesosphere (50 to 85 km), thermosphere (85 to ~600 km), and the exosphere merging into space.\n\nKey features of each layer include:\n- **Troposphere:** The lowest and densest atmospheric layer, containing roughly 75–80% of the entire mass of the atmosphere. Temperature steadily **decreases with altitude** in this layer (which is why mountain summits are much colder than sea-level valleys). Almost all clouds, weather systems, atmospheric convection currents, and surface biological life exist here. Commercial passenger aircraft generally cruise near the top of the troposphere, around 10 to 12 km.\n- **Stratosphere:** Extends from 12 km to roughly 50 km. It houses the critical **stratospheric ozone layer** (primarily between 15 km and 35 km), which absorbs harmful ultraviolet (UV) solar radiation (studied in detail in Topic 4).\n- **Mesosphere:** Extends from 50 km to 85 km. Temperatures drop to extreme lows (reaching $-90^\\circ\\text{C}$), and incoming space debris (meteoroids) burns up upon collision with gas molecules, creating visible meteors.\n- **Thermosphere and Exosphere:** The uppermost zones where gas molecules are spaced vastly apart and air density approaches a vacuum, gradually blending into interplanetary space.\n\nIt is vital to recognize that the air we breathe, pollute, and live in belongs entirely to the **troposphere**. Vertical air movement via thermal convection ([heat-and-temperature-part6.md](../../physics/heat-and-temperature/heat-and-temperature-part6.md)) occurs within this active layer, which also plays an essential role in winter temperature inversions and smog formation (Topic 5).\n\n> **Check Your Understanding:** \"The protective ozone layer resides in the troposphere.\" ❌ Incorrect. The protective ozone layer resides in the **stratosphere** (concentrated mostly between 15 km and 35 km).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Hawa Earth ke chaaron taraf ek **moti parat** hai, par wo ek jaisi nahi hai. Oonchai badalne par uska pressure, temperature aur gun badalte hain. Hum atmosphere ko **paanch parton** mein baantte hain. Neeche ki oonchaiyaan **lagbhag (approximate)** hain.\n\n```\n   oonchai\n   (lagbhag)\n\n   600 km ke upar\n   +----------------------------------+\n   I  EXOSPHERE: bahut hi patli hawa, I\n   I  space mein milti hui            I\n   +----------------------------------+\n   85 km se upar, lagbhag 600 km tak\n   +----------------------------------+\n   I  THERMOSPHERE: bahut patli hawa  I\n   I  (satellite yahan ghoomte hain)  I\n   +----------------------------------+\n   50 km se 85 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   I  MESOSPHERE: bahut thandi,       I\n   I  ulkaayein yahan jalti dikhti    I\n   +----------------------------------+\n   12 km se 50 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   I  STRATOSPHERE: OZONE LAYER       I\n   I  (zyadatar 15 se 35 km ke beech) I\n   +----------------------------------+\n   0 se 12 km\n   +----------------------------------+\n   I  TROPOSPHERE: hum yahin rehte    I\n   I  hain; baadal, baarish, mausam   I\n   +----------------------------------+\n   ======== Earth ki sathah ==========\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Atmosphere ki paanch parte (lagbhag oonchaiyaan, anupaat sahi nahi): troposphere (0 se 12 km), stratosphere (12 se 50 km, jisme ozone layer hai), mesosphere (50 se 85 km), thermosphere (85 km se lagbhag 600 km) aur sabse upar exosphere.\n\nKuch seedhi baatein:\n\n- **Troposphere** mein hawa sabse ghani hai: poori hawa ka bahut bada hissa isi mein hai. Yahan oonchai badhne par **temperature girta hai** (isi liye pahaad thande hote hain). Mausam, baadal aur baarish yahin hote hain. Hamara jeevan aur lagbhag saara pradushan yahin hota hai. Hawai jahaaz lagbhag 10 se 12 km par chalte hain, yaani troposphere ke upari hisse mein.\n- **Stratosphere** mein **ozone layer** hai (zyadatar lagbhag 15 se 35 km par). Wo Sun ki harmful UV kirnon ko rokti hai (Topic 4 mein).\n- **Mesosphere** mein bahut thand hoti hai, aur aasmaan se girte patthar (ulkaayein) yahin jalte dikhte hain.\n- **Thermosphere** aur **exosphere** mein hawa bahut patli ho jaati hai aur space mein dheere dheere ghul jaati hai.\n\nYe baat zaroori hai ki hum jis hawa mein saans lete hain aur jise ganda karte hain, wo **troposphere** hai. Hawa ka \"garam hokar upar uthna\" (convection) isi parat mein hota hai: [heat-and-temperature-part6.md](../../physics/heat-and-temperature/heat-and-temperature-part6.md). Is wajah se sardi ki dhundh ka raaz bhi isi se judta hai (Topic 5 mein).\n\n> **Jaanch:** \"Ozone layer troposphere mein hai.\" ❌ Nahi. Ozone layer **stratosphere** mein hai (zyadatar lagbhag 15 se 35 km par)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: What is Air Pollution? Primary and Secondary Pollutants",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Pradushan kya hai? Primary aur secondary",
            "body": "Air naturally contains small concentrations of substances like carbon dioxide and suspended dust. What then defines pollution?\n\n> **An air pollutant** is any substance introduced into the atmosphere in a location or at a concentration that causes undesirable effects, damaging living organisms, ecosystems, human health, or material structures.\n\nCarbon dioxide is a natural, necessary component of pristine air; however, when human activities dramatically inflate its concentration, it drives global climate disruption. Conversely, substances like carbon monoxide ($\\text{CO}$) or sulfur dioxide ($\\text{SO}_2$) are fundamentally toxic contaminants that have no place in healthy breathing air. Therefore, a pollutant is characterized not merely by its intrinsic toxicity, but by its presence in **harmful concentrations and misplaced contexts**.\n\n---\n\n### Primary and Secondary Pollutants\n\nAtmospheric pollutants are classified into two major categories depending on how they enter the atmosphere:\n\n| Category | Formation Mechanism | Typical Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Primary Pollutant** | Emitted directly from an identifiable source into the atmosphere | Carbon monoxide ($\\text{CO}$), sulfur dioxide ($\\text{SO}_2$), nitric oxide ($\\text{NO}$), soot / elemental carbon |\n| **Secondary Pollutant** | Formed within the atmosphere when primary pollutants chemically react with one another, with sunlight, or with natural atmospheric components | Ground-level ozone ($\\text{O}_3$), peroxyacyl nitrates (PAN), acid precipitation droplets ($\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$, $\\text{HNO}_3$) |\n\nA helpful cooking analogy: adding raw sugar directly to warm tea is like emitting a **primary** substance — it enters the cup directly as sugar. Baking a golden-brown caramel cake from flour, milk, and sugar involves complex chemical transformations that produce a entirely **new** compound — akin to a **secondary** pollutant. Because secondary pollutants form dynamically in moving air masses under solar irradiation, identifying and regulating their precise origin is often a formidable scientific challenge.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Ground-level ozone** is a toxic secondary pollutant that damages respiratory tissues and crops. It must **never** be confused with the beneficial **stratospheric ozone** that shields Earth from harmful solar UV rays. Both possess the exact same chemical formula ($\\text{O}_3$), but their environmental role depends entirely on their altitude. As atmospheric scientists often state: *\"Good up high, bad nearby.\"*",
            "bodyHinglish": "Hawa mein aisi kai cheezein hain jo **hamesha thodi matra mein hoti hain**, jaise CO₂. To pradushan ki sahi pehchan kya hai?\n\n> **Pollutant** wo cheez hai jo hawa mein **galat jagah ya galat matra mein** aa gayi ho aur jeevon ya cheezon ko **nuksaan** pahunchaye.\n\nDekho, CO₂ saaf hawa ka hissa hai, par agar wo bahut badh jaye to dikkat. Aur CO (carbon monoxide) to saaf hawa mein hona hi nahi chahiye. Isliye pollutant ka matlab \"hamesha zehar\" nahi hai, balki **\"nuksaan karne layak matra mein\"**.\n\n### Primary aur secondary pollutants\n\n| Prakaar | Kaise banta hai | Udaharan |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Primary pollutant** | Seedhe kisi source se hawa mein aata hai | Carbon monoxide (CO), sulphur dioxide (SO₂), nitric oxide (NO), soot (dhuen ke kala kan) |\n| **Secondary pollutant** | Hawa mein primary pollutants ke aapas mein ya sunlight se milne par banta hai | Zameen ke paas ka ozone, baarish mein ghula acid |\n\nEk seedhi tulna: **primary** ka matlab aapne kheer ke saath chini daali, to meetha **seedha** mila. **Secondary** ka matlab doodh, chini aur chawal pakane par bani kheer: kuch **naya** bana. Secondary pollutant hawa mein ban jaata hai, isliye uska source dhundhna kathin hota hai.\n\nDhyan do: **zameen ke paas ka (ground-level) ozone** ek secondary pollutant hai aur saans ke liye nuksaandeh hai. Ye wo achha ozone **nahi** hai jo stratosphere mein hamein UV se bachata hai. Dono ka formula O₃ hi hai (Chapter 3), par jagah alag hai, to asar bhi alag. Isse Topic 4 mein dekhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Where Does Air Pollution Come From?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Pradushan kahan se aata hai?",
            "body": "Sources of atmospheric pollution fall into two broad classifications: **natural sources** and **anthropogenic (human-made) sources**.\n\n| Source Type | Description and Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Natural Sources** | Volcanic eruptions (emitting volcanic ash, $\\text{SO}_2$, and $\\text{CO}_2$), desert dust storms, biogenic marsh gases (natural methane), pollen grains, and lightning-ignited forest fires. |\n| **Anthropogenic Sources** | Motor vehicle exhaust (automobiles, trucks, aircraft), fossil-fuel power plants (burning coal, fuel oil, natural gas), industrial manufacturing facilities, crop residue (stubble) burning, construction and demolition dust, domestic solid-fuel stoves, and municipal open-waste incineration. |\n\nWhile natural pollution events have occurred throughout geological history, **anthropogenic emissions have drastically outpaced natural background levels over the past two centuries**. The encouraging takeaway is that because human activities generate the majority of modern air pollution, deliberate human choices, cleaner technologies, and sensible regulations can effectively eliminate it.\n\nA critical chemical insight: many common combustion processes generate multiple pollutants simultaneously. When fossil fuels (such as coal, diesel, or petrol) burn:\n- **Complete combustion** produces carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$) and water vapour ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).\n- **Incomplete combustion** (due to insufficient oxygen) yields lethal carbon monoxide ($\\text{CO}$) and fine unburnt carbon particles known as soot ([chemical-changes-and-reactions-part6.md](../chemical-changes-and-reactions/chemical-changes-and-reactions-part6.md)).\n- Inherent chemical impurities, such as sulfur in raw coal or diesel, oxidize directly into sulfur dioxide ($\\text{SO}_2$).\n\n> **Check Your Understanding:** \"Air pollution is caused entirely by human activities.\" ❌ Incorrect. Natural phenomena such as volcanic eruptions, dust storms, and wildfires generate considerable emissions. However, modern persistent urban and industrial pollution is predominantly human-made.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Source do tarah ke hote hain: **prakritik (natural)** aur **insaani (human)**.\n\n| Prakaar | Udaharan |\n|---|---|\n| **Prakritik** | Jwalamukhi ka dhuan aur raakh, dhool bhari aandhi, jungle ki aag |\n| **Insaani** | Gaadiyon ka dhuan, thermal power plants (koyle se bijli), factories, parali jalana (fasal ka bacha hissa), nirman ki dhool, chulhe par khana, kachra jalana |\n\nPrakritik sources hamesha se hain, par **insaani sources se aaj bahut zyada pradushan** hota hai, aur use ghataya ja sakta hai. Ye baat hamein aashavaadi rakhti hai: jo hamne badhaya, use hum kam bhi kar sakte hain.\n\nEk dhyan dene wali baat: kai sources ek hi cheez ko alag tarah se nikalte hain. Jaise **koyla ya petrol-diesel jalane par** CO₂, CO, SO₂, nitrogen ke oxides aur soot nikalte hain. Chapter 7 mein humne dekha tha ki **poora jalne** par CO₂ banti hai aur **adhoora jalne** par CO aur soot bhi banta hai: [chemical-changes-and-reactions-part6.md](../chemical-changes-and-reactions/chemical-changes-and-reactions-part6.md).\n\n> **Jaanch:** \"Pradushan sirf insaan ki wajah se hota hai.\" ❌ Nahi. Jwalamukhi, dhool bhari aandhi aur jungle ki aag jaise prakritik sources bhi hain. Par aaj ka zyada pradushan insaani sources se hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Particulate Matter: PM10 and PM2.5",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Particulate matter: PM10 aur PM2.5",
            "body": "The visible component of airborne haze consists largely of **particulate matter (PM)**: microscopic solid particles and liquid droplets suspended stably in the atmosphere. Scientists categorize particulate matter by its **aerodynamic diameter**, measured in **micrometres ($\\mu\\text{m}$)**.\n\n$$1\\text{ micrometre } (\\mu\\text{m}) = 1{,}000\\text{ nanometres } (\\text{nm}) = 10^{-6}\\text{ m} = 0.001\\text{ mm}$$\n\n- **PM10:** Inhalable coarse particles with an aerodynamic diameter of **$10\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ or less**. Examples include crushed road dust, pollen grains, and mechanical grinding debris.\n- **PM2.5:** Fine inhalable particles with an aerodynamic diameter of **$2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ or less**. These originate primarily from combustion sources, such as vehicle exhaust, power station emissions, and forest fires. PM2.5 is a subset of PM10.\n\nTo comprehend just how tiny these particles are, consider that an average human hair has a diameter of roughly **$70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$**. The diagram below illustrates this scale, where each `#` symbol represents an increment of $2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$.\n\n```\n   Human hair (70 µm):\n   [############################]   28 markers\n\n   PM10 (10 µm):\n   [####]                            4 markers\n\n   PM2.5 (2.5 µm):\n   [#]                               1 marker\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Size comparison of PM2.5 and PM10 relative to the cross-section of a typical human hair ($~70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$). While a human hair spans 28 markers, a PM10 particle spans only 4 markers, and a PM2.5 particle spans just 1 single marker. Roughly 28 PM2.5 particles placed side by side would span the width of a single strand of hair. (Scale: 1 marker `#` = $2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$.)\n\n**Example 5: How many times thinner than a human hair is a PM2.5 particle?**\n$$\\frac{70\\ \\mu\\text{m}}{2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}} = 28\\text{ times thinner}$$\nA single PM2.5 particle is approximately **28 to 30 times thinner** than a strand of human hair.\n\n**Example 6: How many times thinner than a human hair is a PM10 particle?**\n$$\\frac{70\\ \\mu\\text{m}}{10\\ \\mu\\text{m}} = 7\\text{ times thinner}$$\n\n**Example 7: Express the diameter of a $2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ particle in nanometres and metres.**\n- In nanometres: $2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m} \\times 1{,}000 = 2{,}500\\text{ nm}$\n- In metres: $2.5 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ m}$\n\n> **Verification:**\n> $28 \\times 2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m} = 70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ ✅ and $7 \\times 10\\ \\mu\\text{m} = 70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ ✅.\n\nBecause these particles are so microscopic, gravity acts on them very weakly compared to air resistance. They remain **suspended in ambient air for days or weeks**, drifting over hundreds of kilometres. When inhaled, coarse particles are trapped by nasal hair and mucus, but ultrafine PM2.5 particles penetrate deep into lung alveoli and can even cross directly into the bloodstream (explored further in Topic 5).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Dhundh mein jo cheez hum **dekh** sakte hain, uska bada hissa **particulate matter (PM)** hota hai: hawa mein tairte dhool, soot aur boondon ke bahut chhote kan. Inhe unki **chaudai (diameter)** se naapte hain, **micrometre (µm)** mein.\n\n**1 micrometre = 1000 nanometre (nm) = 10⁻⁶ m.** Yaani 1 µm, 1 mm ka ek hazaarvan hissa hai.\n\n- **PM10:** wo kan jinki chaudai **10 µm tak** hai.\n- **PM2.5:** wo kan jinki chaudai **2.5 µm tak** hai. Ye PM10 ka hi ek **chhota** hissa hai.\n\nInsaan ke baal ki chaudai lagbhag **70 µm** hoti hai (lagbhag, baal-baal mein fark hota hai). Dekho PM2.5 us se kitna chhota hai. Figure mein 1 chinha (#) = 2.5 µm maana gaya hai.\n\n```\n   Baal (70 µm):\n   [############################]   28 chinha\n\n   PM10 (10 µm):\n   [####]                           4 chinha\n\n   PM2.5 (2.5 µm):\n   [#]                              1 chinha\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – PM2.5 aur PM10 ki tulna insaan ke baal se (lagbhag): jahan baal ki chaudai 28 chinha hai, wahan PM10 sirf 4 chinha aur PM2.5 sirf 1 chinha hai. Yaani 28 PM2.5 kan ek ke baad ek rakhein to lagbhag ek baal ki chaudai banti hai. (Scale: 1 chinha = 2.5 µm.)\n\n**Example 5: Baal PM2.5 se kitna chauda?**\n70 ÷ 2.5 = **28 guna**. Yaani PM2.5 baal se lagbhag **28 guna** patla hai (lagbhag 30 guna kehna bhi theek hai).\n\n**Example 6: Baal PM10 se kitna chauda?**\n70 ÷ 10 = **7 guna**.\n\n**Example 7: PM2.5 ki chaudai nanometre mein?**\n2.5 µm × 1000 = **2500 nm**. Aur meter mein: 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m.\n\n> **Jaanch:** 28 × 2.5 = 70 ✅ aur 7 × 10 = 70 ✅.\n\nItne patle kan **tairte rehte hain**, bahut der tak hawa mein rehte hain aur saans ke saath **gehraai tak** phephdon mein pahunch sakte hain. Aise kan sirf aankh se alag-alag nahi dikhte, bas dhundh ki tarah dikhte hain. Kaise hote hain asar, wo Topic 5 mein dekhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Reading the Air Quality Index (AQI)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: AQI padhna",
            "body": "Returning to the digital advisory board Leo observed at his school entrance: the **Air Quality Index (AQI)** is a standardized numerical scale that translates complex atmospheric measurements of multiple pollutants into a simple, intuitive public health score. Environmental protection agencies monitor concentrations of major pollutants — including PM2.5, PM10, ground-level ozone, $\\text{SO}_2$, $\\text{NO}_2$, and $\\text{CO}$ — and compute the AQI based on the pollutant exhibiting the highest relative health risk.\n\nA fundamental rule to remember: **the higher the AQI number, the greater the air pollution and the higher the health hazard.** The standard international/national AQI categories are structured as follows:\n\n| AQI Range | Health Category | Public Health Implications |\n|---|---|---|\n| **0 to 50** | **Good** | Air quality is considered satisfactory; air pollution poses little or no risk. |\n| **51 to 100** | **Satisfactory / Moderate** | Acceptable air quality; a very small number of unusually sensitive individuals may experience minor respiratory irritation. |\n| **101 to 200** | **Moderate / Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups** | May cause breathing discomfort to children, the elderly, and individuals with chronic lung or heart ailments (e.g., asthma). |\n| **201 to 300** | **Poor / Unhealthy** | Prolonged outdoor exposure may cause breathing discomfort to the general public and more noticeable discomfort to sensitive groups. |\n| **301 to 400** | **Very Poor / Very Unhealthy** | Respiratory illness warnings triggered; prolonged exertion outdoors carries substantial risk for everyone. |\n| **401 to 500** | **Severe / Hazardous** | Emergency health advisory conditions; entire population is likely to be affected. Outdoor physical exertion should be strictly avoided. |\n\n**Example 8: Classify the AQI reading of 312 displayed on Leo's school monitor.**\nAn AQI reading of 312 falls within the bracket of **301 to 400**, placing it squarely in the **Very Poor** category.\n\n**Example 9: Identify the category for the following regional AQI readings:**\n- **AQI 56:** Falls between 51 and 100 $\\rightarrow$ **Satisfactory**.\n- **AQI 187:** Falls between 101 and 200 $\\rightarrow$ **Moderate**.\n- **AQI 265:** Falls between 201 and 300 $\\rightarrow$ **Poor**.\n- **AQI 412:** Falls between 401 and 500 $\\rightarrow$ **Severe**.\n\n**Example 10: Yesterday's AQI was 156; today's AQI is 312. By what factor did it change, and how did the category shift?**\n$$\\text{Factor of change} = \\frac{312}{156} = 2.0\\text{ (doubled)}$$\nThe index value doubled, shifting the health classification from **Moderate** (101–200) to **Very Poor** (301–400). Note that because AQI formulas use segmented piecewise linear calculations, a doubled index does not necessarily mean pollutant mass concentrations doubled exactly; it signifies that public health risk has escalated severely.\n\n> **Verification:**\n> $156 \\times 2 = 312$ ✅. The six category spans sum to $50 + 50 + 100 + 100 + 100 + 100 = 500$ ✅.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Pay close attention to boundary thresholds. An AQI reading of 200 is classified as **Moderate**, but an increase of just one point to 201 pushes the category into **Poor**. Similarly, 50 is **Good**, while 51 enters **Satisfactory**.\n\nDr. Higgins concluded: \"Make it a daily habit to check the AQI board, Leo. Knowing the number empowers us to adjust our daily outdoor sports, wear appropriate protective masks when necessary, and make informed choices to safeguard our health.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab Kshitij ke gate ke board par lautte hain. **AQI (Air Quality Index)** ek **number** hai jo hawa ki halat batata hai. Kai pradooshakon (PM2.5, PM10, ozone, SO₂, CO, aadi) ko naap kar sabse kharab wale se AQI banta hai. **Zyada number, zyada kharab hawa.** India ki categories neeche hain.\n\n| AQI ki range | Category | Matlab (aam bhasha mein) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 se 50 | Good (achhi) | Saaf hawa, koi dikkat nahi |\n| 51 se 100 | Satisfactory (santoshjanak) | Zyadatar log theek, bahut sensitive logon ko halki takleef |\n| 101 se 200 | Moderate (madhyam) | Phephdon ya dil ke rogiyon, bachchon aur buzurgon ko takleef |\n| 201 se 300 | Poor (kharab) | Zyada der bahar rehne par aam logon ko bhi takleef |\n| 301 se 400 | Very Poor (bahut kharab) | Lambe samay tak bahar rehne par beemari ka khatra |\n| 401 se 500 | Severe (gambhir) | Swasth logon par bhi asar; bahar ki gatividhiyan kam rakho |\n\n**Example 8: Kshitij ke gate ka AQI 312 hai. Category?**\n312, **301 se 400** ke beech hai, to category **Very Poor**.\n\n**Example 9: Alag-alag readings.**\n- AQI 56: 51 se 100 ke beech, **Satisfactory**.\n- AQI 187: 101 se 200 ke beech, **Moderate**.\n- AQI 265: 201 se 300 ke beech, **Poor**.\n- AQI 412: 401 se 500 ke beech, **Severe**.\n\n**Example 10: Kal 156 tha, aaj 312.**\n312 ÷ 156 = **2 guna**. Hawa ka AQI **double** ho gaya, aur category **Moderate se Very Poor** ho gayi (156 Moderate mein hai, kyunki 101 se 200 ke beech hai). Dhyan do: AQI ek **index** hai, isliye \"doguna AQI\" ka matlab ye zaroori nahi ki pradooshan ki asli matra theek doguna hai. Bas itna ki halat kaafi bigdi.\n\n> **Jaanch:** 156 × 2 = 312 ✅. Chhe categories ki range: 50 + 50 + 100 + 100 + 100 + 100 = 500 ✅ (0 se 500).\n\n**Dhyan rakho:** categories ke beech ki seema par dhyan do. 200 **Moderate** hai, par 201 **Poor** ho jaata hai. 50 **Good** hai, 51 **Satisfactory**.\n\nAb Kshitij ko samajh aaya ki subah ki dhundh ka board kya kehta hai. Wadekar sir ne kaha: \"Is board ko roz dekhne ki aadat daalo. AQI ke saath ye bhi dekho ki aaj bahar kitna rukna theek hai.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Believing oxygen is the most abundant gas in Earth's atmosphere**\n   Nitrogen is by far the most abundant gas, accounting for roughly **78%** of clean, dry air by volume. Oxygen makes up approximately **21%**.\n\n2. **Mistake 2: Assuming carbon dioxide is solely a pollutant and completely absent from clean air**\n   Natural clean air contains roughly 0.04% (~420 ppm) carbon dioxide, which is vital for plant photosynthesis and maintaining Earth's habitable temperature. It becomes a critical environmental threat when anthropogenic emissions elevate its concentration far above historical ecological baselines.\n\n3. **Mistake 3: Treating ppm and percentage as interchangeable quantities**\n   While both express fractions of a whole, their magnitudes differ by four orders of magnitude: $1\\% = 10{,}000\\text{ ppm}$. Always divide ppm by 10,000 to convert to percentage.\n\n4. **Mistake 4: Imagining PM2.5 particles are visible specks just slightly smaller than human hair**\n   PM2.5 particles are microscopic and completely invisible as individual specks to the naked eye. At $2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$, they are approximately 28 to 30 times thinner than an average strand of human hair.\n\n5. **Mistake 5: Assuming ozone is uniformly hazardous wherever it exists**\n   Ozone in the stratosphere forms an indispensable natural shield against lethal solar ultraviolet radiation. Conversely, ozone at ground level in the troposphere is a hazardous secondary pollutant that damages lung tissue.\n\n6. **Mistake 6: Assuming a higher AQI value indicates better air quality**\n   The AQI scale operates in reverse of school test marks: a **higher number indicates dirtier, more hazardous air**. An AQI below 50 represents clean air; an AQI above 300 represents dangerous pollution.\n\n7. **Mistake 7: Assuming air pollution arises exclusively from industrial factory chimneys**\n   Air pollution stems from diverse sources including motor vehicles, power stations, construction dust, agricultural burning, household biomass combustion, and natural events like wildfires and volcanoes.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Hawa mein sabse zyada oxygen hoti hai\"**\nNahi. Sabse zyada **nitrogen** (78%) hai, oxygen sirf 21% hai.\n\n**Galti 2: \"CO₂ pradooshak hai, isliye saaf hawa mein bilkul nahi hoti\"**\nNahi. Saaf hawa mein bhi CO₂ lagbhag 0.04% hoti hai. Dikkat tab hai jab matra aur badhti jaye (Topic 3).\n\n**Galti 3: \"ppm aur percent ek hi cheez hain\"**\nDono ek hi cheez naapte hain (hissa), par 1% = 10,000 ppm.\n\n**Galti 4: \"PM2.5 baal se thoda chhota hota hai\"**\nPM2.5 baal se lagbhag 28 guna patla hota hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Ozone hamesha harmful hai\"**\nStratosphere ka ozone hamein bachata hai. Zameen ke paas ka ozone nuksaandeh pollutant hai.\n\n**Galti 6: \"AQI ka bada number matlab achhi hawa\"**\nUlta. Number jitna bada, hawa utni kharab.\n\n**Galti 7: \"Pradushan sirf factories se hota hai\"**\nKai sources hain: gaadiyaan, power plants, parali, dhool, chulha, kachra jalana, aur prakritik sources bhi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Composition of Clean Dry Air (by volume):** Approximately 78% nitrogen ($\\text{N}_2$), 21% oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$), 0.93% argon ($\\text{Ar}$), ~0.04% (~420 ppm) carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$), trace noble gases, and variable water vapour.\n- **Concentration Conversion:** $1\\% = 10{,}000\\text{ ppm}$. To convert ppm to %, divide by 10,000; to convert % to ppm, multiply by 10,000.\n- **Atmospheric Layers:** Troposphere (0–12 km, where weather occurs and humans live), stratosphere (12–50 km, enclosing the protective ozone layer), mesosphere (50–85 km, where meteors burn), thermosphere (85–600 km), and exosphere (>600 km).\n- **Pollutants:** Any harmful substance present in the wrong place or at an elevated concentration. **Primary pollutants** are emitted directly from sources ($\\text{CO}$, $\\text{SO}_2$, $\\text{NO}$, soot); **secondary pollutants** form in the air via chemical reactions (ground-level ozone, atmospheric acids).\n- **Sources of Air Pollution:** Natural (volcanic eruptions, desert dust storms, forest fires) and anthropogenic (automobiles, thermal power stations, factories, agricultural burning, construction).\n- **Particulate Matter:** PM10 ($\\le 10\\ \\mu\\text{m}$) and PM2.5 ($\\le 2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$). PM2.5 particles are ~28 times thinner than a human hair (~$70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$) and penetrate deep into pulmonary alveoli.\n- **Air Quality Index (AQI):** A standardized scale from 0 to 500: 0–50 (Good), 51–100 (Satisfactory), 101–200 (Moderate), 201–300 (Poor), 301–400 (Very Poor), and 401–500 (Severe). Higher values indicate dirtier air.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Saaf hawa (volume): lagbhag 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.93% argon, lagbhag 0.04% (420 ppm) CO₂, thode traces, aur badalti paani ki bhaap.\n- 1% = 10,000 ppm. ppm se percent: 10,000 se bhaag; percent se ppm: 10,000 se gunna.\n- Atmosphere ki parte: troposphere (lagbhag 12 km tak), stratosphere (lagbhag 50 km tak, ozone layer isme), mesosphere (lagbhag 85 km tak), thermosphere, exosphere.\n- Pollutant: galat jagah ya galat matra mein aayi cheez jo nuksaan kare. Primary seedhe nikalta hai (CO, SO₂, NO, soot); secondary hawa mein banta hai (zameen ke paas ozone, acid).\n- Sources: prakritik (jwalamukhi, dhool bhari aandhi, jungle ki aag) aur insaani (gaadiyaan, power plants, factories, parali, nirman dhool, chulha, kachra jalana).\n- PM10 (10 µm tak) aur PM2.5 (2.5 µm tak). PM2.5 baal (lagbhag 70 µm) se lagbhag 28 guna patla. 1 µm = 1000 nm = 10⁻⁶ m.\n- AQI: 0 se 50 Good, 51 se 100 Satisfactory, 101 se 200 Moderate, 201 se 300 Poor, 301 se 400 Very Poor, 401 se 500 Severe."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Atmospheric helium has a concentration of approximately 5 ppm, and methane has a concentration of about 1.9 ppm. Express each concentration as a percentage by volume.\n\n2. A science laboratory measures $10\\text{ m} \\times 6\\text{ m} \\times 3\\text{ m}$. Using the standard atmospheric composition provided in this topic, determine:\n   (a) The total volume of the laboratory in $\\text{m}^3$.\n   (b) The volume of oxygen contained in the room in $\\text{m}^3$ and in litres.\n   (c) The volume of nitrogen contained in the room in $\\text{m}^3$.\n\n3. Two airborne particles are sampled: Particle A has a diameter of $5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$, while Particle B has a diameter of $0.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$.\n   (a) How many times thinner than an average human hair ($70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$) is each particle?\n   (b) Classify each particle as PM10, PM2.5, or both.\n\n4. Assign the appropriate health category to each of the following AQI values: 45, 95, 150, 200, 201, 320, 455.\n\n5. Classify each of the following as a primary or secondary pollutant, providing a concise scientific reason for each:\n   (a) Carbon monoxide released from a motor scooter exhaust.\n   (b) Soot particles emerging from a diesel delivery van.\n   (c) Ground-level ozone formed under bright sunlight from hydrocarbon and nitrogen oxide emissions.\n   (d) Sulfur dioxide escaping from the smokestack of a coal-fired power plant.\n\n6. Identify the specific layer of Earth's atmosphere in which each of the following is found:\n   (a) A long-haul commercial passenger jet cruising at an altitude of 10 km.\n   (b) The summit of Mount Everest at approximately 8.8 km.\n   (c) The highest concentration of the protective ozone shield at 25 km.\n   (d) A meteoroid vaporizing as a shooting star at 70 km.\n\n7. Classify each of the following as a natural or anthropogenic source of air pollution:\n   (a) A lightning-sparked forest fire in a national park.\n   (b) An active brick kiln kiln emitting black smoke.\n   (c) A volcanic eruption releasing sulfurous ash.\n   (d) A thermal power plant burning pulverized coal.\n   (e) A desert dust storm blowing across arid plains.\n   (f) An open municipal landfill on fire.\n\n8. State whether each statement is True or False. Correct any false statements:\n   (a) Earth's atmosphere consists of approximately 21% nitrogen by volume.\n   (b) A concentration of 1% is mathematically equivalent to 10,000 ppm.\n   (c) An AQI reading of 120 is classified in the Poor category.\n   (d) Daily weather events such as clouds and rain occur almost entirely within the troposphere.\n\n9. In an urban metropolis, the AQI recorded on Monday was 180, rising to 360 by Wednesday.\n   (a) By what numerical factor did the AQI change?\n   (b) In which health categories do the two readings fall?\n   (c) What practical personal health precautions should students adopt on Wednesday?\n\n10. Carry out the following unit conversions:\n    (a) $1\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ to nanometres ($\\text{nm}$).\n    (b) $0.1\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ to nanometres ($\\text{nm}$).\n    (c) $4\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ to metres ($\\text{m}$) in scientific notation.\n    (d) $25\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ to millimetres ($\\text{mm}$).\n\n11. Inside a tightly sealed, crowded lecture hall, the carbon dioxide concentration climbs to 1,200 ppm.\n    (a) How many litres of carbon dioxide are present in each cubic metre ($1\\text{ m}^3$) of this indoor air?\n    (b) Express this volume in millilitres ($\\text{mL}$).\n    (c) Express 1,200 ppm as a percentage by volume.\n\n---\n\n### Answers\n\n1. To convert ppm to percentage, divide by 10,000:\n   - Helium: $\\frac{5}{10{,}000} = \\mathbf{0.0005\\%}$\n   - Methane: $\\frac{1.9}{10{,}000} = \\mathbf{0.00019\\%}$\n   *Verification:* $0.0005 \\times 10{,}000 = 5\\text{ ppm}$ ✅; $0.00019 \\times 10{,}000 = 1.9\\text{ ppm}$ ✅.\n\n2. (a) Total volume: $V = 10\\text{ m} \\times 6\\text{ m} \\times 3\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{180\\text{ m}^3}$.\n   (b) Oxygen (21% by volume):\n   $$V_{\\text{oxygen}} = 180 \\times \\frac{21}{100} = \\mathbf{37.8\\text{ m}^3}$$\n   In litres: $37.8 \\times 1{,}000 = \\mathbf{37{,}800\\text{ L}}$.\n   (c) Nitrogen (78% by volume):\n   $$V_{\\text{nitrogen}} = 180 \\times \\frac{78}{100} = \\mathbf{140.4\\text{ m}^3}$$\n   *Verification:* $37.8 + 140.4 = 178.2\\text{ m}^3$, leaving $1.8\\text{ m}^3$ ($1\\%$ of 180) for argon and trace gases ✅.\n\n3. (a) Comparison with human hair ($70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$):\n   - Particle A ($5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$): $\\frac{70}{5} = \\mathbf{14\\text{ times thinner}}$.\n   - Particle B ($0.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$): $\\frac{70}{0.5} = \\mathbf{140\\text{ times thinner}}$.\n   (b) Classification:\n   - Particle A ($5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$) is $\\le 10\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ but $> 2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$, so it is classified strictly as **PM10**.\n   - Particle B ($0.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$) is $\\le 2.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$, so it is classified as **both PM2.5 and PM10** (since all PM2.5 particles are a subset of PM10).\n   *Verification:* $14 \\times 5 = 70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ ✅; $140 \\times 0.5 = 70\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ ✅.\n\n4. Health category assignments:\n   - **45:** **Good** (0 to 50).\n   - **95:** **Satisfactory** (51 to 100).\n   - **150:** **Moderate** (101 to 200).\n   - **200:** **Moderate** (upper threshold of Moderate).\n   - **201:** **Poor** (201 to 300).\n   - **320:** **Very Poor** (301 to 400).\n   - **455:** **Severe** (401 to 500).\n\n5. (a) **Primary pollutant**: Carbon monoxide is directly released from the combustion chamber of the vehicle into the surrounding air.\n   (b) **Primary pollutant**: Soot consists of unburnt elemental carbon particles emitted directly via the exhaust pipe.\n   (c) **Secondary pollutant**: Ground-level ozone is not emitted directly from tailpipes; it forms when emitted nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds react chemically in the presence of sunlight.\n   (d) **Primary pollutant**: Sulfur dioxide is released directly from the chimney when sulfur impurities in coal oxidize during combustion.\n\n6. (a) **Troposphere** (spans 0 to ~12 km; 10 km lies within the upper troposphere).\n   (b) **Troposphere** (8.8 km lies within the troposphere).\n   (c) **Stratosphere** (spans 12 to 50 km; the ozone maximum occurs between 15 and 35 km).\n   (d) **Mesosphere** (spans 50 to 85 km; meteor friction occurs predominantly in this cold layer).\n\n7. (a) **Natural source** (unless triggered by human arson or negligence, in which case it is anthropogenic).\n   (b) **Anthropogenic source** (industrial brick production).\n   (c) **Natural source** (geological volcanic activity).\n   (d) **Anthropogenic source** (fossil-fuel energy generation).\n   (e) **Natural source** (wind-driven erosion across arid landscapes).\n   (f) **Anthropogenic source** (combustion of human municipal refuse).\n\n8. (a) **False** ❌. Earth's atmosphere consists of approximately **78% nitrogen** and 21% oxygen by volume.\n   (b) **True** ✅. By definition, $1\\% = \\frac{1}{100} = \\frac{10{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = 10{,}000\\text{ ppm}$.\n   (c) **False** ❌. An AQI of 120 falls in the range 101 to 200, which corresponds to the **Moderate** category, not Poor.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Nearly all atmospheric moisture, clouds, and convective air movements are confined to the troposphere.\n\n9. (a) Factor of change: $\\frac{360}{180} = \\mathbf{2.0\\text{ times (doubled)}}$.\n   (b) On Monday, 180 falls within **Moderate** (101–200). On Wednesday, 360 falls within **Very Poor** (301–400).\n   (c) Practical precautions: Avoid vigorous outdoor physical activities (such as sports or strenuous running), keep doors and windows shut to minimize indoor dust intrusion, run air purifiers indoors if available, and wear high-filtration particulate masks (such as N95) when stepping outdoors.\n\n10. (a) $1\\ \\mu\\text{m} \\times 1{,}000 = \\mathbf{1{,}000\\text{ nm}}$.\n    (b) $0.1\\ \\mu\\text{m} \\times 1{,}000 = \\mathbf{100\\text{ nm}}$.\n    (c) $4\\ \\mu\\text{m} = \\mathbf{4 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ m}}$ (or $0.000004\\text{ m}$).\n    (d) Since $1\\text{ mm} = 1{,}000\\ \\mu\\text{m}$, $25\\ \\mu\\text{m} = \\frac{25}{1{,}000} = \\mathbf{0.025\\text{ mm}}$.\n    *Verification:* $0.025\\text{ mm} \\times 1{,}000 = 25\\ \\mu\\text{m}$ ✅.\n\n11. (a) $1\\text{ m}^3 = 1{,}000\\text{ L}$.\n    $$\\text{Volume fraction} = \\frac{1{,}200}{1{,}000{,}000} = 0.0012$$\n    $$V_{\\text{CO}_2} = 1{,}000\\text{ L} \\times 0.0012 = \\mathbf{1.2\\text{ L}}$$\n    (b) In millilitres: $1.2\\text{ L} \\times 1{,}000 = \\mathbf{1{,}200\\text{ mL}}$.\n    (c) Percentage: $\\frac{1{,}200}{10{,}000} = \\mathbf{0.12\\%}$.\n    *Verification:* $1{,}000\\text{ L} \\times \\frac{1{,}200}{1{,}000{,}000} = 1.2\\text{ L}$ ✅. (This demonstrates why opening windows to refresh indoor ventilation in crowded classrooms is so essential!)\n\n---\n\nLeo glanced up at the red AQI monitor one last time before tucking his notebook into his backpack. \"Thank you, Dr. Higgins. Now whenever I see that number 312, I understand what it truly signifies: a Very Poor air quality day that demands caution.\" Dr. Higgins nodded approvingly. \"Knowledge is the first and most powerful step toward environmental stewardship.\"\n\nCongratulations on mastering this introductory topic! You now understand the precise composition of clean air, the layered architecture of the atmosphere, the distinction between primary and secondary pollutants, the scale of PM2.5, and how to interpret the Air Quality Index.\n\n**What comes next?** In our next topic, **\"Acid Rain\"**, we will join Maya on a field trip to examine weathered historic marble monuments and statues, discovering how dissolved airborne industrial gases transform normal rainfall into corrosive acid precipitation.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Helium makes up about 5 ppm of air and methane about 1.9 ppm (approximate values). Express each as a percentage.\n\n2. A classroom measures 10 m × 6 m × 3 m. Using the air composition of this topic, find (a) the volume of the room in m³, (b) the volume of oxygen in it in m³ and in litres, and (c) the volume of nitrogen in m³.\n\n3. A dust particle is 5 µm across and another is 0.5 µm across. How many times thinner than a 70 µm hair is each? Is each particle counted as PM10, PM2.5, or both?\n\n4. Name the AQI category for each reading: 45, 95, 150, 200, 201, 320, 455.\n\n5. Say whether each pollutant is primary or secondary and give a reason: (a) carbon monoxide from a scooter, (b) soot from a diesel truck, (c) ground-level ozone formed in sunlight from vehicle exhaust, (d) sulphur dioxide from a power plant chimney.\n\n6. In which layer of the atmosphere would you find: (a) a passenger plane at 10 km, (b) the top of Mount Everest at about 8.8 km, (c) the densest part of the ozone layer at about 25 km, (d) a shooting star burning at about 70 km?\n\n7. Classify each as a natural or a human source of air pollution: (a) a forest fire, (b) a brick kiln, (c) a volcano, (d) a coal-based power plant, (e) a dust storm, (f) a burning garbage heap.\n\n8. State True or False and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Air is about 21% nitrogen.\n    (b) 1% equals 10,000 ppm.\n    (c) An AQI of 120 is in the Poor category.\n    (d) Weather such as rain happens mainly in the troposphere.\n\n9. In a city, the AQI rises from 180 on Monday to 360 on Wednesday. By what factor did it change, and in which categories were the two days? What simple precaution would you take on Wednesday?\n\n10. Convert: (a) 1 µm to nm, (b) 0.1 µm to nm, (c) 4 µm to m (in powers of ten), (d) 25 µm to mm.\n\n11. In a closed, crowded classroom the CO₂ level rises to about 1200 ppm. How many litres of carbon dioxide are there in 1 m³ of this air? Give the answer in litres and in millilitres, and as a percentage.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Percent = ppm ÷ 10,000. Helium: 5 ÷ 10,000 = **0.0005%**. Methane: 1.9 ÷ 10,000 = **0.00019%**. Jaanch: 0.0005 × 10,000 = 5 ✅ aur 0.00019 × 10,000 = 1.9 ✅.\n\n2. (a) Volume = 10 × 6 × 3 = **180 m³**. (b) Oxygen 21%: 180 × 21 ÷ 100 = **37.8 m³**. Litre mein: 37.8 × 1000 = **37,800 L**. (c) Nitrogen 78%: 180 × 78 ÷ 100 = **140.4 m³**. Jaanch: 37.8 + 140.4 = 178.2 m³ aur bacha 1.8 m³ (1% of 180) ✅.\n\n3. 70 ÷ 5 = **14 guna** patla, aur 70 ÷ 0.5 = **140 guna** patla. 5 µm wala kan 10 µm se chhota hai, par 2.5 µm se bada, to wo sirf **PM10** mein gina jaata hai. 0.5 µm wala kan 2.5 µm se bhi chhota hai, to wo **PM2.5 aur PM10 dono** mein aata hai. Jaanch: 14 × 5 = 70 ✅ aur 140 × 0.5 = 70 ✅.\n\n4. 45: **Good** (0 se 50). 95: **Satisfactory** (51 se 100). 150: **Moderate** (101 se 200). 200: **Moderate** (upari seema). 201: **Poor** (201 se 300). 320: **Very Poor** (301 se 400). 455: **Severe** (401 se 500).\n\n5. (a) **Primary**: scooter ke exhaust se seedha nikalti hai. (b) **Primary**: soot dhuen ke saath seedhe nikalta hai. (c) **Secondary**: ye seedha nahi nikalta, vehicle ke exhaust ki gasein sunlight mein milkar hawa mein banati hain. (d) **Primary**: SO₂ chimney se seedha aati hai (koyle ke sulphur ke jalne se).\n\n6. (a) **Troposphere** (0 se lagbhag 12 km). (b) **Troposphere** (8.8 km, 12 km se neeche). (c) **Stratosphere** (12 se 50 km, ozone layer lagbhag 15 se 35 km). (d) **Mesosphere** (50 se 85 km).\n\n7. (a) **Natural** (jungle ki aag, agar bijli girne jaisi prakritik wajah se lage; insaan ki wajah se lagi ho to human). (b) **Human**. (c) **Natural**. (d) **Human**. (e) **Natural**. (f) **Human**.\n\n8. (a) **False** ❌. Hawa mein lagbhag **78% nitrogen** aur 21% oxygen hai. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **False** ❌. 120, 101 se 200 mein aata hai, to **Moderate** hai, Poor nahi. (d) **True** ✅. Mausam ke baadal aur baarish troposphere mein hote hain.\n\n9. 360 ÷ 180 = **2 guna**. Somvaar ka 180 **Moderate** (101 se 200) hai, aur budhvaar ka 360 **Very Poor** (301 se 400). Budhvaar ko bahar ke kheloon aur lambi der bahar rehna kam karo, khidkiyaan band rakho, aur zaroorat ho to mask lagao (Topic 5 mein aur dekhenge). Jaanch: 180 × 2 = 360 ✅.\n\n10. (a) 1 × 1000 = **1000 nm**. (b) 0.1 × 1000 = **100 nm**. (c) 4 × 10⁻⁶ m = **4 × 10⁻⁶ m** (yaani 0.000004 m). (d) 1 µm = 10⁻³ mm, to 25 × 10⁻³ = **0.025 mm**. Jaanch: 1 mm = 1000 µm, to 25 µm = 25 ÷ 1000 = 0.025 mm ✅.\n\n11. 1 m³ = 1000 L. 1200 ppm = 1200 ÷ 10,00,000 = 0.0012 (hissa). 1000 × 0.0012 = **1.2 L**, aur 1.2 × 1000 = **1200 mL**. Percent mein: 1200 ÷ 10,000 = **0.12%**. Jaanch: 1000 L ke 10 lakh hisson mein se 1200 hisse = 1000 × 1200 ÷ 10,00,000 = 1.2 ✅. (Isliye bhare, band kamre mein khidki kholna achha hai.)\n\n---\n\nKshitij ne board ki taraf ek aakhri baar dekha aur copy band ki. \"Sir, ab main is number ko padh sakta hoon: 312, yaani Very Poor.\" Wadekar sir ne sir hilaya. \"Aur ab roz padhna: jaanna hi pehla kadam hai.\"\n\nShabaash! Tumne ye topic poora kar liya: ab tum saaf hawa ki banawat, atmosphere ki parte, pradushan ke prakaar aur sources, PM2.5 ka size aur AQI ke ank padh sakte ho.\n\n**Aage kya?** Agle topic **\"Acid Rain\"** mein hum ek school trip par Agra chalenge, jahan Hardik ko ek monument ka safed marble dhundhla aur peela dikhega, aur dekhenge ki hawa mein ghuli gasein baarish ko khatta kaise bana deti hain."
          }
